      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <p>MESSRS. FLETCHER. RUSSELL'S</p>
        <p>NEW WORKS.</p>
        <p>At the invitation of the directors and shareholders arid a large number of invited gueats visited the new works at Wilderspool onTours lay. The visitors were furnished with a few retrospective pal ticulars and descriptive notes, forming a connecting link between the past and the future history of the company. The erection of these central works has been undertaken with a view Ist, of centralise the workshops and administr4t on of ras business ; 2nd, to provide more accommo /ation f ,r the increase of trade and for further extensiona, embracing latest improvements on a well prepared general grand plan, designed for the future crmolete works ; 3rl, to save carriage and trading expenses as between depart- mental works ; 4th to prepare for general economies to meet growing competition. The company was formed and incorporated on the 16th February, 1892. and pur- chased the amalgamated imagines, namely of Mfs,rs. Thomas Fletcher and Cempany,Warringtou and London; and of Messrs. W. and A. C. Russell and Company. of Pendleton, Manchester, ard London. The former Warrington works in Thynne-strcet are closed, and are offered for sale. The presen buildings are designed to form a specific portion only of the com- plete works, but will meanwhile be used for ceneral purposes. An avenue will in due time form an approach from the highway to the chief administration blcck and central entrance. The engine house contains a com- pound tandem engine,kdicating about 130 horse power. From the main counter shaft in the engine house there are twe generating dynamos driven, one for distribut- ing electric power to the various degattments, and the other for lighting the entire works by electricity. E'ec- tric motors and hoists are fixed all over the premises. The general plan of the entire works gives every fa ility for the economical and quick handling of the Castings to the various departments, which are heated by steam ard electrically lighted throughout. The various buildings are connected Ly gangways. An extraordinary general mseting of shareholders and debenture-holders of Messrs Fletcher. Rtusell and 05., gas stove makers and artistic ironfounders, was held on Thursday, presided over by Mr Thomas Fletcher. Messrs Thomas Fletcher, Alexander Neil, William Russell, and AC Rusiell were elected managing directors; and Messrs John Neil, Charles Clare, W Neil, and E L Fletcher, ordinary directors, for the ensuing twelve msntbs. Mr Thomas Fletcher was appointed chairman, and Mr Alexander Neil.v ice-ch</p>
        <p>_ Subsequently a lunch was provided in the showroom, at which there was a large numb,r of visitors. Among those present were the Mayor and Maroress of Warring- ton (Alderman and Mrs J E Wright), Rev H V Pigot, Messrs Thomas Fletcher, W Russell, A C Ruezell, Charles Clare, W R Neil, and E L Fletcher (directors). W H Budge (secretary). W Russell, jun. (a.sistart secretary), Mr and Mrs W J Forshaw, Mr E Guthrie, Mr J Jackcn, Mrs T Fletcher, Miss Fleteher, Mrs A Nel, Mr T W Fletcher, Mrs AV Russell. Miss H S Ruvell, Mr W Rus- sell, jun., Mr PC Russell, Miss Duncan, Mrs W R Neil, Mr J L Whittle (town clerk). Mr T Lone,din (borough surveyor), Mr and Mrs and Miss Fainurst. Mr Hunter, Mr Swale, Mr Cook, Mr W A Wood, Mr Rie.kford, Mr Thompson, Alderman Sutton, Courrzillor Smethurst. Mr Melbourne, Mr Evrart, Mr Hallow, Mr and MI s Gilb^rt, Mr Tinker, Mr Daine, Mr Henshall, Mr Haddock (gas engineer). Mr Davenport (builder of works), Sergeant Bodle (East Warrington employee), &amp;C. After the lunch, Mr. Wituala RUSSELL, who presided after the departure of Mr. Thomas Fietcher, said he was glad to see the Mayer of Warrington present. That gentleman had been the architect in the new works and closely connected with him had been the vice-^hairman, Mr. Neil. Mr. Neil had really been desigeer of the works, as, being old hant-lsas ironfenn 'leis, they knew what they were likely to want in the new works. He would proceed with the toast lie, and propose the health of the Queen and tile members of the Royal Family. This vzas drunk with musical honours, the band of the works playing the National Anthem. Mr. W. J. FORSTLivr proposed " Succe4st of the Com- pany." He said thst the new works reflected the great- est credit upon these who had been concerned in its erection, and the shareholders were to be congratulated upon the fact that there were operations being carried on at the present moment. Mr. WILLIAM RUSSELL briefly replied. Mr. A. RUSSELL proposed "'fle Town and Trade of VfarringtOrl." He said the industries of the town were very varied. There were ironworks, wireworke,tanninz, soap works, glass works, breweries, and many other in- dustries. It was certainly very fortunate in having so Inlay, and they that day were come to settle down to increase the number of those manufactures. There Time. noweyer,severAl things which mieht he improved. They ought certainly to do something to take away the</p>
        <p>THE MARVELLOCS POLYPEION MUS i CAL BoxTeo - _BY THEMSELVES like ordinary musical boxes. but mu oh su- perior tone. HUNDREDS OF TUNES can be played You, can choose your own tunes. GEISHA AND UP-TO-DATE U.UNES. Prices from 12s. 61 to £25. • ARCHER &amp; SONS, 43 TO 47 ORD.STREET, LIVERPnOL.</p>
        <p>40XES PLAY '.1...-V2.31111 „</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <p>privilege of one or two firms of putting out as much smoke as they pleased. Toe drainage was also not of the best. There were great trading facilities at War- rington, and Warrington woald,he was sure, be a much larger town in the future than at the present, The MAYOR returned thanks, and heartily responded to the toast. He was sure that the Corporation were as hoaEst as ever they were in their desire to promote the prosperity of the old town, and they heartily wel- comed the new works of Mesirs. Fletcher, Russell, and Co., in xhicii he believed before long there would be 600 or 700 men employed. He wished to thank the directors for the courtesy they had extended to him as architect, and he wished to thaak Mr. Davenport, the bader, for the way in which he had asiisted him. Alderman &amp;ITT )N proposed "The Chairman." _</p>
        <p>. . Mr. RUSSELL, in reply, referring to the manufactures of the works, said the firm were at the present time making ad kinds of electric apparatus as well as gas applrltus.</p>
        <p>A mould Wag afterwards mad and cast in the presence oi the viAtors. The Works bind sai in attendance and played selections of 111113iC, Under the conductorship of Mr. Kirk.</p>
