      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>WARRINGTON ASSESSUENT COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ASSESSUENT COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this clmmittee was held in the Board Room at the Workhouse on Friday morning. Mr. G Pilling pre,,d,d, and there were also present : Messrs. William Hickman. J Clarke, E L Bent, William Hough- T Halton, A J Jo!ley, J BiHinge, S Davies, A Jolley, with Mr. J C Sutton (clerk), Mr. J L Tunstall (assistant overseer), Mr. Owen (valuer).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>CLOSING OF A POTTERY.</head>
        <p>CLOSING OF A POTTERY.</p>
        <p>Councillor GRACE appealed against the assessment of his pottery in Winwick-nad which was fixed at £4l gross, £35 rateable. He stated that he was the owner of the place but sinc-i the first week of Novembn it hA cot been used for the manufacture of pottery, but h-, had cortinued to use a part of the works for the storage of goods. He had built a new works outside St. ficlens, and did not intend to make U.B of the Warring- ton works again.—The Valuer : The ma.nufanturing portion is closed entirely P—Cauccillor Grace : That is so.—lt was deci led that the matter should be left in the hands of the Valuer to make a re-assessment of the pre- mises.</p>
        <p>vALuEa's RitPoRT.</p>
        <p>Mr. OWEN in his monthly report said he had made the following inspections and valuations :—ln the township of Warrington, Messrs. William Reynolds and Company, Limited, Merser-street, appealed against office and tan- nery, gross £667 103, rateable £561 ; cOttage,9B,Mersev" street, gross £9 14 rtealale £8 ; 5 cottages 16 to 24, Smith-street. gross £6 134 rateable £5 10s each. Messrs. William Reynolds and Company have a lease in the whole of the premises, including all the fixed machinery and shafting and the cottages at £5OO per annum for three years. There was but little machinery attach3d to the premises which did not belong to the landlords and which Was not included in the lease so that an assessment of £5lO gross would cover all. Mr. Thomas Halton, George HAel and Stabling, of Sankey-street. Mr Haltcn appealed against the assessment of gross £lOO rateable £B5. This was a free house on an annual tenancy and the rent paid was £95 having been reduced to this amount in the year 1889. Ihe Brew BOlicf, which was used up to 18 months ago was now dismgntled and was about to be pulled down. He was of opinion the assessment could be reduced to the rent gro.s £96, rateable £Bl. Messrs. Fletcher,Rus- sell and Co., Limited, works, Wilderspeol-road, gross £l,BOO, rateable £1,500. This valuation was for the wo ks fully eccupad but at present the occupation was not fcomplete. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
