      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>I will tell you of one of the latest and prettiest coats to be seen. It is very stylish, too, is the spring coat of cloth shown, and here there are double lapels, the upper ones of coloured cloth, and pearl buttons of the same tint. All the edges are stitched, and it is indeed difficult to find any smart tailor garment which does not show an infinite amount of stitching. The centre basque tab is repeated at the back, and the little V of white cloth is a portion of the mat, and attached to the high collar.</p>
        <p>As to bonnets you will see one very frequent now which is very smart, being composed chiefly of roses and lilies-of-the-valley, with upstanding bows of green ribbon. This is a very becoming model, which can also be made with other flowers or ribbons of a different shade, Some very pretty hats are also to be seen one being all black, save for the loops of velvet matching the vest, and a black and white veil is worn. On the right is a striking combination of grey and green cloth, the skirt entirely novel in cut and arrangement. Here the toplis made as a yoke and the skirt stitched on to it under a cloth band. The Eton coat has inlet of the darker cloth, and the fronts form points at either side, the dark band running up as far as the collar, where the coat is held together by a strap of cloth.</p>
        <p>Looking over the " Lady " and other fashion papers I have noticed what a lot of fashionable weddings there have been recently and was struck with the fineness of the brides' attires in some. For instance in one the bride, wore a picture gown of cream satin Duchesse similar to the one shown, the dainty bodice corded to form a small round yoke, with tiny diamonds set on silver tivead between the cords, the same outlining all the seams of the long trained gown and tracing a wave round the hem. A Brussels lace veil was arranged to form a loose coat, the back of which veiled the whole of the train ; the small sleeves were fastened up to the elbow with single stone diamond buttons, and the inner seams were outlined with stones. She wore a tulle veil, while her bouquet was of lilies of the valley. Her ornaments were two dia- mond brooches, a pearl necklace, and a diamond brooch.</p>
        <p>There were quite number of others which I should like to mention had I time, but you will no doubt have seen them for yourself and so I will proceed to tell you what I think about your remarks of last week as to dresses generally. When purchasing a dress, due consideration should be given to the choice of colour and material. It is almost impossible to provide for every pnr- ticular. case, but a few hints may not come amiss to those of my readers who want to look their best. Grey is only suitable to slim and fresh-coloured women. Fawn and grey must be avoided by stout figures. Black I consider the most unbecoming of all colours. It accentuates the lines of age. Yel- low makes one look slighter; it is well suited to evening gowns. All reddish-violets, petunias, and heliotropes may be selected by florid golden-haired blondes, who also claim old rose, blue in the corn- flower and turquoise shades, hyacinth, rose•pink, rose-red, and all the vivid and fresh shades of green from apple to emerald, taking in the moss bronze greens. A pure linen white may be worn to advantage, and all the shades of pinky mauve are favourable. Cold blondes with rasher pale faces and silvery fair hair must be careful to select lavender blues, warm tints of pinky grey, grey. blue, RdirSda primrose, and nearly all shades of ruby, prune and petunia. All shiny surfaces, like satin, &amp;e., increase bulk. Stout figures, if the wearer is tall, can take large brocade designs, stripes and all Vehaped arrangements, such as vests or tunics, otherwise known as "over- dresses."</p>
        <p>There is a blouse of white pongee quite worthy of describing, dezpite its simplicity. The bodice was full, tucked across, and fastened on the left side under a frill of wide lace. Each tuck had a row of Übe black velvet ribbon and a piece of Valepiciennes lace. The blouse was just slightly pointed in front, and it had a drapbd waist-belt of the white silk. Bows of black velvet ribbon with paste centres gave a characteristic note at the neck and belt. The sleeves ware entirely rucked, and had lines el narrow velvet and lace from shoulder to wrist. The collar was transparent, of lace like the frill, and fastened with a bow of black velvet ribbon at the buck. I see short supple corsets in all the shops—the instruments of torture hitherto worn have been appalling. Corsets should be quite short leaving perfect ireedom to the bust and hips. Your loving JENNIB.</p>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
