      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, MARCH 10, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, MARCH 10, 1899.</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>During the Queen's sojourn at Cimiez, the State Apartments at Windsor will be open to the public again, tand visitors to the Metropolis should not lose an oppor- unity of taking a peep at our Sovereign's statel home. has been said, and truly,that Windsor Park is always beautiful. In winter, owing to the leafless condition of the trees, the views are grand ;in spring, when flowers stud the grassy places,and greenness wreaths the boughs then the glades of the ancient woods take on a softened beauty which by contrast, adds to the grandeur of the massive grey Castle ; while in summer Nature seems to show her fairest talents here. The absence of the Queen and the Princess of Wales from the March Drawing Rooms, and the Prince of Wales from the Levee, has occasioned much dissatisfac- tion. The show of Royalties too on each occasion was not calculated to improve matters. Princess Christian who represented Her Majesty, is an immense favourite, but naturally Court-goers prefer to be received by the Sovereign herself, or out. future Queen. Among the Princesses,Victoria of Schlessvig-Holstein,elder daughter of Princess Christian, looked superb. She is, like her cousin, Victoiia of Wales, a "bachelor girl" and a very stately and fascinating one. It is expected that next year the Arch-Duchess Elizabeth of Austria, granddaughter of the Austrian Emperor, and only child of the ill-fated Crown Prince Rudolph, will appear at the great Court festivities and later be betrothed to Prince Rupprecht of Bavaria. She is possessed of considerable intelligence,and many talents and the careful education that she is receiving is training her to be a most intellectual and charming Princess. The young Arch-Duchess is said to be very fond of English books, and some of the first read by Her Im- perial flighaess were Miss Yonge's stories, which are re- garded on the Continent with great approval. The Home of Rest for Authors, wh'ch has been so literal ly endowed by Lady Murray is now available,and promises to he extensively patronised. It is situated at Antibes. near Cannes. Makers of books, worn out with brain-fag will not fail to bless Lady Murray for her generosity, and it is hoped that someone, stimulated by this excellent example, will shurtly start a similar " Home of Rest "for weary Journalists."</p>
        <p>Speaking of authors, it does not seem to be generally known that" John Strange Winter "(Mrs. Arthur Stan - nerd) whose soldier stories are too well known to need mention is the great, great granddaughter of the cele- brated actress Hannah Pritchard, whose monument may be seen in Westminster Abbey, not far from the bust of Shakespeare. Mrs.Stannard is the daughter of a clergy- man. and is just forty-four years of age. Sloe is a hand- some woman, with hair fast turning grey, bright, dark eyes, and a mouth full of expression and kindlioess,and a general atmosphere of healthy common sense about her. She published her first story a quarter of a century ago: . _</p>
        <p>Miss Helen Mathers. who is the author of one of the most delightful novels ever written, " Comin' thro' the Rye," was born at Misterton,near Crawkerne, Somerset- shire. She is the wife of the well known surgeon, and specialist on orthopaedics, Mr. Henry Reeves, and is es- sentially a domesticated woman. She loves literature ardently, but has never allowed it to interfere with her duties as a housewife and mother. " Helen blathers' possesses a gilt rarely to be found in wemen—t hat of distinctive inherent . humour. She has an immenFe sense of the ridiculous, and talks exactly as she writes. She is highly imaginative, and is always looked upon as the social lode-star in the midst of literary gatherings because she is never at a less to laugh, and be merry,and knows the secret of infecting her company with her own gaiety. It has frequently been asserted that once upon a time it was a rare thing for a woman's study of the morning newspaper to extend beyond the "Hatch, Match, and Despatch," column. There is doubtless some truth in this accusation, but thinga are altered nowadays. Such a type of woman is as extinct as the dodo. Now that we are graciously permitted to possess property of our own, even, strange to say, the money that we earn, we all peep at the city article, as a matter of course. Poli- tics too interest us, and then there are always the columns devoted to our doings, junk.ttings, and attire. The journalistic world caters for another kind of woman a woman who has a life, a career, and possibly a fortune of her own.</p>
        <p>Earrings are much worn by up-to-date women. They are still however, quite small, and there seems little likelihood of the heavy long variety making its appear- ance, as was feared some time ago. Whether we think so because we are accustomed to them, or whether it is difficult to say, but the impression remains that the two points of glittering light, serve to frame in well - modelled cheeks of fresh and dainty coloring very ap- propriately. It was always a mistake to weir earrings with very large and ugly ears, and in fact the fashion lost some of its style by being adopted by women pos- sessed of these.</p>
        <p>Among rings, the marquise shape, composed of one stone, is very modish. it used to be worn on the index finger, but is now not liked on that one since its appease suet, is awkward and affected. The wedding ring has not altered materially in shape for some time. It should be neither very broad,nor very narrow, a medium sizn is the correct one. Engagement rings ara taking no special form but the cross-over pat-ern, as it is technically called, is very new and charming. The gold circle meets, and the ends cross in front, both being encrusted with precious stones. -</p>
        <p>Turquoises are well to the fore, which is scarcely an item to rejoice over, as of all precious stones th,, tur- quoise is perhaps the least beautiful. It is of Persian origin, though it has teen found in India, in Mexico,a9d even in Auctralia. The real gem is the "Old Rock" turquoise, known to the Romans as the Callais, which is of the purest blue. The " New Rock "stones are of less value,having a greenish tint. Th. re are also some pure green and a fs w white specimens. A circumstance wortt: the notice of possessors of the best stones is, that they change colour it kept near camphor, and that damp is deleterious to them.</p>
        <p>None of the b(st cut skirts have, apparently. any opening. The fashionable woman, to the uninitiated appears to be sewn into this paticular garment. As a matter of fact, the plack6t opening is usually at the side of the front seam, and this is fitted with close set hooks and eyes, the same as the bodice. It is an impos- sibility that skirts can grow any tighter from waiet to knee, and soon there must come reaction. Let us fer- vently hope that modistes will not rush off to the other extreme and ask us to. don the crinoline, the crinolette, or some other atrocity of the "bunchy " persuasion. Coats are being built with the shortest of basquee, some only five or six inches deep, and few more than six of seven inches. The leading idea at the moment seems to be how small we can appear about the hips, and therefore it is not likely that we should spoil the effect by wearing long or bulky basques. Sleeves are tight fitting, and in all cases exhibit but the slight est suspicion of fulness on the top. Fish Rolls—Take half a pound of any raw white fish, free of skin and bone, and pound it in a mortar with a tablespoonful of butter, and two tablespoonfuls of braid crumbs. When reduced to a paste,add two well-beaten eggs, half a teaspocnful of anchovy sauce, salt and cayenne to taste, Blend thoroughly and make into rolls about the size of an egg. Brush over with egg. dip -twice in breadcrumbs, and fry a golden brown, Serve garnished with slices of lemon and sprigs of parsley. Cold cocked fish can be used:for this dish, if more handy.</p>
        <p>~ MADGE."</p>
        <p>ifo rn *</p>
        <p>Califta Syrup of Figs acts as a perfect laxative should, cleansing and refreshing the system without weaken- ing it; permanently curing constipation and its effects.</p>
        <p>Agreeable preventives in season are much surer than belated drugs. A healthy con- dition of the Kidneys, Liver, and Bowels is the strongest safeguard against Head-</p>
        <p>aches, Racking Colds or Fevers. Mild and Sure in action and absolutely free from ob- jectionable substances. Physicians re- commend it. Millions have found it invaluable. Children enjoy it; delicate ladies find it an ideal medicine; busy men take it without inconvenien discomfort. Imitations and substitutes are numerous: you must ask for "CALIFORNIA" Syrup of Figs.</p>
        <p>Look for the name and trade mark of the CALIFORNIA PIG SYRUP CO., which is on every bottle of the genuine. Of all Chemists, r/ri and 14 A Depot :32 Snow Hill, London, E. C.</p>
        <p>-1 see a younger man tnis time, toolisti, easily led, impressionable, and naturally straightforward He also is under the spell of the siren, and freedom will only come to him when it Nimes to his rival for the lady's affections. At twelve o'clock to-morrow night they are to be removed elsewhere." _ _</p>
        <p>"And these," indicating the seal to which the piece of broken watch-chain had been restored. And these belonged to a man of stronger passions than either, and his fate is less clear than theirs. He is dark, stout, middle-aged, and uncultured, but is nevertheless made much of in society on account of his remarkable financial success. The same unscrupulous siren has been at work with him; and although I cannot see its outcome, I see a violent struggle. After that, all is darkness." With the last words, Madame Celia sighed heavily, after the manner of a person who is throw- ing off a great burden. The room was suddenly flooded with light, and everything looked common- place and natural again.. "One moment," exclaimed Lord Alfred. "You mentioned a house in Hammersmith. Can you tell us exactly where it is ? " "I can tell you nothing more," was the grave reply, "for I am quits unable to recollect anything I see or say while I am clairvoyante." "Then can you not return to that condition ? I implore you to do so, madame, for great things depend upon our being able to trace our friends at once. And if money--" "Peace, sir!" was the impatient rejoinder. "My gift does not come and go at the will of the highest bidder. At some other time I may be able to assist you • but at present the effort would be useless; and I must ask you to leave me, for these ordeals exact their punishment in bodily exhaustion." There was evidently nothing more to be gained by pertinacity, and the three men departed, even Mr. Raleigh labouring under a strain of unwonted excitement; while poor Mr. Quicke could hardly control his exultation.</p>
        <p>"Thank God t" he exclaimed, as soon as they were all three in the street. "Thank God, my boy is not dead, after all, for this woman says that he is a prisoner in some house at Hammersmith; and if every house in the district has to be searched before we are successful, he shall be found and liberated. So shall my old friend; and then we may find out who is to blame for all the mischief, though it isn't hard to guess." "I wouldn't be too confident if I were you, Mr. Rizzen-Quicke," advised Lord Alfred, "for we shall no doubt have unexpected difficulties to contend against; and it must be remembered that our foes are unusually cunning. Happily, they do not seem to be deliberate murderers as well as thieves, other- wise they would not have hampered themselves by keeping their victims in custody." "I have not the same opinion.of their leniency that you have, my lord," observed the detective. "If they hesitate at murder, it is entirely for their own sakes. A live man is worth more than a dead one any day, especially if he has a lot of rich relations • and I daresay they have some black- mailing plan in their. heads. Besides, people don't get hung for robbery and kidnapping; and it only shews extra cunning when such a gang as is now at work draws the line at murder. You see, my lord, so long as their victims are kept under safe watch and ward, they can't tell many tales; and in the event of their being charged with the capital crime, it is always possible for them to prove them- selves innocent."</p>
        <p>"But we are by no means sure that there has not been a murder committed," retorted Mr. Rizzen- Quicke, "for everything seems to point to foul play in the case of Mr. Moseiithal."</p>
        <p>"Yes," agreed the detective; "things do look black in that direction, I grant. But if we were to go and say that we had got all our information from a woman in a trance, we should be pretty well laughed at for our pains, for that sort of evidence wouldn't be accepted in a court of law." "Evidence or not, I intend to act on it," said Mr. Quicke, and both he and Lord Alfred displayed such energy that, before they went to sleep that night, their plan of campaign for the immediate future was definitely arranged.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XVIII. ACTING ON INFORMATION RECEIVED."</p>
        <p>UAL twelve o'clock they will be removed else- where."</p>
        <p>Such had been the prediction of Madame Celia; and everything else which she had told the three consultants had been so remarkably exact that they believed her to be just as able to predict future events as to cast her mind upon the equally mystic past. The difficulties in the,way of acting upon the clairvoyante's information were not so great with Mr. Rizzen-Quicke and his intended son-in-law as they would have been in the case of men whose pecuniary resources were limited. "On the outskirts of Hammersmith" was a some- what vague direction, but it was bound to be within a mile or so of the Broadway, either to the east or to the west, as it was said to be close to the main road. If you go more than a mile from the Broad- way towards London, you are in West Kensington; and if you go more than a mile in the other direction, you are in Chiswick. This, therefore, narrows the search for the old house in which John Mills and Teddy Quicke were said to be incar- cerated, down to a two-mile stretch of open road in a populous neighbourhood, where an empty house, either great or small, is likely to be a rarity. By twelve o'clock on the day following the visit to Madame Celia, Mr. Raleigh had received his reports from various emissaries whom he had despatched to Hammersmith; and it transpired that there was only one house which fully answered to the clairvoyante's description. This was Hamlet House—a gloomy old mansion with an evil,reputa- tion—situated near Ravenscourt Park. It was said that its former owner, a Mr. Hamlet, although in prosperous circumstances, committed suicide in the house. This in itself would not have been sufficient to consign such a valuable estate to ruin and neglect; but it was asserted, on the 'authority of a number of credible witnesses, that Mr. Hamlet had had the bad taste to re-visit the scene of his last days so pertinaciously that he was gradually left in undisturbed possession. For nearly sixty years Hamlet House had remained unin- habited, and the trees had gone on growing until they were• veritable giants. It was a very likely place in which to incarcerate prisoners, for it was set so far back amongst the dense growth of trees and shrubs that it could easily elude unwelcome observation; and if a stray shriek happened to be heard emanating from the building, it would only result in making the place more shunned than ever, for the ghost would be blamed. Having quite decided in their own minds that the missing men were imprisoned in Hamlet House, their would-be deliverers drove down to Hammer- smith to reconnoitre for themselves, arriving there about two o'clock. A huge board informed them that the house and grounds were "To Let or For Sale," and that application must be made to a local house and estate agent. It only required a few minutes to pay a visit to the agent's officer, to ask a few questions concerning the rent and terms of tenancy, and to procure the keys, on the pretence of wishing to see if the hpuse would suit Mr. Rizzen-Quicke as a future risidence. "You will find it rather out of order," said the agent; "but, of course, if you rent it on a long lease, the owner will willingly undertake all neces- sary repairs and decorations. At any other time Mr. Rizzen-Quicke would have chuckled at the agent's business-like reserve concern- ing the history of the residence which had been a non-productive property for so many *cedes. But he was too full of anxiety to see anything amusing in connection with his present errand; and never before was the inspection of "A House to Let" entered upon with more seriousness. The ponderous front-door key turned noisily in the lock, and the explorers wjre by no means surprised at the evidences of fieglect and decay which assailed them on all sides. Nor did they trouble to discover whether the house was capable of being put into repair or not. What they were concerned to find out was whether Madame Celia had spoken correctly when she informed them that it was already tenanted. The indications all pointed in the negative; and Mr. Rizzen-Quicke was feeling bitterly4lisappointed, when the sound of footsteps was heard coming from one of the upper rooms, and a rough, unkempt head leaned over the banisters of a staircase which had once been very handsome, but which was now, as the agent would put it, rather out of order. At sight of the three men in the hall, the ownel of the head hurriedly withdrew, but was soon over- taken by Lord Alfred, who scaled the creaking stairs two at a time, and grasped the fugitive's arm just as he was entering one of the rooms on the top storey. "One moment, please," he said. "We understood from the agent that this house was quite untenanted. Do you mind explaining what you are doing here ? " By this time the man had recovered his self- possession, and retorted with . considerable assurance:</p>
        <p>"I don't see that it's any, business of yours as long as you're not the agent himself." "There you are mistaken, my man," was the reply, anal= ia very conciliatory tone by Lord Alfred,</p>
        <p>"If you are thoroughly well acquainted with tn. place, you can conduct us all over it, and point out rooms which we might otherwise miss. Before w either buy or rent the house, we want to know alt about it; and you shall be paid well for any troublu you may incur by being civil to us." "Oh, well, if that's it, sir," said the man, willing to do my best. But I have lived here ore the Q.T. a good while; and if I am to be turned out through somebody else coming to live in ths house, I hope you won't mind making it up to me.al, Lord Alfred looked at the man very keenly, and fancied he detected a gleam of amusement in his eyes. _</p>
        <p>- "The fellow thinks he can lead us off the scent,al he thought, "by pretending to be little better than a friendless tramp. But in spite of his unkempt hair and his dilapidated clothing, he looks toe smart a man to belong to the genus loafer; and the best thing we can do is to appear thoroughly taker&amp; in by him." With a great show of readiness the man whomi they found in Hamlet House officiated as guide but he ignored two rooms on the third floor; and on trying the handles of them while his back was turned Lord Alfred discovered that the doors were locked. Waiting until he heard the guide's foot- steps on the floor above, he stooped down and called softly through the keyhole: • "Are you there ? " There was no intelligible reply from within buts Lord Alfred felt sure that he heard someone moving in the room, ankdirectly afterwards the sound of returning footsteps warned him of the necessity of caution. Without a word of comment he joined the others as they went downstairs again, but prepared himself to act upon his discovery at once. 4 It had been thought that by raiding Hamlet House at the hour mentioned by Madame Celia, it would be possible to effect the capture of the kidnappers,, simultaneously with the release of their prisoners; but there really seemed no reason why the latter should languish in durance vile a moment longea- than was needful. "We must overpower this man and prevent hire from giving the alarm to his accomplices, and we will come back at twelve o'clock to-night with plenty of assistance; but if John Mills and Teddy; Quicke are shut in those rooms upstairs we wilt open their prison and take them away forthwith." 4 Such was the purport of what Lord Alfred whispered to Mr. Raleigh; and meanwhile the conspirators, fully aware of all his movements and. plans, were preparing to bring to a successful issue( 15ne of the most daring schemes upon which they- had as yet entered. (To be continued.)</p>
