        <p>CROMWELL. WARRINGiON CA7I-11-0-LICS UP IN ARMS.</p>
        <p>On Friday afternoon a deputation ef the Roman Catholic Clergy of Warrington waited upon the, Mayor at the Town Hall for the purpose of present.- ing a memorial against the proposed erection of a statue of Oliver Cromwell in the town. It will be remembered that at the last Council meeting it was decided to accept the offer of Mr. F. Monks, J.P., C.C., to present a bronze statue of Oliver Cromwell in celebration of the ter-centenary of the birth of that grant Englishman. The memorial was presen- ted by thellev.Fatber Wilson,and it had been signed by 2,121 Catholics. The text of the petition is as follows :—" May it please your Worship. We the undersigned Catholic Clergy and people of Warring- ton having heard with deep regret that the Town Council has accepted on behalf of the town a statue of Olieer Cromwell are pained to find that the Council could so far disregard the well-known senti- ments of so large a number of those whom they represent and with whose interests they have been entrusted. We hereby most strongly and indignantly protest against the erection of the statue on any public site or in any place belonging to the town. If not an eye-sore it certainly would be a daily heart sore in our midst. 'The evil that men do lives after them,' and we should regard the erection of the statue only as an outrage on our feelings, calculated to awaken anew and to perpettie ate in our minds the bitter remembrance of the wrongs wrought by Cromwell, a remembrance which all his greatness and the lapse of 250 years have not been able to efface. We conclude by earnestly hoping that when so much is being said about the peace of nations, nothing will be done which is calculated to disturb the peaceful relations at present existing between the citizens of out town."</p>
        <p>_ The Mayor promised that the memorial should have the fullest consideration and that he would lay the matter in the proper quarter. It was, however, pointed out to the deputation that the Council had accepted the statue and that it was on its way,to Warrington.</p>
