      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>(Copynght. )</head>
        <p>(Copynght. )</p>
        <p>PRINCESS DELILAH.</p>
        <p>By MRS. GEORGE CORBETT, Author of "When the Sea Gives up .its Dead,* "The Prince Physician," "The Vindication of Olive Trevethick, ' "The Star of Yukon," "The Adventures of an Ugly Girl," "Deb Melly's," "A Fair Blockade Runner," dr.o.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XVII. SOME REMARKABLE REVELATIONS.</p>
        <p>&amp;tr. Rizzen-Quicke eagerly embraced the chance of accompanying his intended son-in-law to the house of the famous seer, who had already been consulted by an ever-increasing clientele, in spite of the large fee which she charged for her services, and in spite of the independence she shewed in refusing to make any appointments except at times which suited her own convenience: Polly was just as eager to go as he was, but was destined to be disappointed, for Lord Alfred explained that if he were accompanied by too many people there might be some difficulty in securing the expected interview with the capricious fortune-teller. "But," he continued, "you shall know every- thing that takes place, and then we shall be able to judge if it will be wise for you to visit the lady on your own account."</p>
        <p>Virith this arrangement Polly had to be content, and while she and her mother speculated as to what would be the result of the coming interview, the two gentlemen, bearing the blotting-paper and seal with them, as well as a walking-stick which John Mills had left behind him on the night of Mrs. Rizzen-Quicke's memorable reception, hurried off to join Mr. Raleigh, and by nine o'clock they had reached their destination, which was a house in one of the streets abutting on Leicester-square. It had been agreed that no disclosures concerning the identity of either Mr. Rizzen-Quicke or Lord Alfred Vavasour-Pomeroy should be made, as it was considered that much more weight could be attached to the clairvoyante's anticipated revela- tions if she was kept in ignorance of the names and station of the people to whom those revelations were made.</p>
        <p>Privately, Mr. Raleigh thought that his com- panions had more money than wit when they unmurmuringly disbursed no less a sum than one hundred and fifty guineas, fifty guineas per head being Madame Celia's standard, fee for con- sultation.</p>
        <p>"However," he thought, "it doesn't matter much to me, so long as I haven't to pay the money myself, and in any case I expect to have a little fun to-night." The room in which the visitors were received by a handsome but taciturn Oriental was on the ground-floor, and when all preliminaries had been arranged to the said Oriental's satisfaction, he touched an electric biitton, whereupon another Oriental appeared and conducted the expectant gentlemen to an expensively-furnished room on the first floor.</p>
        <p>"Will you kindly sit down, sirs," he said, "until I see if madame is quite ready for you ?" All three complied with this invitation, and as madame was evidently not quite ready for them, they had plenty of time in which to observe their surroundings, which, though of a costly descrip- tion, were by no means conducive to cheerfulness. The upholstery of the furniture and the heavy velvet hangings which abounded in the room were of so dark a shade of blue that they were almost as depressing to look at as black itself would have been. Nor was there anything else about the apart- ment that was more calculated to divert attention from the approaching conference.</p>
        <p>It was apparently Madame Celia's desire to cultivate in her clients a state of mind in harmony with the gloom of the presence chamber, and when she ultimately shewed herself, her striking appearance accentuated the serious impression which had already been created. She was tall and of a commanding figure, and walked into the room with a dignified bearing which could not fail to do its share towards banishing any feelings of levity which might still persist in obtruding themselves. She wore a very handsome black tea-gown, and her head was partially covered by a Spanish lace mantilla, which made her pale face look positively ghastly. Thick coils of purple-black hair gleamed through the folds of the mantilla, and there was something altogether so uncanny about her that Mr. Rizzen-Quicke caught himself wondering if any possible good could come through attempting to have dealings with the devil. Mr. Raleigh did not go auite so far as that, for he was firmly convinced that the whole affair would turn out to be nothing but hocus-pocus. Never- theless, the solemnity which was observed had its due effect upon him, and he began to think some useful revelations might be made after all. As for Lord Alfred, his studies had brought under his notice such strange examples of mental phenomena that he was prepared not to be surprised at anything he might see or hear. "I am, ready, sirs," said Madame Celia, in a deep, rich vice, seating herself upon an ottoman facing her visitors. Almost imperceptibly the room had grown darker, and even as the clairvoyante spoke, a weird crimson radiance seemed to concentrate itself upon her, leaving all else in more intense shadow than ever. "I am ready," Madame Celia repeated. "If you have anything to. ask me, do so at once, for my time is valuable, and my Gift of Divination is not always with me."</p>
        <p>Lord Alfred, as spokesman7for the party, imme- diately produced the ,walking-stick which had belonged to John Mills. _ _</p>
        <p>"IVe have brought a few articles with us, madame," he said, "concerning whose owners we are hoping that you will be able to give us some information. This stick belonged to a gentleman whose whereabouts we are anxious to discover."</p>
        <p>"What are the other articles which you have brought with you ? " inquired the clairvoyante, the quietness of her manner being in striking contrast to the eagerness of her visitors. "We have a piece of blotting-paper and an article of jewellery. But all three things belong to different persons, and we thought--" "Excuse me," was the stern interruption, "I must be the sole judge of the advisability or other- wise of dealing with the things separately." Thus rebuked, Lord Alfred at once handed his "proofs" to the attendant who had followed his mistress into the room, and the man immediately laid them on a small table, which he placed in front" of her.</p>
        <p>Madame Celia held up her hands as a warning to her consultants to keep perfect silence, and then, even as the light shone still more dimly, a violent shudder was seen •to pass through her frame, and she gazed down at the table for several seconds without speaking. Presently the wave of emotion which had seemed to assail her passed away, and she sat calm, erect, and rigid, the while she droned, in a strangely impassive tone, the purport of her vision. Laying one hand upon John Mills's stick, she said: "I see a man of thiddle age and middle height. He seems to be prosperous now, but has the appearance of a man whose fight with the world has been a hard one. His hair and beard are flecked with grey, and he walks with a slight limp, the result of an accident in some far off country. "Good Heavens!" exclaimed Joe Quicke, 'that's him to a T!"</p>
        <p>But he was not permitted to speak further, for the attendant shook him somewhat roughly by the arm, and whispered: "Hush! If you interrupt like that, you will wake madame out of her trance, and she will tell you nothing more." "For God's sake keep quiet!" advised Lord Alfred; and henceforth, no matter how violent the surprise might be, the visitors curbed their emotion sufficiently to enable them to refrain from speaking again until there was no longerEany necessity for silence.</p>
        <p>"He is h• man," continued the clairvoyante, ."with no domesticities, and he is reserved in his dealings with most people. But there comes a time when he falls under the dominion of a designing woman, who schemes to get the whole of his wealth into her own control. His friends ;know nothing of this woman, for he is naturally taciturn, and the woman does not move in his social set. She is an adventuress, who hesitates at nothing. She promises to marry your friend. She decoys him to a large house which stands in its own grounds, has been untenanted for many years, and is supposed to be haunted. It is situated in the main road, on the outskirts of Hammer- smith, and is surrounded by huge trees. With the assistance of some confederates, ' she detains him there as a prisoner, while she converts into money a number of precious stones which he has been secretly hoarding, but which his infatuation for her has induced him to hand over to her for safe keeping."</p>
        <p>At the' close of this remarkable pronouncement Madame Celia was silent for a few moments, con- centrating her attention the while upon the blotting-paper, ere she resutned: _</p>
      </div>
