      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 10, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 10, 1899.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>This Council met in the To wn Hall on Tuesday even- ing. The Mayor (Alderman Wright) presided, and there were also present :—Aldermen Sut:on, Roberts,Hutchin- son, Blackhurst,Boltoa.Councillors Shaw Green,Cannell, Crosfield, Eleaton, Monks, Wilkinson, Pluropton, Smeth- urst, Lnngton, Bennett, Jahn Evans, Ellison, 13urton, James Evans, Baird, Tinnion, F R Roberrs, Peacock, Grace, Jolley, Rogers, Wo ds, Soillcock, John Rlberts, Lee, with Mr 41 Lyon Whittle (town clerk), Mr Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), Dr Gornall (medical officer), Mr Fairhurst (borough treasurer), Mr Longdin (bo,ough surveyor), Mr Dees (water engineer), Mr Haddock (gas engineer), Mr Madeley (librari In).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>TRIM AUDITORS.</head>
        <p>TRIM AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>Tbe TOWN CLERK announced that Messrs. John Harri- son and James Bolton had been re-ele3ted borough audi- tors, and that Mr. Councillor Bennett had been appointed Mayor's auditor. ,THE OMNIBUS ALTERATIONS. LIVELY DISCUSSFON. DISSATISFACTION EXPRESSED.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved, and Aldermai, BLACKEWAST secon- ded the adaptiou of the minutes of tue WAtch Com- mittee. _ . . _</p>
        <p>Councillor Beeresee drew attention to the minutes re- lating to the places fixtd upon by the committee as the 'cumin' of the 'buses plying for hire within the borough. He said he had heard numerous complaints about the alteration that bad been made, and it did seem to be putting the public to a large amount of unnecessary in- convenience. One great objection te the present arrange- ment was that ladies found it a great inconvenience t- carry parcels to the present termini, and then, perhaps, have to wait five minutes before the 'biases started. There had also been another objection raised which would no doubt, appeal to his temperance friends. (A laugh.) By the new arrangement the termini in almost every case were opposite public-houses. (Cries of" No," and laughter.) While the ownere and fortunate proprietors of tee public-house would no doubt derive benefit from that fact, he was not quite sure whether it was in the interests of teeop-rance, because they knew that the 'buses stopped for five minutes at a time in front of those pieces, and it was an inducement for thirsty souls wait- ing for the 'buses to go and have a " whetter up." (Laughter.) He suggested that, if possible, the com- mittee should revert to the old arrangement on Mondays Tuesdays, Thursdays, and Fridays. Councillor TINNION did not wish to interfere with the arrangement in regard to the termini of the 'buses if there had not been so many complaints. His idea was that the minute might be referred back to the commit- tee, and see if they could not adopt some arrangement whereby they could have through 'buses on certain days. Thera was certainly a great amount of inconve- nience occasioned by the present arrangement, and he thought they should consider the conveaionce of those who used the 'buses. Alderman SUTTON said it would be a strange thing, if there were not some objections to any alteration which might be proposed, either in reference to the regulation of the 'buses, or aay other alteration which might be made in the town. What he desired to point out was that the Watch Committee would have no ehjection to consider any suggestions which were mede to them, b let him ask them net to be too precipitate in the matter. (Hear, hear.) They had not yet had an opportuaity of seeing how the new arrangement worked. Let them give it a fair trial. The question had been discussed over and over again by the Watch Committee and they came to the conclucion that in the intereve of the particelarly, it was desirable that they should arrange the termini as they had been arraneed. From what they could gather from the Chief Constable and the inspector of 'buses, they found that the new arrangement was working very well, and that it was a great convenience to the public in every way. . Councillor BURTON said he had heard complaints in that part of the town where he resided, and those com- plaints were loud and general. Pri or to the alteration which the Watch Committee had made, the public were much better catered for in the matter of convenience. He would be glad if the committee could see their way to go back to the old arrangement. Councillor' WOODS said Alderman Sutton had spoken about the alteration being made in the interests of the shopkeepers. New, Alderman Sutton was not a shop- keeper, but he (Couucillor Woods) was, and he argued ' that the alteration had been dianut-ically opposed to the ' interests of the shopkeepers, for the simple reason that it was a most ridiculous alteration. (Laughter.) They had been told that another object which the committee had in view was to ensure the better controlling of the traffic, but he wished to inform the Council of an ex- perience of his own. last Thursday. He had occasion to go into Cheshire in a trap, and when near the Cairo-st. Chapel he narrowly escaped colliding with a 'bus which was coming round till corner. He was nearly costing the price of a public funeral in Warrington—(laughter) but by judicious handling of the reins,with a good horse in front of him, he managed to wcene. (Renewed laughter.) He was afraid that if the Alderman had been in his place they would have had to wear mourning for a long time. (Loud laughter.) He thought Alderman Sutton was speaking—as he himself sometimes said—off the book when he spoke about what he called the inter- ests of shopkeepers. - Alderman Smote : You are not the only shopkeeper.</p>
        <p>-ao.unoiller WOODS: I know I am not ills Only One. He is another. (Laughter.) Continuing. he urged that Market-gate should rot be blocked,but it did not follow that Saakey Bridges and the Cemetery should be 53 far disconnected that people had to walk neatly a quarter of a mile to get their connection. They had a bigger staff now in the police force than before, and if that staff could not cope with what had been going on during the past few months it was about time the Watch Com- mittee went into the matter as ta what the police force did. He said without fear of contradiction that the town could be managed in a better way as regarded its traffic without putting those people, who bad to pay the rates to keep the police going, and to assist to keep even the streets going, to the inconvenience to which they had been put since the Watch Committee adopted what he called a most foolish arrangement. Councillor Shaw Gam; said he constantly found in passing Sankey-street that the stopping of the 'buses at Bold-street Wad a great incolvenience. He wished to suggest that they should stop the Sankey-et. 'buses in Corporation-street. ' Alderman ROMIRTS said as one coming from the other end of the tewn he was rather surprised to hear the complaints which hal been made, and he thought at only fair to the Watch Committee and the police to let the new arrangement have a trial. Most of those who had spoken so far seemed to think that everybody who came from Sankey Bridges wanted to go to the Ceme- tery. (Laughter.) He could say this,that the people at his end of the town were very pleased with the arrange- ments, and the shopkeepers within a radius of 50 or 60 yards of Market-gate were also very pleased. He raised his voice in protest against any impression that might go forward that it was the unanimous opinion of those outside the Connell that the new arrangement was a failure. _</p>
        <p>1 --00-u-n—cillor SMETHURST said he had been waked to pre- sent a petition against the alteration of the termini, but he advised those who appreached him to let the commit- , tee have an opportunity of giving the new arrangement a fair trial. However, from/what he had heari, it was proving a great inconvenience. - Councillor JAMES EVANS suggestsi that the buses ,hould not stand at all in the centee of the town, but that they should simply stand at the termini. His idea was that they shmuld run right throueh. e Csuncillor GRACE said Councillor Evans had made a ood and reasonable suggestion, viz., that th 'buses be at allewed to Stand in vrhat they might call the centre and pick up their passengers on the way with- Vof the town, but go through the town from terminus to obstruction. lie moved that the minutes o"utetracealunstlien,a any be confirmed with the erception of the one relating tá the 'buses. Councillor PEACOCK Seconded C euncillor Grace's amendment, remaritinq that since the alteration of the termini of the 'bas,s the main streets hod been used more by lurrins and the heavier class of vehicular traffic than ever before. Courap,iller BURTON mapported the amendment. He idea t alteration was, and he thought he should psaid h 'had been asked over and over again whose silly age ti the q ry over to the %reel Committee. (Laughter.) It was for them to answer it. (R-mewed laughter.) As far as ho was conctroel, it was not a wise idea. (Heir, hear.) , ColliVillOr BENNETT wishei to know if the relic° in Warringten had such great difficulty in controlling some fatty or fifty 'hoses, how did the police in London, Man- chester, and Liverpool cope with the enormous amount of trafic they had to conteud with in more congested parts than any they had in Warrlngton ? He particu- larly mentioned Chancery-lane and the Strand. Councillor Woons : If the amendment is not cirried I think the police of Warrington are remiss in their duties: Alderman SUTTON thought it only fair that he as a merhber of the Watch Committee should resent such statements. If the police and the Watch Committee were not competent to understand this vestion, he did not think any other members of the Council were able to understand it. The only people he had heard speak about silly ideas were the individuals who. considered themselves wronged by the alteration having been made, as it removed the 'buses Poen their particular places of husinesz, and that, he took it, was the cause of Mr. Woods's contention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>CADBURY'S COCOA, on the testimony of the Lancet. " represent s the stsndand of highest purity.' It is entirely free from all foreign substances, such as kola, ma,t, hops, So., nor is alkali used to darken the colour (and so deceive the eye). Dr. Andrew Wilson, in a recent article in the illustrated Lonion News writes : " Cocoa is in itself a perfect food, and requires no addition oc drugs whatever CADBURY's Cocoi is absolutely pure, and should be taken by old cud young, at all times and in all seasons ;fr Children it is an ideal beverage, promoting healthy growth and development in a remarkable degree. Insist on having CAD PURY's, as other Cocoas are often substituted for the sake of extra profit, sou only in Packets and Tina.— Advt. (3.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>Councillor WOODS (warmly') : I strongly object to this, Mr Mayor. Alderman Sutton is au alderman, but if he was a young man he would perinea be a little less than an alderman. (Laughter.) I strongly object to this. I have spoken in what I corteid er acad,em'c language. I have not insulted him, and lam not going to be inallted by him. The MaYon : There is no question of insult. Couneillor WOODS : He used my name. The MAYOR: Ho did not. Coencilor %Tomos : He did use my name as Coun- cillor Woods. Alderman SUTTON wee about to continue his observa- tions when Councillor Wools called out " Sit down." The MAYOR: Order, order. Alderman SUTTON (resuming) did not see why C cain- cillor was entitled to use any other person's name half-a-dozen times and then suppose that that person should net have a word to say about himself. (Laugh- ter.) He urged that the Watch Committee should be permitted to give the new arrangement a fair trial, and he did not think members of the Council were justified yet in asking the committee to make any alteration. The MAYOR considered that it was very unfair indeed of members of the Council to enter their objections against the action of the Wateh Committee until thie al- teration had had a fair trial. He had heard n othing but praise about the alteratioes of the termini. What the committee had done had been in the interests of the public at large, and for the safety of the public, and not is the interests of just a few people. (Hear, hear.) It was unfair to attempt to send the resolution back until the scheme had had a fair trial. Oa a division 11 voted for the amendment and 16 in favour of the recommendation of the committee. The minutes were carried. THE VACCINATION ACT, 1898.—THE CONSCIENCE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>CLAUSE.</head>
        <p>CLAUSE.</p>
        <p>At a meetinst of the Health Committee it was resolved that a representation be made to the Local Government Board urging the necessity for the repeal of Sections 2 and 3of the Vaccination Act, 1938 ; that the Medical Officer of Health be authorized to make arrangemears with the University College at Liverpool,for the bac'erin- logical examination of sputa of diphtheria ; that the Chairman, Deputy-Chairman, and the Medical Officer of Health be appointed to attend the Sanitary Science Con- gress at Southampton, commencing August 29 h next ; that Mr. H. H. Ferguson be appointed a veterinary inspector for the borough, under the Diseases of Animals Act, 1894. Alderman SUTTON, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, said the Committee had decided that represen- tation should be made to the Leeal Governmint Board, urging the necessity for the reneal of sections 2 and 3 of the Vaccination Act of 1898. That resolution, he believed, was in accordance with simi'a, regolutions passed by tie other cornorationa and public bodies. The sections mentioned referred to the conscience clause,and it was considered that it should be abelished as well as the one which provided that no accumulated or repeated penalties were to be enforced. So far as Warrington were concerned thny were pleased to find that there WAS little or no opposition On the part of the inhabitants to compulsory vaccination, but at the same time it was considered that the restoration of vaccination stations would be most desirable under certaio condi- tions. There was another resolution to which he wished to call attention, and that was with regard to the bacteriological examination of sputa of diphtheria. They were favoured with the views of Dr. Gornall as to its importance,his exelanation being that diphtheria wai a disease which simulated ether f irma of throat affections, and was with d'fficulty distinguished from them, especi- ally in the early stags of the disease. The object, there- fore, of a bacteriological examination was to secure the early diagnosis of this dangerous infe:etious disease, so as to secure early isolation at the hospital. In answer to a question Alderman SUTTON sal d that the appointment of Mr. H. H. FeTgll3ol was to fill the position lately held by Mr. ShAWCrO3S. There were three inspectors besidea Mr. Ferguson, viz., Mr. Ferguson, sen., Mr. Hewitt, and Mr. J. Dixon. The minutes were confirmed after being seconded by Alderman Roberts.</p>
        <p>SANITARY WORKS COMMITTEE.-LONGFORD</p>
        <p>DEPO r.</p>
        <p>At a meeting held on the 22nd February, it was re- solved that the tender of Messrs. Joseph .damson and Co.. to supply a new dryer at Longford Depo:, be ac- cepted. Councillor Moxxs,-in moving the a :option of the minutes, said they were to ROW) extent anticipating their estimates for next year in regard to the proviiiin of a new dryer, but it did not mean an increase in the expendi'ure next year as compared with the present year. The reason for bringing the resolution forward was that the dryers at Longford Bridge had been in a defective condition for some time past. Tile dryer pre- viously purchased from Messrs. A.damson and Company, which had been working for about a month, had given every satisfaction, and the committee felt it was such an improvement on the old type that they could safely recommend the Council to confirm the present mrder,be- cause they believed it was the best dryer known at the present time. They had, as he had told them, had a month's experience of the dryer, and the way in which it was working gave every satisfaction. .</p>
        <p>Councillor .1-On-g EveNs -seconded the resolution. Councillor 'MONKS added that the conamittea had !told all the manure they had at their disposal.</p>
        <p>The tninutes were pAssed. THE PAVING OF STREETS AND PASSAGES.</p>
        <p>The llile.Yoa moved the confirmation of the minutes of the Paving and Sewerags Committee, He said he would like to draw attention to the enormous number of streets and passages which had been paved durinr: the past few months. The number was no less than 28, and the cost £2,417 10a. Haying regard to the fact that there was such a difficulty in getting hold of paving material, he thought the Council would admit that the committee had not been slow in doing what was absolutely nec.s- sary. Councillor HEATON seconded, and the minutes were confirmed.</p>
        <p>-T-1-1E MUSEUM COMMITTEE AND THE GYMNASIUM.</p>
        <p>Alderman ROBERTS moved the confirmation of the minutes of the Museum, Library, Arts and Technical In- struction Committee, and suggested a slight alteration on page 13 of the agenda paper, namely, that refPrring to the Museums and Gymnasiums Act. He moved that the vsolution read " so far as the same relates to the Museum." The Alderman explained why he di .1 so, and pointed out that they as a Museum Committee had no right to interfere with tha management of the Gym- nasium, which was in thoroughly competmt hands. (Hear, hear.) Councillor BENNETT seconded, and the motion was agreed to. THE CEMETERY.</p>
        <p>it was reported at a metin f the Cometery Com- mittee that tho intermonts for the past four weeks were —Adults, 52; minors, 62; stillborn and prematur' e 9. Corresponding period last ycar:—Adults, 37; minors, 36; stillborn and premature 9. The receiots for the pas.; four weeks were roported to ho V-01173 lid ; corns- ponding period last year, £B2 63 9d. The la:limit:el were approved, on the motioa of Coun- cillor JoaN EVA.NS' seconded by Councillor SHILLC3CII. TOWN HALL, PARK AND BATH i COUNIIrfEE. THE HIRING OF PLANTS. _ -</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the &amp;rove mentioaed committee held on the 23rd February it was resolved that a scale for the hire of shrubs and plantt be prepared; and that a charge be made of 23. 61. per hour for man's time occupied in conveying plants lent by this department for charitable and other purposes. Alderman BOLTON moved the confirmation of the minutes of the above cummittee, and said it would be seen that Mr. Robert Garnett had presented the com- mittee with an indiarubber plant, for which they were much obliged. A resolution had Iron passed that a charge be made of 21. 6d. per heur for men's time occu- pied in conveying plants lent by the department for charitable and other purposes. This decision had been come to because so maoy people were mine the plants. A great deal of the time of the gardenera was occupied in taking away and bringing back plants. Two men were required for the pur- pose, and it took up a great deal of time. Alluding to the fountain to be erected, Alderman Bolton said some of the outside comments had ben changed, and the changes had been considered suitable. The tender of Meosrs. Macfarlane and Company. Ltd., had been accepted. A scaffolding had been erecte 1 in front of Town Hall to enable the Committee to decide upon the b3st site. The first site was found to be too near to the hell, and then a site Was tried in the centre of the grass plot, and on Thursday next the Committee would have to consider whether it was suitable or not. ge thought the fountain was too large to b 3 placed in front of the Town Hell. (Hear, hear.) They wanted to keel up the individuality of the building, which had one of the finest fronts in Lancashire, and to put up a fountain to obliterate it would be wrong. It would not do to place it too near the gates. However, that was a matter fer the Committee to decide. (Hear, bear).</p>
        <p>Cruncillor Dr. CANNELL iiCcoaded the con-firmatlon of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor CROSFIELD thought a much batter plsition would be on the north side oithe hal!, in the centre of the graaa plot there. (Hear. hear).</p>
        <p>Tho MAYOR sai&amp;M.S3IS. Walker would. he felt sure, be content to leave the matter in the hands of the Coun- cil.</p>
        <p>Councllor F. R. ROBEXTS remarked that the charge of 20. 6d. would lead to a great deal of trouble, and he thought it would be preferable to make an inclusive charge. If they charged for the men's time by the hour there would be muctefriction as to whether the en'en had been employed an hoer, of an hour or 61 or 65 minutes. (Laughter.) People in charge of religious aod</p>
        <p>charitable bazaars were the most " tickle " and dis- agreeable peop.e it W 3.9 possiule to deal with. They w mid wrangle over a toreepenny bit when a man in the straet would throw it at you. (Laughter). He would move an amendment that an inclusive change ba made. Councillor BAIRD seconded the amandna,nt.</p>
        <p>Councillor Jons ROBERTS,: Half-a-crown an hour is too mach, particularly if you only send two men. Alderman BOLTON : Throe, one to hold the cart. Con- tinuing, he said the committee did not wish to lend plants at all ; as decorators said" You are interfering with our trade, you are decorating flr balls and parties in Parr Hall, art I taking trade out of our hands ; you ar. doing it for nothing, and how are we going to live?" They had no right as a comm,ttee to do this work for nothing. It required one man, in removing plants, to hold the truck and two men to hand the plants out.</p>
        <p>Alderman ROBERTS said the fact that the committee had len`, plams on so many occasions had given great satisfacion and pleaswe to many people in the town, and he hoped the practice vrould not he discontinued. It should be remembered that the property of the Parks Committee was the property of the general body of rate- payers, and he thought the suggestion which had been made eras a reasonable one,and that an inclusive charge should he made. They had no right to lend plants out- side the borough, atri if they made a charge to include the whole of the borough it would be better. Councillor HEATON : If people can have plants free of charge I shall want them in my back yard every night for my horses to look at. (Laughter.) Alderman Sturon rose to speak amid some interrup- tion and asked members to be quiet. . . .</p>
        <p>Councillor WOODS (satirically) : Be quiet, Alderman Sutton is talking. (Laughter.) Alderman SUTTON : rty and behave yourself, Mr. Woods. He contended that the cemmittee were losing by 13nding out plants; £lO had been lost this year alone.</p>
        <p>Councillor PirooDs : Scandaloul. (Llughter.) The MAYOR asked Councillor F R Roberts if he pressed his amendment.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS : I think so. Alderman BOLTON : We only want an expression of opinion. We don's want to adopt a hard and fast line in the case of charitable people, although we don's want to be a universal-lending-of plants committee. It was eventually decided to alter the resolution to read " That a reason tble charge be made for men's time," and the minutes were carried. THE ELEC ['RIC LIGHTING STATION. A NEW SITE. THE TELEPHONE MONOPOLY.</p>
        <p>Councillor MONKS moved the confirmation of the minutes of the Electric Lighting Committee, and allud- ing to tae Lancashire Electric Power Company and the South Lancashire and Cheshire Company, Limited, said the Council would be glad to know that their opposition had been successful, and that the Bills had been thrown out and done away with until next year. Then the question arose as to who should pay the expenee. They proposed te ask other Corporations to join with them in sharing the expense, because walla they took action on their own initiative they els) pined the Corporations of Manchester, Liverpool, and others in the efforts they were mek,ng to secure the same object. Referring to the agreement with the National Telephone Company, he said he should like to have had a stipulation to have had the effect of improving the service of Warrineton. They walla get an improvement indirectly because part of the object of the agreement with the company was to lay the wires undereround. Councillor Monks explained why that was an advantage, as there would be a me- tallic return instead of an earth return, which enabled them to hear more distinstly. The Gevernment had introduced a Telephone Bill into the House of Com- mons, and one of the provisions was that towns of 50,000 inhabitants and upwards should be eraatel a license by the Postmaster-General, if required'. If the company did not improve their service it was for the Corporation to start a telephone system of their own,and rather than run the risk of that the Telephone Company would d) something to improve the service. He must confess he had noticed an improvement of the tele- phone service durina the past two or three montas. Ceincillor Menks alluded to the deputations to Liam- ' dudno and Chester, and explained why these places were visited. He added that it was intended to visit two or three other places where there was an electric plant of the newest kind. They could not go on visits of teat character without learning a great deal. (Hear, 11,ar.) New as to the site for the central electric station. The gas works site was not the best. In the first place they would have to take over some old build- ings which were not suitable, and would require exten- sive alterations. The price of the land would be qd or 51 per yard, whereas that at Howley would only be lid (Hear. hear.) Then they could save something lice £4,000 in the cost of building. The works at Howley would be situated on the river bank, and they could use the water of the river for condensing purposes, and in that way get a more economical station than if they were in the centre of the town and cempelled to use high pressure engines. In tbeit own interests, as well as in the interests of the Gas Committee, it was desir- able to adopt the site at Howley. Alderman BOLTON seconded, and said the Howley site was the best for the reasons which had been named. The land at the gas works was saturated with gas ref use,and the electric light might cause explosions. Councillor BENNETT was phased the site had been changed, as it offered several advantages. A large num- ber in the neighbourhood of the gas works were against the proposal to erect a destructor there, and it would be a relief to their minds to find that the site had been changed. It was :a great advantage to be able to use the water for condensing purposes, but could they not also use the water at the weir ? He had been told there was not sufficient force over the weir to render the matter worth consideration. Had the opinion of an en- gineer been taken on the question ? At times a consi- derable quantity of water went over the weir, and be stioald not like the matter to be passed over wi;U9Ut some consideration being given to it by an expert. Alderman ROBERTS said he did not think cue Council was sufficiently aware of the importance of the first minute. Powers were sought by electric companies to overrule the powers of the Corporation, and tuey were advised by the Town Clerk to oppose the Bills on Stand- ing Orders. They did so, and his advice was Wired to be sound, and reflected great credit upon him. (Hear, hear.) It hal amazed him to find that only Warrington and two comparatively small diserict councils were round to oppose the Bills on Standing Orders,aud if they had not taken the action they did, they would. have road to tight the matter in Committee at much greater expense and greater risk. They had, therefore, a claim on other towns to join in tee comparatively small ex- penses that had been incurred. It was very satisfactory to had that the advice which had been given by toe Town Clerk was so sound and good. (Hear, hear.) The Mame said the interests of the Corporation in being aole to supply their own electricity were being closely watched. He thought the resolutiou to remove the site to Rowley was as admirable one, and he hoped it would meet with the unanimous support of the Coon- ell. In the Gas Works site they would have to deal wita old buildings,atul they would cost as much as an entirely new site, and the buildings when completed would not have been as satisfactory. Couecillor MONits see' if there was one thing , he did admire about Councillor Bennett it was his pertinacity. (Laughter.) The question ha had raised had been answered on previous oectsions. With regard to the destructor there was no nuisance ia connection with the working of it,and what- ever opinions people might hold to the contrary he con- tended it was born of ignorance and prejudice. It was quite possible to have a dustruecor workiug without people knowing it had been started. Any attempt to utilize the weir would be like getting an elephant to pick up a pin. They had no right to the use of the weir, acid there was no power in it. The Boroueh Surveyor had informed him that there was not an ounce of water go- ing over the weir that day, and that often happened, and when there was a flood or a 16 feet tide the water was level at the weir and there was no power to be util- ise I. 'There was no power in it, and any attempt to fix machinery would bs useless, as it would be idle halt tee time. (dear, hear.) The minu-as were carried. FAIR CONDITIONS OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Goneral Purposes Committee, a letter was read from the Warrington Trades and Labour Council requesting that the " fair conditions of labour " be strictly observed in all contracts given out lov the Corporation, and the Town Clerk was instructed in re- ply to state that the resolution passed by the Council on .the subject had been adhered to. The ininutea were agreed to. oa the motion of the Mayor, seconizbd by Alderman Sutton. THE PROPO3EO TECHNICAL INSIITUTE. A SUGGESTION BY COUNCILLOR MONKS. The MAYOR asked the Council to consider the advis• ability of acquiring a site fora Technical lostitute, and to pass resolutions thereon. He proposed that the ole: of Mr. John White to sell a plot of land in Museum-street adjoining the County Coon, the Gymoasiana and Parr Hall, be accepted, sunjoct to a slight medifiaation as to street making, which, he added, would be doae to their entire satisfaction. Councillor MONKS seconded, and said the matter had been considered very carefully, and on the ground of economy they recommended the purchase of the site. The other site was, perhaps, the most important arid valuable. It seemed to him that here . was a splendid opportunity for a gentleman to buy the site and make it arida sultanle building a present to the town.(Elear,hear andapplause ) It was an opportunity which had not presented itself before for a gentleman or a number of , gentlemen to erect as a memorial a building which would be put to excellent use. He would like to throw , out the suggestion in the hope that someone might come ! forward and purchase the site and put tap tnere a ' handsome building which would be a place of great pub- lic utility, and a moaument to any man. (Hear, hear.) I The motion was then carried, and the Council rose.</p>
