      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>EDITH DORA</head>
        <p>EDITH DORA</p>
        <p>Of all the remedies given to children, there is none sosplendidly adapted to their complaints as the genuine Scott's Emulsion. There are so many pro- perties of Scott's Emulsion which have decidediy beneficial effects upon the growing body, that it is extremely difficult in a limited space tl dwell upon them. It really takes a physician to understand why Scott's Emulsion bas such far-reaching effects.</p>
        <p>The cases where Scott's Emulsion has trai;formed sickly babies and children into perfect types of health are continually coming to notice. Here is one where a little baby was wasting away with con- sumption of the bowels and marasmus. You can read the hl6t3r7 of it in the following letter :- 4, Vermont Road, New Town, " Ruethall, nr. Tunbridge Wells, "April 23n3, 1898.</p>
        <p>"Dear Sirs,—l have much pleasure in recmn- mending your valuable medicine. I have proved its worth by personal use with my baby ,who is now one year and two months old. She seemed to be a nice baby born, mita she was six weeks old, when she had a kind of a fit, and was almost strangled. After that she gradually got worse and worse, until she became quite a skeleton. We tried everything we could think of, but nothing seemed to do her any good. She was wasting a way with consump- tion of the bowels and marasmus, and wasting of the body. I resolved to try Scott's EMULILM, which to my greatest pleasure has proved very beneficial, for the baby that was so very thin has now got so fat and strong</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.6.A. SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Seciety commenced its seventh year on Sunday afteraooa. Tne Rev. John Yonge presided. In the pulpit with the pastor was Dr. Waite, of Rock Ferry and his sou, Mr. W. H. Waite. Mr. Waite gave an excellent rendering of the solo. " The King of Levis my Shepherd is," tor which he was warmly applauded. —rhe Rev. John Yonge said he was pleased to welcome them at that their seventh birthelay.ls hardlylooked like six years since they were bra. They would recollec7, the anxiety they felt on that birthday, how they vroa- dered bow things were going to turn out, the suspense they were all in, and the fear which took possession of their hearts up to ten minutss to three o'clock; but from that time, the 3rd March, 1893, their hearts were need with joy and their tongues with rejoicing, for the mem- bers came in as it they knew their way to the plaee,and, thank Goa, Lucy tied been coining in ever since. (Ap- plause.) One good result of the society was that more efforts had been made la Warrington since for toe wel- fare of men, as vario as missiee meetings had been es- tablished in churches and. chapels. He noticed to his great delight, certainly wen curiosity, that his friend the Vicar of Holy Trinity,who had not been at all averse in days past to pour a great deal of criticism and con- tempt on these meetings for men, had started one for himself that afternoon, and he hoped he would have a good meeting. Of course they knew that the new Vicar of St. James's Letcleford,with the zeal which often cher- acterised a new beginner, had started a gathering on Sueday afternoon for men with considerable success, and he wished him all prosperity also. (Applause.) At the same time, they were anxious that their own P.S.A. Society at Wycliffe dread flourish, prosper, and grew. (Applause.) Tue Rev. Jahn Yonge concluded with a re- tereace to the siek club. (Applause,)—Dr. Waite, Wll3 was warmly applauded, !observed that there were in the district of Liverpool a dozen of these P.S.A. meetings, all of whictethe visited occasion- ally, and it was gratifying to think that in all of them the interest was well maintained and the attendance kept up. They believed that the P.S.A. movement was not only a most important one, but that it was destined to be one of the most valuable auxiliaries of the Church of Christ. The speaker proceeded to refer to" flee rela- tions b.Lt wees Ceristianity and Service," accentuating tiete3 points—ths history of service, its mutual charae- tor, and Christ's ideal of it. He pointed oat ths.t during the last half of the List century Liverpool was the most important slaving port in the United Kingdom ; the profits derivee trom the traffic were enormous, and the result was that ie was a very strenuous opponent of abolition. Entire abolition became the law et the land in 1810. Reference was also made te feudalism and the wages geestien, aad he pointed out that service to-day was being treated as a marketable commodity. The worker hal his rights and his political liberty, and he could protect himself against oppression. Nevertheless people were now regarding the wages system only as in a transition stage, and there were indications in differeat parts of the country of tic leavening power of Cnrissianity, to venial they owed all their liberty. For instance, in a village near Birkenhead they saw miles of streets and roads surrounding the great factory there. There ese-.re to be seen beautitully built and well-planned cot:agee for the employes, not built in rows like fist walls, but in two 3 and threes, ,semi-detached, dotted out amongst the trees, and letting at from 3s to 6s per week—while in the great hall lectures were given, as well as C3tlCCriS and entertainments. There were also billiardi aud read- ing rooms, got and cricket grounds, a girls' nstiseLe, a day school, and services were hsld every Sunday morn- ing and evening,conducted by ministered all denoiniva- , tions. That was an indication ot the signs of the times, land of the leavening influence of Christianity. The mov- ing spirit in all that enterprise a great and prosperous concern, was brought .up as a boy in the Sunday school of a Congregational Church. (Applause.)— Mr. Waite having given. an excellent interpretation of " Honour and Arms," for which to was warmly ap- plaud Lei, the flee. John Yonge announced that 425 members had been enrolled that afternoon, and they would like to see the number increased to 1,000. Mr. Bristow and Mr. Healey also took pars in the meeting.</p>
