        <p>We mentioned at the °inset of this article the many uses of Scott's Emulsion, and we think you will yourself understand better its scope when we tell you Vat it contains cod-liver oil, hypophos- phit--,s, and glycerine, so perfectly blended into a perfect emulsion that it is absorbed into the blood almost without any digestive effort. It is so pala- table that children love the taste of it,and the eager- ness with which they take Scott's Emulsion speaks volumes in its favour, as every mother whose chil- dren have a natural aversion to medicine will admit. Scott's Emulsion nourishes and strengthens babies and children, overcoming their wasting tendencies when all othz,r remedies prove of no avail. If you have a child that does not thrive, even if it be the smallest infant, we suggest that you obtain Scott's Emulsion from your nelrest chemists, and see for yourself the wonderful good it does. You may obtain a sample of Scott's Emulsion by sending threepence to cover postage to Scott and Bowne, Ltd., Manufacturing Chemists, 95, Great SaffronSaffronHill, Lancloo, E.C., and mentioning this paper.</p>
        <p>1 THE BURNING FATALITY AT WARRINGTON. On Monday afternoon, at the Borough Police Station, Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, held an ini quiry into the circumstances attending the death of Thomas Gearing, six years of age, son of Richard Gaerina, brewer's tabourer, 38, Queen-street, which took place on Friday at the infirmary, from injuries received the; previous Sunday.—Hannah Gearing, deceased's mother, said that on the morning in question, at seven o'clock, her husband made the fire and went to work. About 8 o'clock her daughter (Margaret Alice) called out to ask witness if she was coming downstairs, and then said almost in the same breath " Our Tommy's on fire ! " She ran downstairs and put the flames out with water, and he was taken to the Infirmary. Deceased was wearing a flannelette nightdress at the time.— The CJroner : That is infiarnmable.—liargaret Gearing said that on the morning in question her brotheriwas standing on the fender, striking the chimney with the poker, when his nightdress caught fire. She called her mother.—Nurse Hill said the deceased when admitted was suffering fr2m burns on face, body, legs and arms. He died at dm o'clock on Fri-lay morning last.—The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental death."</p>
