      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>EXNMINEI:,</head>
        <p>THE EXNMINEI:,</p>
        <p>MARCH 10th 1899.</p>
        <p>THE Liberals at Hull have so LCounciliberals in obviously been in better spirits that some persons may wonder what it all means. The matter cannot be easily explained, except one strong word be employed—reaction—and that drives us back into other statements and references. The Liberal party is beginning to recover its vigour, its good spirits, its general combative disposition. Prob- ably the reception given to Sir H. CAMPBELL- BANNERMAN, as the new leader 5.n the Commons, would seem to indicate that his selection has had something to do with the improvement. We honestly believe this to be the case. Ties fighting speech he made at Hull, the effect it had upon a big audience, and the effect it will have upon Liberals elsewhere may be taken as evidence that something has changed, and that more stirring times are ahead. We intend to convey no disrespectful suggestion relative to Sir WlLLrem HARCOURT'S leadership and retire- ment. Ho has largely contributed to bring ' about the betterment which his successor will en- joy. The Liberals, as a bo,4y, however, have recovered from two or three great disasters—Mr. GLADSTONE'S retirement and death, the great-de- feat of 1895, and the competition which was to carry into law so many beneficent measures that the Liberal programme was to be superseded and rendered obsolete. The anticipations of 1895 have not been realised. We have not had a strong Government, either in foreign or in domestic affairs. Concessions have been the order of the day all round. Not even the supporters of the Govern- ment have been satisfied with the conduct of foreign affairs. Its strongest critics have been found amongst its elected supporters or in the journals which had previously believed in Lord SALISBURY as the one strong and capable mau who would shew the world what England could do. In domestic affairs, as Lord CLAUD HAMILTON has said, the supporters of Lord SALISBURY are a "flabby party," They have allowed him to give doles to Voluntary schools and to landlords, and to disarrange our financial system. These matters could not escape Sir H. CAIIPBELL-BANNERMAN'S attention at Hull. The Coalitionists are by no means at peace with themselves. They are split into groups, and the accidents of 1895 are magni- fied into a declaration that engenders arrogance and pride. They think that Liberalism is dead for a generation. The Hull meetings are an answer to these extravagances. Liberalism is very much alive. It is daily recovering from its defeats. It is beginning to organise itself for another trial of strength against the forces of reaction, priestcraft and privilege. The battle will be more severe next time than it was last, and there will be a rear- rangement of electoral forces. Conservative schemes of reform are always more imposing on paper than they are in practice; and it is obvious that general dissatisfaction exists, which must manifest itself strongly when the opportunity of another General Election occurs. Hence Liberal counsels at Hull were tinged with enthusiasm and hope.</p>
        <p>DIE report:of the Special Com- mittee of the London Chamber of</p>
        <p>- Commerce on Secret Commissions is a valuable and significant document. It makes it clear that the giving and taking of commissions has now reached almost every branch of business, professional as well as commercial, and that unless a stand be made against it, and legislation be in- troduced to make some of it forms a cr;nainal offence, there will be widespread demoralisation. The committee finds that " the practice is produc- ing great evil, alike to the morals of the com- mercial community and to the profits of honest traders." The evidence received, chiefly of a private and confidential natere,has enabled the committee to describe the various forms which commis- sions take, and hence the full extent of the evil is disclosed. The committee points to the purity at- tained by various public bodies and servants within the last fifty years as a hopeful encourage- ment in the struggle for commercial purity, and to the bearing of legal decisions, some of which are epitomised in an appendix, as an indication 1 that the law has some remedies to offer. On the point as to whether more legislation is required the observation is cautious and shrewd. " If legislation is to be attempted, it appears to your committee that it should render criminal the pay- ment, the receipt, the offering, and the solicitatiora of any corrupt payment, and also the giving of any invoice or other document calculated to enable the recipient to commit a fraud upon his principal; and that it is expetlient that such legislation should be i ntiated by your Chamber, or supported by a petition by them." Should the Chamber regard legislation as desirable, the preparation of a Bill I ought to be remitted to a committee," with such , assistance as they may think proper to invite." In the meantime some useful suggestions are made. They re-state the civil rights of the principal—as recovery of the commission, instant dismissal, re- pudiation of contract, (U.—and call attention to the example set by the Institute of Pritish Archi- tects and Civil Engineers, in making a by-law by 1</p>
        <p>which any member can be expelled if found guilty of taking or giving commissions. A definite agreement with traders, stipulations about refusal of commissions in contracts between master and servant, circulars to traders and ser- vants, and increased vigilance all round, are recommended by the committee. Commit- tees of clubs and other public institutions may very well make provision in their agreements ad- versely to commissions. Buyers and others are often paid inadequate salaries, and are thus ex- posed to temptations. Useful as these suggestions are, they do not cover the whole ground, and they cannot be worked with effect unless heads of firms are themselves anxious to ensure purity and be at the trouble to keep their eyes open. The gifts which mark Christmas and other times of the , year cannot very well be dealt with as illegal, and yet they often become so marked as to amount to direct bribery. Press and pulpit can help the Chamber of Commerce ir this beneficent crusade, as well as the House of Commons. Every kind of force will be needed to bring about more honest trade, and there is hardly a household in the land which will not benefit by the purification of a sys-, tern which too often ends in dishonesty and the payment of high prices for inferior articles.</p>
        <p>We offer our hearty congratulations to the Warrington Corporation on the satisfactory issue of the poll which has been held during the week end. The majority of over 7,000 in favour of the Bill, and the fact that there were only 2,38:2 votes against it form a striking testimony to the confidence reposed in their representatives by the ratepayers. The fuss made by the "Property Owners' and Ratepayers' Association," and the virulence of their famous manifesto, suggested a much closer contest. It is now evident that the real opposition came from a few only who sought by misrepresentation and clamorous outcry to influence the minds of the people against the pro- posals of the Corporation. There was some reason to fear that the inability of many persons to fully grasp the salient points of the Bill would create a serious obstacle in the way of its progress towards consummation, but all apprehension has been dis- pelled by the decisiveness of the poll. The Cor- poration may now "push on," and bring to early fruition the more urgent schemes embodied iOlict comprehensive whole.</p>
        <p>Cruikshank believed in the conversion of the evilly disposed by pictorial representations. The same kind of idea has occurred to other people since his day ; and are we not living in the age of the artistic poster ? In France just now there are moral artists, intent on weaning the people from the increasing vice of drunkenness. Pictures of drunkenness in all its stages are now to be seen ia the barracks, workshops, and other places where persons meet who are at all likely to be affected by an appetite for alcohol. It is believed—and probably with some show f:A reason—that the artistic nature of the French people will enable them to see that it is very "bad form" to imbibe too freely, and that is lowering the sober Frenchmen to the level of—well, the English artisan, who is often supposed to be drunk because he is clumsy, and to be poor because he is both. Unhappily, whatever the explanation may be, there can be no doubt of the increase of in- temperance in France. The consumption of alcohol per head was always large, and as long as it waq pure and wholesome, as well as cheap, there Nw very little intoxication. But France is very heavily taxed, and workmen and others are tempted by cheap wines and by absinthe, which have Leen very ' much adulterated of late, to indulge too freely in their potations, with disastrous effects to their home life and general character. The pictures we have referred to are part of a temperance move - ment, and we wish them every success. But we are disposed to think much stronger measures will be required to check the degeneration of the ; French people.</p>
        <p>We cannot say that the Catholics of Warrington have displayed good taste or done that which ig best calculated to preserve what they have termed "the peaceful relations at present existing between the citizens of our town," by presenting a memorial to the Mayor against the erection of a statue of Oliver Cromwell. Whatever harm the erection of a work of art can do the Catholics we fail to see. They might just as well object to the hang- ng of a picture of Henry VIII in the Art Gallery or seek to pull down the statue of William IV. in Dublin. The objection comes with less grace see- ing that the Council has already accepted the gift of the statue. What is the use of accepting the gift, if, forsooth, it is not to be erected "on any public site or in any place belonging to the town?' Surely at the dawn of the Twentieth Century we have sufficient religious toleration to be above these petty displays of feeling; if not, it is a striking commentary on the teaching of the Christian Church. We understand that the statue is now on the way to Warrington, and if it pos- sesses anything of the nature of its original, it will arrive in town as certain as the great P.. otecter did at the time of the Civil War. The most suitable site would be in front of the Old A.cademy,and ag , this seems to find most acceptance it is to be hoped that the Catholics will, on second thoughts —which are proverbially best—withdraw their objections.</p>
        <p>America and Americans have had some peculiar experiences within the last few weeks, as a resulb of the intensely severe weather which has prevailed, but none we have heard so far equal a story from Kansas. In this State prisoner profited by the obsorption of his gaolers in tha problem: how to keep warm, to make his escape. But the enterprising gaol- breaker's triumph was short-lived. A temperature varying between 50 and 80 degrees of frost was too much for him, and after an hour or two of freedom he came to the conclusion that imprison- ment with warmth was preferable to liberty em- bodying every chance of being frozen to death, and he returned to gaol a sadder and wiser man. It is a pity for some things that similar weather has not prevailed in this country. The police might then have had a chance of laying by the heels that troublesome law-breaker—John Gar- lick, alias many other attractive names. Still, Gar- lick, at large is, perhaps, more endurable than the blizzards which devastated some parts of America last month.</p>
        <p>;uliar •esulb has far ite</p>
        <p>18atre 1nt%11.!., 3 3 V•L, be C. 3._.6,11. ovIP, boo 24 tbe estedi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>MESDAY.—Before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, ma</p>
        <p>Mr. J. D Jolliffe (registrir of the court.) THE LISTS —There were 12 plaints, 8 default plaints. S adjourned plaints, and 8 judgment summons.—The fended cases were heard before the Registrar. THE AFFAIRS OF A RUNCORN TONTINE CLUB—GOOD Arovico.—This was a case in which James Percival, nuisance inspector to the U.D.C., sued Thomas Findlow. Blidgewater- street, and George Littlemore, engineer, Waterworks (the secretary and treasurer respectively of the "George Inn Tontine Club for the sum of 6s.—James Percival stated that, for several years he had been the treasurer of the 1` George Inn Tontine Club," and WAS in the habit of paying the cea- tribution for the president of the club, himself and another member. He then went on to describe how laxly the affairs of the club were conducted, when his Honour asked how the claim was to be established. Three letters were submitted by the plaintiff who then stated that he claimed the returrt of the money Otl behalf of George Riley and himself, on the grounds that the club was so badly carried on.—His Honour failed to see why the claim should have been brought before him, but decided to adjourn the ease until the next emir.. In the meantime he advised that the Tontine Club hold* meeting to which they should invite the plaintiff and let him see some sort of accounts as to the disposal of the fund,' and how the club had been carried on. 1-f-?. would adjenoi the case in order that the defendants took that course. There was no other business of importance.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT TO TO AN ACTOR AT THE: ROYAL C3trap</p>
        <p>THEATRE.</p>
        <p>An accident happened at the Royal Court Theatre on Sunday afternoon, prior to the cimmeaceiwat! of a s2cred concert and exhibition of pictures ufg Messrs. Ward and Daisley's company, wno had beer performing at the Theatre Royal lt week. !!-,;! Edwin Daisley, one of the company, Wal rem 011_,," some goods, when he fell on his back on t 3 a sustaining serious injuries. He was r :I:l3ved t o Infirmary and, after havinz been n3di.;aily tr eat'/ was detained as an in-patient.</p>
