      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Sr. LITRE'S SUND klr SCHOOL P.S.A.</p>
        <p>In connection with St. Luke's Sunday School the usual monthly meeting was held on Sunday. Mr. Slade occupied the chair, and introduced the p °gramme. Toe principal speaker was Mr. Parker, and solos were ren- dered by M 199 Jones and Mr Harrison, and a recitation by Miss E Owen. There was a large audience, and the proceedings wore greatly enjoyed.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OLD HELSBT RESIDENT.</p>
        <p>William Collier. of Helsby, died on Wednesday at the age of ninety-two years. For twenty-six year the deceased was matriger of the Weston Chemical Works, near Runcorn, and afterwards was a manufacturer on his own account. Thirty years ago he retired, and engaged in farming. He was a man of original views, a strong Radical, and a Nonconformist. He was widely known in Cheshire. SONS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>The ordinary me-ting of the above order was held on Saturday evening under the presidency of Mr. Thomas Cooke. There was a good attendance. Before the close of the meeting the Chairman referred, in feeling terms, to the late Mrs. Heesom, who took a,deep interest in all matters relating to temperance for many years, and had done all she could to further the interests of their society. Messrs. A. Noden, Ed. Key, T. J. Holt, and Daniel Bisbrown also spoke of the loss sustained through the death of Mrs. Heesom ; especially to the cause of temperance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN.</head>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night, Mr. John Littler delivered a lecture on the" Life and travel of Dr. Livingstone " (illustrated with lantern slide) at the above Mission, to a large and attentive audiPnce. At the close of the lecture a number of comical slides were exhibited, as well as the illus- trated temperance story " The lopttle and its results." The lantern and slides were manipulated by Mr. Box- 'worth. Votes of thanks to Mr. Littler and Mr. Box- worth for their kindness in providing so esjovable an evening were carried with acclamation on the motion ef "Mr. W. Shaw (missionary). •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WIDNES POPULLR CONCERTS</head>
        <p>THE WIDNES POPULLR CONCERTS</p>
        <p>Saturday night saw the Baths Assembly Room well filled with an appreciative audience. Mr A Bennett occupied the chair. Mr F Price was applauded for his humorous songs" Oh ! don't you feel jolly and gay ?" •and " Baby language," as well as for his sketch" Re- citers up to date." OEr humorous items were contri- buted by Mr H Gregory, "A country choir concort," and 4IA tale of the Mile End-road," being his best. The other items included banjo solos by Mr J Hart, entitled "The Fusilade" and "The Indian Patrol," and songs by Messrs Smith and W Hackney. The accompanist was Mr J Gill.</p>
        <p>HARD LINES ON "KNOBBY."</p>
        <p>At Runcorn Police Court on Friday, Henry Cooke, alias" Knobby," a well-known character, was charged in custody with being a suspected person frequenting premises unlawfully, with intent to commit a felony, on the Ist March.—Rose Williams, married woman, living in Ivy-street, said she was at Mr. Hailwood's sale on Wednesday March Ist. Prisoner was there and she was very much annoyed at his conduct, for he continually placed himself behind her, wherever she moved to. She complained to Mr. Weston (chemist), about the man. Cooke was not known to her, and did sot put his hand in her pocket or molest her, but she was very much an- noyed.—J. Henry Weston, chemist, Dsvonshire-square, said he was present at the sale. The last witness com- plained to him about Cooke, she being just on the point to go out as he cam) in. He said nothing but watched Cooke. and saw that he was continually placing. himself behind females. He did not see him put his hands into their pockets, though Mrs. Williams had said" she believed he intended to pick her pecket."—P.C. Gregory gave evidence of apprehension. Numerous complaints had been received and some things were missed, but nothing was found on the prisoner.—Cook said he was sorry it had occurred, but his intention was not to do anything of the sort. He was simply there looking for a job, and would be glad enough to snap up anything.— The Bench sentenced him to 2 months imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>.RUNCORN NOTES,</head>
        <p>.RUNCORN NOTES,</p>
        <p>(BY A CORRESPONDENT.] One does not like to engage in a tirade against anyone, still I feel bound to call attention to the fraternity that asse,mble with regularity and pre- cision in the vicinity of the Station Gates. Com- plaints of their profanity are coming in by the score, so I suggest that one of our missionaries should travel in that direction occasionally—he need not necessarily wear buttons.</p>
        <p>The Parish Councils do not these days appear to be the attractive offices that the rush consequent upon their inception would have led one to believe, and in Runcorn Union this year the whole lot will be re-formed with simply a contest at Weston- -where the Weston Ward has 11 candidates for 8 seats.</p>
        <p>The case of neglect which came before the Run- corn Bench on Monday discloses facts which should make our`Grban District Councillors shudder. Are they doing their duty, by allowing people to live in such property. Where is the Nuisance Inspector? Where are the ministers of the several churches ? Are their conciences so elastic that they can allow such •filth and squalor to exist in their district? Per- sonally I think that if "those in authority "had done their duty that poor man would never have 'had to do .4 months.</p>
        <p>The sight which the Public Hall presented on Wednesday was one in striking contrast to the 4ne the preceding note alludes to. Seated to a capital meal were numerous aged persons, and their wants were attended to with care end consideration by a number of young ladies and gentlemen who ap- peared to take a delight in their onerous task. It was a happy picture, that gathering of the aged 'poor from all parts of the district. My only hope is that now they have been discovered and brought to light, some one may be stirred to take a general interest in them always.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>There were no flies' on the form displayed by the Runcorn team on Saturday—it was good old cup form, and" nowt else." From start to finish there was only one team playing a winning game. 1 don't mean to say Oldham were not playing —for really it was very evident they were doing their beet; but what I want to impress upon you is that Runcorn were doing better. The way those for- wards worked! Why it was a treat! And if all the glowing accounts of "How the Scotch forwards ran over the Welsh," were rewritten in the praise of the Ruucorn pack it would only be their due. They were all good. If anyone doubts it.; let them ask Fletcher and Martin.</p>
        <p>Oldham were fairly beaten and Fletcher, Martin and Thomas bad to work their nails off to avert going under in the first few minutes, and as for the second hail? Well ! They had hardly any nails left, and so could not come up to " scratch." There was hardly a man in the Oldham team that did not play as he never played before. The form displayed at times was equal to anything that Oldham ever did display. The game was keen, exciting,and withall a fine exhibition of the Northern Union code. The referee gave the men just as much licence as could have been desired, and they took it, for they were up and down like nine pins all the time, Luckily ,Ino one was seriously hurt.</p>
        <p>T'ne rush of candidates for seats on the Urban Coun3il is somewhat surprising. At first it was generally understood that Bridzewater was the only ward likely to cause an election, but according t) the return of the nominations there are now three candidates for Newtown, and two for Bridgewater tuid Mersey respectively. What tile probabilities are with regard to elections is difficult; to tell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>FRODS HAM.</head>
        <p>FRODS HAM.</p>
        <p>UEOVING FROM Doom TO Doom.—Thomas Thompsln (a tramp) charged on Monday before Mr. Charle3 Rey- nolds, with bezc,in.r.., from door to door in Chester Main- road, He!shy, was committed for seven days. Tte case was proved by P.C. Needham.</p>
        <p>Fooropkr.r..—The return fr,otbill match between Prod- * ham and Elelsby in the Chester and District Junior league, excited cmisiderable local enthusiasm and not- withstanding unpropitious weather, the H:±lsby gmund was well patronised. The final result was Helsny 6 .goas ; Frodsha.m 1 oal.</p>
        <p>CON.CERIV AT THE TOWN' HALL:—On Wednesday even- ing a eoncert of a miicellaneaus nature, wai given by a 111minblar of Cnestsr artistes, at the Town Hall, Frodsham, and wal well attended. The puceeds were in ati of the Pricaitivl MethPdist Chapel. The programme proved biaty id:nesting, acid included amongst other itani : Songs by Mice Maggie Walmsloy, and Mr. James Hun4hreyi • recitations by Miss James, and humorous readings by Mr. W. Z. Hallmark. A ladies string band also contributed setections of music, which wcr3 highly appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WESLEY Grrmo.—On Monday evening Miss London nave as interestiag lecture entitled " Emerson " to the members of the ITesley Guild irr the Wesleyan school- room. The Presidint, the Rev. G. H. Roberts, was in the chair, and there was a large aLtendance.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIU UNDER THE Mali FoR BEST</p>
        <p>BOOTS iN LANCIVEHIRE.—Advb,</p>
        <p>A DEPLORABLE HOME IN RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A FATHER SEN r TO PRISON.</p>
        <p>A deplorable state of things in a home in Lightfoot's- square, Rencorn, was disclosed before the magistrates (Messrs. W. Timmins and J. Ookletton) on Moeday, when James Hampton' of that place, was brought up charged with being drunk and disorderly on the 4th of March, and alsi with the unlawful neglect of his son Walter, aged 8 years, on the 3.Bte of February, 4th of March and other days; tech neglect being dangerous te- the health of the child.—Hamoson, wno said his wife, had died two years last Christmaa,pleaded guilty to both charges.—The little child (who attended the Court) was a pitiable object; his face was covered with scans, his clothing of the raggedest, and withal he was a cripple, having suffered from a stroke about three years ago, due according to the evidence submittal to being exposed on a bank one cold night about that time.—Polic Constables Gregory and Bowyer and Sergeant W. Brewst.r gave evidence which briefly summarised disclosed the following facts : Hampton wags labouring man, his average earnings be- ing about £1 Is. per week with overtime. He had three children, Arthur 18 (working and earning 93. a week), Joseph, 16, and Walter, aged 8 years. On several occa- sions witnesses had neticod the little boy Walter beg- ging about the streets, and had occasionally given the lad a copper themselves. The poor little fellow was ill- clo:hed and had on one occasion been seeking shelter un- der the football stand in Ir well lane. Upon Constable Gregory visiting the defendant's house, Hampton swore at him, and asked " What he wanted there ?" and the constable had so much difficulty with him that he had to lock him up. An examination of the house disclosed that the place was extremely filthy and destitute of furniture. It was a small house in No. 2 Court, Lightfoot's Square. The back window in the upstairs room had not a pane of glass in it, and was stopped up; " there was jest' to use the words of Constable Bowyer, (who went and ex- amined the place with Inspector McAteer. of the N.5.P.C.C.,) " merely an old mattress and it was very dirty, and hardly any elver whatever ; the father said that they all slept in that one bed—the father, Arthur, Joseph, and Walter." Downstairs there was an old table two chairs, and an old sofa frame destitute of any kind of covering. On the last occasion the house was visited there was a little butter, and about a loaf and a half of bread there, but on a previous occasion there was none. —Prisoner elected te be summarily dealt with, but had nothing to say in his defence.—The Chairman address- inc Hampton said it was a disgraceful caee,and he ought to he ashamed of himself treating his children so. The case of drunk and disorderly would be dismissed,but tor the case of neglect he would have to go to prison for 4 months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN CHARACTER SKETCHES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CHARACTER SKETCHES.</p>
        <p>BY Q.E.F.D. No. 3.</p>
        <p>It is safe to assert that the moral reformer is an individual of so much importance, that without his presence Society would eventually be found in a state of stagnation. Were his removal from our midst ever to be effected, the most disastrous conse- quences would follow. What incalculable blessings he has worked and is still working in humanity? In his train folio 77 all that is brigut, and pur, and good. He is like the small piece of leaven, leaven- ing the whole lump. Unnoticed he commences his work of reorganizing Society. His influence being confined to a limited sphere is not at first duly appreciated. Timorously it may be he puts forth the tender leaves of hope, and waits with anxiety the result of his labour. The morrow finds the seed sown in fear,blossoming into a glorious liar- vest,and he thus becomes recognised as a monument of power, and when he falls a victim to the fierce onslaught of time he carries with him, as a benedic- tion, the tears of a grateful multitude. The fact is worthy of consideration that the world has never been without its moral reformers. From the time of Zoroaster and Moses there has been an ever flow- ing stream of persons whose mission seems to have been to rouse nations from a lethargic slumber. Nor has this been confined to certain parts of the world. While there have been personages who stand out of a dark past in bold relief as shining lights, there is every reason to believe that there are to be found in hidden corners of the world men who work out, sometimes, indeed, unconsciously, the salvation of their fellow men. It is in this respect that the person under con- sideration presents a personality so striking that it cannot fail to evoke notice,even though it may fail to secure admiration.</p>
        <p>His spare figure—rather inclined to the stoop which is natural to age—benevolent countenance, ; the somewhat seedy habiliments and quaint top hat, once so fashionable, instinctively carry the mind back to the old fashioned days of yore. He . forms an interesting link between the past and pre- sent, for he lives continually in the days which have long since been swept away into the eternity of the past. Though not a Runcornian born,—having come from a large manufacturing centre in Lanca- shire, he has resided in the town for upwards of forty years, so that it can be claimed that he is, at • all events, a naturalized resident. Needless to say the Runcorn of that period was not the Runcorn of to-day. It could with truth be said that large stretches of undulating fields composed the village- hamlet of Runcorn, together with a comparatively , few modest cottages dotted here and there, instead 1 of the houses and magnificent (?) buildings to which the inhabitants can look with a certain amount of , pride. Nonconformity, too, had not reached , that power which it now enjoys. The Church day schools had been for a long time the only place where the rising generation could be educated. Fortunately Mr. -- had been in con- tact, previous to his arrival in Runcorn, with some sturdy Dissenters. He had also imbibed their spirit. He brought with him, consequently. strong convictions, and materially assisted in the rise of Nonconformity which has, since that period, held the balance of power in the town. At the outset he identified himself with those objects which make Government good, His sympathy for young men had already preceded him, and no one, conse- quently was surprised when he initiated an intel- lectual movement calculated to give a healthy moral tone to the community, and from which scores of persons, young and old, have reaped con- siderable advantage. At the preliminary meeting he was elected president, and he has had the dis- tinguished honour to be at its head for thirty-seven years, a term of service seldom equalled ; and it may fairly be claimed to be a record, when the fact is taken into consideration that he has attended these weekly meetings—which are held regularly six months in the year—and only missed occupying the chair on but two or three occasions. During this period he has presided over those who have since made their mark in the financial, literary, moral and scientific worlds. His influence is a magnetic one: he never fails to attend; and his courtly manner overcomes the most sullen tempera- ment. . _</p>
        <p>It is in religious matters, however, that he is seen to advantage. The Bible, and everything relating to it, has been made the occasion of a profound study. The controversial points upon which he has exercised private judgment,have only been decided after long end mature thought. It is easily explain- able, therefore, that his mind is a mine well-stored with the truths of Divine Revelation. It would not be inapproptiate to describe it as a well mixed book-shelf. Being so familiar with the various books by constant"turning-up"the possessor is never at a loss for an illustration. Yet it must not be thought that he has devoted his time exclusively to the stuny of Religion,ignoring en bloc other enually important subjects. Even those who are intimately acquainted with him are surprised at his wonderful condition. In one respect he was somewhat similar to the late Mr. Gladstone for he is a most omnivor- ous reader. There are few subjects of which he has not obtained an insight even though it be some- what superficial. lt must be noticed, however,that the poets have occupied but little attention. This is a regrettable fact, for he has thus missed some of the purest gems of thoaght, which would have bet- ter enabled him to understand the working of a human soul in quest of truth. Novels, too, have been .summarily dismissed as unworthy of atten- tion. But in deducing such a conclusion the reason- ing is vicious. The aphorism of Pope. "the proper study of mankind is man," is indubitably correct.lt is only in novels, plays, etc.,issuing from the pea of standard writers, that an insight is given of man's knowledge of man. All have not the genius to see the frailties and the sublimity, the humour,and the pathos of life; and fewer still possess the necessary qualification to render intelligible to others the thoughts which sometimes dazzle the mental vision of the most unreflective mind.</p>
        <p>alt would naturally be thought by those ac- quainted with him, that a man of such extensive reading could not fail to be also broad minded, but we regret that the reverse is actually the cast. Toleration, especially on religious Triatters,is an un- known word in his vocabalary. It would, indeed be more correct to say that it is never in evidence in his actions for it is an op3n secret, that he is rather inclined to be somawhat et a bigot. His tem- per, too, is of a peculiarly viol.trit desc,lotion. The unruffled exterior, is not always indicati7e of a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 10, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 10, 1899.</p>
        <p>mental calm. A study of his character reveals the fact that there is an underlying unrest which breaks into a turgid excitement when any pet theory is molested by rude hands. Yet, he has a wonderful fund of genuine sympathy for all in dis- tress; and the quiet grip ot the hand sometimes conveys the thoughts which the heart feels but which words fail to describe. With children he is a general favourite. As a business man he moves about with a quiet matter-of-fact air which is as much as to say: "When you come to see a business man, it is unfor- tunate that he cannot bear to be crossad in any controversy, and is excessively (login ttic in all his opinions, do your loaminess, and when you have completed your business go about your business." Punctuality is one of his notable characteristics. His habits of life are of a most regular nature. It is pleasant to record that he has never been ad- dicted to anything of a vicious tendency. He was one of the taremost champions in the spread of the temperance cause at Runcorn. Smoking also is a practice which he looks upon with extreme dis- favour. Nominally a Radical he betrays his innate conservatism in many ways. Losing sight of the the fact that the church must keep abreast of the times.he advocates,and that most strenuously—that all modern innovations be done away with. A plain service conducted by a plain man in a plain build- ing, appeals to his inner soul in a far more touch- ing manner that an ornate ritual conducted in a sumptuous edifice. It may be added that he has 'held several public positions each of which he has filled with credit. It is as well to record for the benefit of those who are climbing the steep hill of difficulty in an endeavour to reaeh th 3 goal of fame, that he is an entirely self made man. As far as these world's goads are concerned he is not lavishly endowed. Wealth, however, does not constitute true man- hood. There is something infinitely superior to the riches of the world, and that is when the casket of the mind is filled with gems of gratifying experi- ence and sublime sentiments. By iron will he de- termined when but a bay to improve his position. In order to accomplish this self imposed task he worked 'early and late and eventually succeeded in being able to read and write. He also taught himself the elements of English grammar. Perseverance was at last rewarded. The circumstances which threatened to crush him have been but the rocks upon which he has climbed to greater heights. In- stead of the environment making the man, the man has made the environment. He is devoted to the cause of God and Goodness. His Christianity is a spring from which he draws constant consolation in distress, and is to him a stronghold in time of trouble."</p>
        <p>EPIGRAM. A man who from his early youth, Has laboured in the cause of truth. Whose aim to make a better world. And keep the flag of truth unfurled, Has e'er been with him night and day. And o'er his action abed a ray, Which classes even sin away. HALTON PAR tSfl COUNCIL</p>
        <p>THE FUTURE OF Asniooa BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of tho Balton Parish Council was held in the sch )01 ;corns on 1113nd ev Triers were present—Msirs. R Huzhes (in the chair). J Icy, J Astbury, S Barrow, J S Jeacock, G Leithwe3d, J Looker, J M inning, W Moires, A Povy, J Stokes, and A Wo3dier (clerk).</p>
        <p>The Chairman (after the usual lapse of time) : Does I any candidate wichdraw ? None did so, and there was a show of hands, with the following result, Messrs T R Westbrook . and Bradshaw being appointed tellers :—Mr Clarke 42 ; Mr Cowdell,7 ; Mr Davenport, 36 ;Mr Fairhurst, 30 ; Mr Hayes, 31 ; Mr Mason, 36 ; Mr Mclntyre, 48 ; Mr Pearson, 50 ; Mr Pembert)n, 27 ;Mr Welsby, ; and Mr Whitley, 32. The hignest number of votes were, therefore, relorded for Messrs Pears n, Mclntyre, Clarke, Mason, Daven- port, Whitley, and Hayes. The Chairmaz : Subject to a poll being demanded at the meeting I hereby declare that the gentlemen Oast named) have been elected parish councillors. After a few moments' silence Mr. Pemberton, Mr. Mason, and others demanded a poll. Mr. Welsby and Mr Cowdell subsequently intimated their willingness to withdraw to avoid a poll. Mr. Davenport : I think we ought t) save the ex. perm of a poll. Mr. Pembirton : I think it is most unfair for any pressure to b 3 brought on a candidate; I don't think it ought to be allowed: Mr. S. W. Dallow (also warmly) : Shut up. (Laugh- ter.) Mr. Pemberton: I appeal to the chair. Mr. Dillow: Time is up.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : Gaqtlemen offered to withdraw. I don't think any prasuro has been brought to bear upon anyone. Mr Pemberton : It has. The Chairman: Not on the meeting. Mr. England: Too much is said about the cost of a poll. The Chairman : We cannot have any more speeches, It is out of order altogother. Mr. Hayes said he was willing to withdraw. The Chairman : Withdrawals can be made after the meeting.</p>
        <p>The Clerk annolneed that the poll would take place on March 27th, from 12 to 8 p.m. _</p>
        <p>Mr Magnalt : I should- like to know if the demand for a poll has been upheld, The Chairman replied in the affirmative. Mr. Clarke pointed out that a poll would cost £lO, awl that it would be throwing money away. Mr. Pemberton : I don't agree with that. A poll has never cost more than £7, and it means about ti in the £. If you do away with a poll certain cliques get to- gether and elect anyone they like. I would sooner pay the expense myself and have a poll. At the close of the meeting the following candidates applied for and signed withdrawal forms, viz :—Messrs. Thomas Cowdell, fulan Fairhurst, Ashton Hayes, and S F Webby. This left seven, who will form the Parish Council for the next twelve months without the neces- sity now of a poll. The seven who form the Council are Messrs Joseph H Clarke, C W Davenport, G H Mason, William Metntyre, Frederick Pearson, John Pemberton, James Whitley. LA.TCHFORD WITHOUT.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting was held in the Baptist school- room, Wash-lane, on Monday evening. There were present —Cooncillor J Lee,Captain Waring,Mr. G Pilling (clerk), Mr. W Richardson,and Mr. G Hopkins. Captain Waring was elected chairman. The election of councillors for the ensuing year took place with the following re- sult :—Messrs. J. Fairclough,W G Hopk ins,W Pierpoint, Cr Pillin, W Richardson, 11 Roberts, Turner, and Lee. A vote of thanks was passed to Captain Waring for pre- siding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>ASTMOOR BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>ASTMOOR BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Mr, Stokes raised the question of the dangerous state of Astmoor Bridge. He said the inhabitants of Runcorn and Halton were more generaily concerned in the bridge than he was, for it was net a way that he went often, but he had brought the matter forward on behalf of the public. The reason why he broached the subject now was becau,e at the last meeting of the District Council mention was maae of the bridge, and it was stated that since the road was taken over by the County Council it was out of the hands of the District Council, and it was for the Parish Council to deal with. He thought they ought to take up the subject. They had all heard of the narrow escapee that had taken place on the bridge. He was surprised that such a bridge should ever have b /en put up on such a public road leading from Warrington, and as it was now going to be a County Council road it might be even more frequently used than at thelprasent. He moved that their clerk be instructed to for ward a letter to the Cheshire County Counc.l, calling their at- tention to the necessity of improving the approach te Astmoor Bridge, and pointing out the dangerous condi- tion it was in. He was not the only one who had noticed the bad condition of the bridge.— Mr. Leatiarvold said he lived where he could see all the traffic pass over the bridge. Every day horses came to the bridge that would draw what they were put to, but the bridge affected them so that they would not draw a pound. It was a pretty constant thing for his horses to have to render assistance to horses that otherwise would not be able to mount the bridge. He had also seen a man killed there.—Mr. Stokes asked if at the inquest there was not any rider put to the verdict.—Mr. Leath- wood replied there was, and two surveyors examined the bridge, but nothing further was done. The County Council had taken over a great many roads from Sir Richard Brooke, and had a large amount of work to do. He thought when they could sea their way they would turn their attention to the bridge. He did not see why they should attack the County Council.—Mr. Stokes said that he was not attacking the County Colincil. He moved the reselution in the interests of the public.— Mr Moores supported and said it was painful to have witnessed what had happened on the bridge. He had seen many horses injured, and one drop down dead. He had also seen a man killed there. It nearly made hint shudder to see anything go up the bridge. The lette.r,he understocd, was not to dictate to the County Council, but to call their attention to the bridge.—Mr Ackerley pointed out that another source of danger was in the canal horses which passed without any drivers. Ho had seen other horses get entangled in the lines. It was not originally made for a high road, but for the convenience of the Bridgewater Canal Company. When Sir Richard Brooke turned it over ta the County Council, they al- lowed him to use it.A public bridge had never been built there.—Mr Povey said that for 35 years he had seen many accidents occur, one man killed, and several hurt to say nothing of the number of horses injured. He had great pleasure in supporting the resolution.—The Chair- man put the motion which was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>CONITSTS LIKELY IN BRIDGEWATER, MERSEY AND NEWTOWN WARDS.</p>
        <p>Notices with reference to the election of District Councillors for the Urban District of Runcorn, baying been duly issued by Mr. F. A. Lake (the returning officer), the following persons were on Thursday declared validly nominated as candidates, for election to the vacancies caused. HEzena WARD.—*Jarnes Pritchard, the Elms, ship owner (unopposed). HALTON WARD.—*Francis Wiswall, 10, Stanley Villas, , civil engineer, (unopposed). CENTRAL WARD.—*Ellis Gatley, Irwell Lane, trade union secretary, (unopposed). mEnsEy wARD.— (1 seat)—*Jenathan Horssfield. Canal-street, coal merchant, proposed by Arthur Riley, seconded by John William Hogg. Thomas Jones, Leinster Gardens, wholesale spirit merchant, proposed by Edwin Gregory, seconded by John Bebbingtou. NEWTOWN WARD.—(l)—Alfred Rob?,rt Norman,Linden Lodge, Mou.lhlancis-lage, pipe manufacturer, proposed by W Handley, seconded by Richard Marsh. Samuel Walton, 39, Victorit-road, gentleman, proposed tv William Rushtori,seconded by Dinah Simpkin. Murdock McKenzie, 34, Greenwar-road, medical doctor. proposed by Thomas James Kirkham, seconded by John N McLeod. BRIDGEWATER WARD.—(I)--*Williarn Handley, Nor- man-road, Savings Bank actuary, proposed by Henry Do id,seconded by Thomas Boote. William Shaw (jun), Waterloo Buildings, High-street, tobacco merchant,pre- posed by Samuel Crosby, seconded by George Heasley. From the foregoing it will be seen that all the old members* with the exception of Mr J Jones, who re- presented the Newtown Ward ,seek re-election ,—Tuesday next has been fired for the last day for withdrawals, but it is quite likely that there will be contests in the Bridgewater Mersey and Newtown Wards. In fact we understood that addresses have already ben issued by several candidates. Tho elections will take place (if any) out he 25th of March.</p>
        <p>SICK NURSING CLA.SS AT WALTON. The beginning of a sick nursing class in ,this parish is a matter of considerable satisfaction. During previous winters the men have been taking Up .ambulance work, and now the other sex are flaying their turn,and are learning those facts about sick nursing which all women, and especially. all wives and mothers, should become acquainted with. The class, organized by Mrs. Darwall, numbers just 69 members. The lectures given by Aliss ilawkes, under the auspices of the Cheshire County Couuctl, are listened to, week by week, with close attention and . well-sustained intera.st. The result must inevitably be the lessening of many of those dis- comforts from which cottage folks suffer when they are ill. It stands to reason that the acquirement of knowledge uprin these matters must prove a real boon to every parish throughout the c)untry.</p>
        <p>VIILOLR LAALP3.—TI) comply with the new Borough Bye-Laws. Lowest prices.—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge-etreet, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>LOCAL PARISH MEETINGS,</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARISH MEETINGS,</p>
        <p>PARISH MEETINGS IN RUNCORN UNION. Annual parish meetings were held in the several parishes of the Runcorn Union on Monday evening, and below are given the returas of the persons nomina- ted as Parish Councillors for the ensuing twelve months. HALTON (NO CONTEST). The annual parish ineeting was attended by Messrs J Alkerley, J Astbury, S Barrow, J S Jeacock Fryer, J Welker, R HuE,,,hes, G Hulme, G Leathwood, J Locker, J M %snaring. W Moores, A Po vey, J Stoke;, r R Wright, J Hancock, and A Woodier (clerk te the Parish Council.)—Mr Fryer was elected chairman and received 12 nomination papers. No poll being demanded the fol- lowine were duly declared elected Parish Councillora for Flalton :—J Ackerley, J Astbury, S Barrow, J S Jeacock, R Hughes, G Hulmo, G Leathwood, J Looker, J Manning, W Mores, A Povey, J Stokes. The constitution of the Board is unchanged.— Mr A. Woodier submitted the Overseers' accounts for the half-year ending March 25th, 1898. Balance, £2B 113 51 ; received from the poor rates, £283 8s 6,1 ; total, £3ll 193 11d. Expeediture : Contributions paid to the treasurer of the Union, £l3O ; aontribution to the Ruocora District Council general expenses, £ll9 ; special expenses, £l5 ; sundries, £36 163 lid; total, £3OO 16s 11d; balance against the overseers. £ll 3s. Per the half-year ending September mh, 1898 :—Balance, £ll 3s; releived from the poor rates, £298 13s; sundries, 3; total, £309 19s. Expenditure contributions to the Treasurer of the Union, £133; contributions to the Runcorn District Council general expenses, £llO ; special expenses, £l5 ; sundries, £3l. 15s 9I; total, £292 15s 9J; balance against the overseers, £l7 33 3d. Doe acoounts of the St. Tnomas's charity were submitted by the Clerk, from which it was seen that the balance in hand was £4 193 lid ; income, £25 63 2d; total. £3O 63 id. Fifty-six persons load bees paid, one •2s 6d, two at 53, and the remainier at £1 each ; the to. tat payments were £26 12s 6d, leaving a balance in hand of £l3 13s 7d. OTHER PARISHES IN THE RUNCORN UNION. The following is a list of the councillors returned as elected unopposed by the chairman of the Parish meet- ings throughout the Union. The asterisk* denotes a new member. The returning Ai :er for the purposes of the electien Mr. John Astitoa (clerk to the Runcorn R.D.C.), has carried out the arraneenvints for the holding of the meetings in the several parishes in a most thorough and satiifac'ery manner. ACTON GRANGE.—James Mort Barnett, William Bps. wick, Peter Hall, Samuel Wright, *John Walker. (5). ALvANLEY.—SamueI Blythe, William Snelson Britland, William Dugdale, Arthur Greenway, W Henry Lowe, Thomas Wright, *Harry Hutchinsen. (7). ANTROBUS.—James B Bradbury, Henry Booth, John Burgess, Thomas Burgess, Peter Frith, Thomas James M,sley, Mark Plumb, Philip N Williamson. No change. (1)- APPLETON-WITH-HULL.—John Marsh, Edwin Birch, James Birch (jeer.), Huh Derbyshire, Henry Eyes, Robert Rouehsedge, John Whitlow. No change. (7). GREAT BUD WORTH —Peter Monteith Adair, Thomas W Newton, Joseph Porter, Gooree Egerton Warburton, *John Lever, *Gilbert Lowe, *Edmund Cragg, *John Drinkwater. (8). CLIFTON.—Joseph Stoles, William Fletcher, Samuel Rowland, John Wilson, *Coition Latham. (5). DUTTON:—Robert Fowles, Joseph Baxter, Abraham Blain, Peter Bunn, Thomas Johnson, Herbert Potts, A H 'Telhot, Thomas Wardle. No change, (8). FRODSHAM.--C.) E Limiter, John Barrow, Peter Booth, *Same&amp; Davies. Thomas Eerlam, George Gleave, James Illidge. Joseph G Kydd, George Rodgers, J W Bothwell, W E Shore, John Josiah Wallowa, William Youd, J M Wiltiamo, (14). FRODSILM LORDSHIP.—Henry Tiley, Ralph Bate, Thomas Birchall Cart-r, John FI Davies, John Dobson, Thomas Lewis, George Okell, James Percival, Charles Reynolds. Benjamin Alfred Rigby, Alfred 'Thomas *rncenss Donning Timmins. (12). GRAPPENHALL.—Richard Bo frlman, John Bradford, Francis Ellison. Alan Lister-Ka,ve, Witiann Lomos, Wil- liam Long, Thomas Smith, Joseph Warburton, *Thomas Chamberlain. *Stephea Hosk,r Jenkins, (10). HATTON.—William Done, Thomas H Feirhurst, William Fowles, James Flatton, Henry Mamdsley,Arthur Morton, Richard L Warburton, no change (7). HELSBY.—James Taylor, J T Collier, WilFam Edward Davies, George C Taylor, James White, *William Mus- grove, *W Bentley Barlow, *Nilliain Davies, *George Eilams, *William Griffiths (10). EINGSLEY.—John Norcross, William J Frodeham, Samuel H arvev, Abraham Lewis, Samuel Lewis, *Wm. Littlernore, *Doodles Ned. James Oultram. John Oul- trim, Join Raven.croft, W H Warburton (11). Moofte.--John White, Hubert Congrsve, William Dun- bevin, Bessie Duttom, Thomas Fleet, Pater Gleave, John Elighfield, lease Lawton. (8). NORLEY.—*Mbert Chrimes.Arthur Done,john Gerrard, Thomas Lightfoot, Joseph Niekson, John Savage, James Travis. George Watson,*James Lewis, *John Lewis (10)• NORTON.—John Acton, Clement Baguley, John Fryer, John II II u•ton, Thomas Horten,Charles Percival, Henry Reynolds, E lward Slater. No change. (8) PRESTONo'-Tlf-HlLL.—Daniel Basnett, W G Jackson, Samuel Jones, T Henry Jones, James C Priestley, Peter James Webb, H T Whitlow, Joseph Whitlow. No change. (8). SUTTON.—Henry Nugent Linaker, Edwin George Aitken, John E Bate, Alfred Lomas, James Okell,Thomas Turner. *William Lowe, *Joseph Williamson. (8). WALTON INFERIOR.—W H Woedcock,William Allinson, Beswick, *Elenry Dodd, *William R Hardy, Joseph Meunfield, Joseph Ward. *Sohn Weir. (81. WEIITLEY SUPERIOR.—Henry Nield, Alfred Barker,John H Clarke. Tnomas Dutton. William Fairhurst, William Fletcher, Edeeard Hatch, Herbert Samuel Wright. No change. (8.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WESTON. ELEVEN CANDIDATES FOR EIGHT SEATS.</head>
        <p>WESTON. ELEVEN CANDIDATES FOR EIGHT SEATS.</p>
        <p>The Weston Parish Meeting was held on Tuesday night, at the National Scholl% when the following per- sons were nominated for Weston Ward f—John Ball, JE C Banks, *Daniel Basnett, John Basnett, Arthur G Guest, *W Duubavin, W E Orme, David Simerville,*G H Garner, James Tudor, and A Pdaitlani Wood. A poll was demanded, and unless three of the candidates with- draw there will be a contest.—Weaver Ward nominated the retiring councillors. viz. :—Perey R Allen, Louis Rosenberg, Samuel Savage, George Whitby, John Griffitbe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>ACTON GRANGE.</head>
        <p>ACTON GRANGE.</p>
        <p>The parish meeting took place at the house of Mr. Thema,' Basnett, Acton Granze. when the attendance was small. The following were elected parish council- lors :—Messrs J Basnett, William Seswick, Peter Hall, Sam Wright, and John Walker. Mr Thomas Basnett pre- sided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>MOORE</head>
        <p>MOORE</p>
        <p>There was a poor attend-a-Woe-in the Board School at the meeting of the parish. Mr 9. de Prez presided. Only four nominations were hand,A in, consequently Messrs J White, H Congrave, Peter Gleaye, J Efighfield, I Law- ton, William Dunbibia, Thomis Fleet. and Miss B Dutton were re-ele3ted members of the Parish Council for the ensuing year. STOCKTON HEATH.—A. POLL AVERTED.</p>
        <p>The meeting at Stockton Heath was held in St. Thomas's Schools, and was well atenied. Mr. Fred Rose was unanimously voted to the chair. Mr. Bradshaw clerk to the Parish Council, read the notice convening the meeting. It was explained that the nominations must be in writing, and that the parochial eleators might sign seven nomination papers only: The papers were then handed in, arm it was found that the gentlemen nominated were Mesirs. Joseph Houghton Clarke, cashier; Thomas Cwdell, auctioneer and valuer; C'narles William Davenport, builder ; John Fairhurst, provision dealer ; Ashton Fl ent;George Henry. Mason, commercial traveller; William Mclntyre, commercial clerk ; Fred Pearson cashier; John Pember- ton, builder; Samuel Fitzgerald Welsby, solicitor's man- aging clerk, and James Wnitley, general merchant— eleven candidates.</p>
        <p>The Chairman declared the,p all the nomination papers were valid.</p>
        <p>Mr* E. En!'aril asked for an explanation co? the work done by the Parish Council during the past 12 months. He should like to put ques;ions and dila:e upon them. The Chairman : You cannot dilate upon them. Ques- tions only may be put. Mr. W. Aria )1d aakei whettrr it was true that ths Parish Council lead request the District Council to appoint another distr;ct cJuricillor for Stooktou Heath. Mr. Davenport : Certaiaiy not. Mr. Gordon : Will each candidate notafnat td agree to stand by the cifTision of the meeting. (Laughter). The Chairman: The question. is out of order. Mr. Eagland asked whether the candidates would endeavour to alter a c3rt sin arrangement. Thera was a signed agreement between tbe Latchford Without and Stockton Heath Parish Councils in connection with the sewage scheme between the two parishes: An arrange- ment had been entered into by which each house that would be erected in Latchford Without should pay a sum of 3t in the 2. The pravions Parish Council, after going well iato the matter, thought that 61 would be a proper and reasonable charge. Could the candidates see their way to hava the matter re-coniidered ? _</p>
        <p>Mr. Parson did not think the question was in order, unless Mr. England was dismiling a nittter ot policy. No question could be asked bearing upon past or future policy. . _</p>
        <p>M. England rose to speak, when the Chairman told him to" put the question without debating it." Mr. England: I have a perfect right to ask whether the candidates will cantinue the present arrangement. 'rho Chairman: Pat Mae qu'istion to some particular candidate.</p>
        <p>Mi. England: I put it to the whole of them. Mr. Pearson : I 3111 not astionA of the part I have taken in the matter. I be!ieve we have made the best harzain that; could oe made, and a profi:able bargain. (4. Question.")</p>
        <p>• - i Asked if t was intended to continuo the present ar- rangement, Mr. Pearson replied in the affirmative. Mr. Clarke : I shall maintain honourably tho agree- mnit made between Latchford and S:OCilt011 Heath. It W4E4 pisied unanimously, I believe. The Chairman : It does no:, matter whether it was unanimous or not. The question has been answered:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>THEL WALL.</head>
        <p>THEL WALL.</p>
        <p>The parish meeting was held in the National School- room on Monday night. The Rev T Pyrn Williamson as chairman at the Parish Council presided. Thirteen nomination papers were handed in, and uoon a show of hands being taken the following were declared duly elected :—Mr George Astall, Mr William Ford, Mr IN Houghton, Mr Edwin Hewitt, Mr J Kite, Mr N Naylor, Mr J Tack, Mr P Warburton. and Mr T Whitlow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>STRE rToN.</head>
        <p>STRE rToN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Smith presided at the commencement of the Parish Meting, but on being nominated hs with- drew, and Mr G J Baxter presided during the remainder of the evening. The following gentlemsn were nomina- ted for election to the Parise Council. The Hon and Rev C F Cross, Messrs A Smith, A Faulkner, W Win- stanlcy, J Rutter, J Harvey, P Leather, G Blackshaw, C T Whitley, R Billington, J Hewitt, and A Sankey. Messrs J Hewitt,and I Sankey, withdrew their candida- ture. A show of hands resulted as fellows :—A Smith, 25 ; A Faulkner, 24. ; W Wins'Anley, 22; J Rutter, 17 ; J Harvey, 17; P Leather, 14- ; Hon C F Cross, 11 ; G Blackshaw, 10; C T Whitley, 10; R Billington, 8. A demand for a poll not beMg made, the Chairman de- clared the seven receiving the highest number of votes elected members of the Parish Council for the ensuing year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>APPLETON.</head>
        <p>APPLETON.</p>
        <p>The annual parish meeting took place at the Apple- ton Thorn Schools. Mr. J. Knowles presided over a small meting. The following tontlemen were elected Messrs H Darbyshirs, John Marsh. E Britch, H Eyas, John Whitlow, J Britch, jun., and R Rouglasedge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>PENKEI'II.</head>
        <p>PENKEI'II.</p>
        <p>On Monday night lasr, the annual meeting for the purpose of electing members to serve on the Parish Council for the next 12 months was held in the Wesleyan School. A record number of ratepayers attended. and Mr J B Aitken was unanimously elected to the chair. Mr E Forster (clerk) was present. The Chairman on opening the proceedings asked for nonn;nations and a, large number was handed in. Alter the time had ex- pired for receiving nominations the Chairman an- nounced that the following gentlemen had been proposed and seconded for service on the Council :—Messrs A Appletan, E W Clarke, J Dignum, J Eden, R Garnett, B Hobson, W Lengshaw, J Millington, T A Murry, C E Parker, M Pawnall, Joseph Pownall, J C Routledge and J S Snoddy. Mr. Millington rose and said one of those nominations was in his opinion not legal. The Chairman replied that he was satisfied with the nomination, and said 10 minutes were allowed for any candidate to withdraw if he desired. The meeting was now open to ask any question of any of the candidates. Mr. Tnnmas Tunstall said it would be instructive if the Council would furnish a report of the work accom- plished during the last 12 months. The Chairman said that n 9 report could be demanded from the retiring Councillors, but the ratepayers could ask any reasonable question or if any candidate liked to explain anything he could do so. Mr. Millington at once rose and avked how it was the rates had increased to as much as 4s in the £ as against 23 in the £ of former years. He asked Mr. Dignum (Dis- trict Councillor), to explain. , Mr. C. E. Parker said that demands from the Urban ' Council, had to be met, and the overseers levied accordingly. Mr. R. Garnett, J P., Chairman of the retiring Cenci- cil, said he would like to give a brief sketch of the w ark of the Council for the past year. The cottages at the Looks had been without a proper water supply, but it had Iseea remedied. Classes for Technical Instruction had been formed for cooking, dressmaking, and short- hand, and were well attended. They had endeavoured to get a footpath made from Greystone House across to Fiddler's Ferry Station, which would mean a great sav- ing in time for persons travelling by that line. Foot- paths that needed repair had been looked after, and where possible made right. The provision of a mortuary was a serious question, and they had no desire to in- crease rates by Providing a new one, but arrangements were being made with the Park Company, to use a room at the Day Star Institute for the present. The muzzling of doga had been under their notice, but the Council had no power in this direction. The assessment of the Recreation Ground had been very heavy but they had been granted a reduction of 75 per cent. A reduction in the pric of gas to the extent of 8d per 1000 feet had also been accomplished and they hoped to get the price for the gas lamps further reduced. A deputati con- sisting of Messrs. R B Matthews, J Dianne.' and William Owen had waited on the Corperation'Water Committee, and through their exertions the 8 par cent charge on the outlay was to cease, as they were now taking so much water as to make this possible. (Applause.) Mr. Garnett in conclusion spoke of the loss they bad sustained by the death of their late colleaee Mr. R B Matthews, whose valuable knowledge and advice had always been readily given. He also sympathised with Mr. J C Routledge in his indisposition ani_wished him a speedy rezovery.</p>
        <p>Mr. Millington said hs would like to know bow it was there wai no pr;vision for gas in Heath-road. This road was now well populated, but they were entirely in the dark. As to new footpaths he said there were more than enough for the Council to look after already. He would venture to say that some of the councillors had never seen half of them. They should be made to look over them in wet weativr and they would be attended to. Mr. Pickton hoped that the new Council would make a point of seeing that the Sanitary authorities atteccled to their duties, especially in regard to the clearing of the ashnits, wWch needed attention. The Chairman said he was much gratified at the in- terest shown in the Parish affairs, and he would now submit the names for a show of hands, he himself act- ing as counter. The result was then read out as follows Dr T A Murray 69 votes, R Garnett 63, C E Parker 57, J C Rutledge 50, J Dignum 49, J S Snoldy 47, W Long- sha.w 44, .1 Millington 35, E W Clarke 31, J E.len 27. M Pownall 25, A Applet an 12. James Pownall 11, and B Hobson 7. The Chairman then announced that subject to a poll being demanded, the fires seven gentlemen would constitute the Coumil for the ensuing year. Mr. James Millington at once demanded a poll, and he was supported by Messrs. M. Payrnall, W. PiAton, Peter Boardman and John Eden.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. E. Withenshaw suggested that the gentlemen desirous of a poll should pay the expensAi tor the same.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parker said he would willingly withdraw to save the expenses of a poll if ths gentlemen would be williag to withdraw their demands.</p>
        <p>Messrs. T. Wilkinson and T. Tunstall also spoke against a poll in the face of that niOst's voting. The Clerk said the last poll cost £ll 10r. 61. Messrs. Pickton and Millington here rose to srak to - gather, but the Chairman requested them to sit down. Mr. Pickton said it mined to him that it was rather a case of he House of Lords and the House of Com- mons. The Lords could &amp;wak as they wi2iied, but the</p>
        <p>Commons could not. He did not think it good enough. Let every ratepayer have a fair hearing. The Chairman announced that she demand of the fi7e gentlemen would be granted and a poll would be held, and he would advise the returning officer accordingly. A vote of thanks to the Chairman for lais able service concluded the meeting. The poll will be held on Monday, the 27th inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>The annual Parish meeting for Great Sankey was held on Mond ty evening in the Be ird Schools, Great Sankey. Mr. J Wright presidel, and there were also present : Messrs T Halton, Joseph Kilshaw, G Clarke, T Cooke, J Robinson,Thomas Perks, W Grange,W raylor, G Tnomp- son, T Kilshaw, Unsworth, Dugdale, with Mr. J. Smith (clerk.) The Chairman submitted a letter he had received from Mr. James Summersgill, stating that he was very sorry that owing to ill-health he was unable to be pre- sent. He deprecated a poll as worse than unnecessary, a waste of time and money. Nomination papers were then halide in, and Mr. Wright finding that he was nominated, said the meeting must appoint anther chairman. Mr. George Thompson proposed, and Mr. Thomas Halton seconded tne proposition that Mr. George Clarke be appointed chairman. This was unanimously agreed to.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he had received a letter from Mr. G. Pownall stating that he was called away to a Parish meeting at Penketh and would be unable to be present. He wished to vote for the following members on the Great Sankey Parish Council :—Messrs Joseph Wright, Thomas Cooke, Thomas Kilshaw,G H Kilshaw, W Taylor and T Halton.—The Chairman said he could not allow anyone to vote by proxy as the vote must be taken by show of hands. It might seem hard upon Mr Pownall, but he could not take his vote. The Chairma then pro- ceeded to read the names of those who had been nomina- ted, which were as follow 3 :—Cook Thamae,Holly House Farm, Great Sankey,proposed by Joseph Henry Kilshaw seconded by Thomas liaison ; Cooper Charles, Hood-lane Great Saokey, proposed by James Ray, seconded by Matthew Tonge ; Halton Thomas, Dine Cottage, Chapel, lane, proposed by Joseph Wright, seconded by Themes Cook ; Kilehaw Joseph Henry, Ivy house Farm. Cow- lane, proposed by William Ta.yl3r, seconded by Thomas Cook ; Kilstraw Thoneas,Whittle Hall, Great Sankey,ps °- posed by William G:ange, seconded by John Robinson ; Singleton Henry, Hill Crest, butcher, proposed by George Thompson. seconded by Matthew Toage; Taylor William. Brow Farm, proposed by Jelen Robinson seconded by Thomas Perks ; Thompsoa George, Derby House, proposed by John Clara Ridgvvay, seconded by William Henry Leatherland Gorse; Wright Joseph, Chapel Brow, proposed by Thomas Halton, seconded ty William Taylor. Voting was then t siren by a show of hands, and resul- ted as follows :—Joseph Wright, 19; William Taylor, 16: Thomas Ualton, 14; Joseph Henry Kilshaw, 13; Thomas Kilshaw, 13 ; Thomas Cook, 12; George 'Thomp- son 8; Henry Singleton, 3; Charles Cooper, 2. The usual time having elapsed without a poll being dernanded.the first six gentlemen were elected members of the Parish Ceuncil. The alteration frem that of last year is the substitution of Mr. Wright for Mr George Thompson. The Chairman said it was now open f et. any elector to ask any question re!trring to parochial metters gen- erally. They had now an opportunity of ventilating any grievances. He would like to mention a matter which would no doubt be of interest. The Board schools during the past year had neo cost the ratepayers of the parish a halfpenny. (Hear, hear.) Not only had the schools paid their way but they had also paid back to the rates £29 63. 61. (Hear, hear.) Such a result had not been obtained before, and it had been accomplished with lessening numbers owing to tbe opening of a neigh- bouring school. They had been able to pay all expensee as regarded school work and save the rates £3O in addition. The only call upon the rAte3 for School Beard purposes was the payment of the Clerk's salary which would not run more than a penny in the L. No della some of the ratepayers would no wondering why they were called upon to pay a smaller rate but the reason was the one he had referred to. He did not wish t 3 Cairn any special credit for it. They had gone on with their work as usual, but had achieved better results. Mr. T. Halton said he was the district councillor and the representative for the township on the Highway Board which was shortly to be merged in the Rural District Council, and if there were any complaints he would be glad to hear of them at any time. He pro- posed a vote of thanks to the Chairman. Mr. Georee Thompson congratulated the Chairman lupon the happy result of the year's work at the schools. (Hear, hear.) He thought they might also congratulate themselves upon that result because it would be a little if not much in their pockets. There was a question he would like to refer to. He did not see the reason of 'having a parish meeting every twelve months. He thouebt, and there were e great many of the Same opinion, it would be better if the elections took place every three years. (Hear, hear.) They had the elec- tions for Boards of Guardians, district councils and School Boards every three years which were more im- portant offices, and he did not think the Parish ought to be put to the expense of an election every year for Parish Councillors. He estimated that the Parish Coun- cil had cost Great Sankey betweea £4O and £5O. What use had it been? He thought if they passed a resolu- tion and sent it to the Local Government Board and the County Council they would do themselves a good turn. Ho would therefore propose that the Clerk be instructed to write to the Lecal Government Board and County Council urging the advisability of triennial elections for Parish Councillors instead of annually, and that they be held concurrently with the other elections.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wright seconded, and thought that three years was quite short enough. The Clerk stated that a private bill had been intro- duced into Parliament with tbe object of bolding Parish Council elections every three years. Mr. Thompson then secondel the vote of thanks to the Chairman, and said he wished t) thank thoo gen:Jo- lson who had supported him. He had done his best while he had been a member of the Parish Council, and it was perhaps fortunati for him that they had released him from the office. He had plenty ot work to do, and when he was put out of one office he generally got put into (Aber work.</p>
        <p>The vote of thanks was unanimously carried, and the Chairman having responded, the meeting terminated.</p>
        <p>BURTON WOOD.</p>
        <p>Tha annual meeting was held in the Board School, Mr. William Faircloug,h, chairman of the Paristi Council, presiding. There was a good attmdance. The candi- dates elected are as follows :—Mr. William Fairclough, Brook Head ;Mr Richard Foram w,Coliins Green BzewerY; Mr John Fairclough, Yew Tree; Mr Thomas Forster,lvy- lane Farm; Mr Herbert Gawthorp*, Mercer-street; Mr William &amp;maim, Rose Villa, Collins Green ; Mr John Warburton, Dial Post Farm. The next business for con- sideration was the lighting qlestion9 and atter a long discussion, it was further adjourned for six months. It was resolved that the Paris n Council ask the County Council to divide Burtonwood into wards for the pur- poso of being better repre3ented on the different Boards. WINWICK-WI 111-1115LME.</p>
        <p>The annual Parish Meeting was held in the school- room, Winwick, on Monday evening. The folllwir.g gentlemen were elected members of the new Parish Council :—Mr T C Shaw, farmer, Winwick; Mr P Long- too, farmer, Winwick ; Mr W G Wallace, steward, Win- wick ;Mr 1' Warburton, farmer, Halm, ; Mr Joha Cald- well, farmer, Orford, Mr T Corbett, tanner, Otford. The Chairman also intimated that he thought the Govern- ment would eventually elect Parish Councils for three years. A quarterly meeting of toe Pariah Council wa s afterwards held.</p>
        <p>WA!? RINGTON, INDEPENDENT METHODIST BIeICK-STREET MISSION.</p>
        <p>With a view of assisting the senior married men's effort towards the forthcoming sale of work, a tea meeting was held in the Mission Hall 9n Tuesday evening. About 120 sat down to tea and after ample Justice had been done to the good things pro- vided,the capabilities of the caterers to amuse were fully justified by a short entertainment by the Klondyke Minstrels,whose style and make up caused much laughter and mirth. The minstrels acquitted themselves creditably in the following solos" Ring the Bell. Watchman " and " Only to see her face again," James Shaw; " Poor Old Jeff" and" The Minstrel Boy," Edward Roberts; " The Hungry Army," and "I can't forget my Martha, dear." Ben- jamin Warburton; and" Where are you going my pretty maid," William Hibbert. The remainder of the evening was given up to all kinds of games,and a thoroughly enjoyable time was spent. OtH NA TEA SERVICES, Dinner Services, Toilet and Trinket Sets. Cheapest honse.—J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, Bridge-street, Warrington. AC&amp;DEMY-STRNNT HALL.</p>
        <p>On .Sunday Mr. Percy Beswick, of Manchester occupied the platform, aud after a few remarks by the Chairman, gave a large number of clairvoyant delineations, On Monday evenuag, nearly the whole time was devoted by Mr. Bowick to clair- voyance and psychometry, and in both branches of phenomena he was very tairly successful in demon- strating the fact of spirit return.</p>
        <p>"LINSEED COMPOUND for Con hs and Colds, allays irritation. 'fives iinMediate relief. —Advt. ASK or CUNNINGHAM HIGH-Ct ASS MINERAL WA.TERS.—Adot LAMPS, Oil stoves, Coke Stoves, Brass Fenders. Largest selection.—J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, Bridge. Street. Warrington.</p>
