      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>SIR JOHN BRUNNER mui TILE QUESTION OF ITBERCULOSIS.</head>
        <p>SIR JOHN BRUNNER mui TILE QUESTION OF ITBERCULOSIS.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Nertawich Rural Council, on Friday, Mr. Millington presiiing, th 3 Clerk read the following letter which he had reeeivel from Mr. J. C. Hammond, Sir John Brunner's private secretary : "9, Ennismore Gardens, S. Vv ~ February 2713, 1899. Tuber- culosis milk. Sir John Brunaer desires me to acknow. ledge the receint of your letter and to say that as yotz will see from the enclosed copy of a let:er which be addressed t 3 Lord Crewe on the subject last November, he agrees with your Council in thinking that the matter should be dealt with by a general act rather than by thy grant of powers to individual municipal corporations.' The letter which Sir John addressed to Lord Crews was as follows :—" Mr. Ar'llur P. Smith, secretary of, the Chester Farmers' Club wrote to me on the 15th November from Grosmor Chambers, Ciresnr, stating that at a general meeting of the club held on the 12tts February a resolution wa3 unanimously passed condena- niug some recent acti in by the Manchoiter City Council in reference to the compuleery seams., without full com. peesstion of cattle which were affected with tuber. culosis, and urging the ileal tnenaberg of Parliainerr, to use their influence te prevent the passing of a clause to this effect, presumably in " a Private Act applying to Manchester. I wrote to Mr. Smith to ask for the detailS of the action taken by the MalchPster City Council, and to day I have a letter from Mr. Pownall Goodall, of Beachwood Avenue, Caorlton.cum-Hs,rdy, Manchester. stating that it.a.A.P.Smith,of the Chester Farmers' 3lub, had requeoted him to send in a copy of his article, en- titled' Oar milk supply' which had appeared in :ha Press the previous week. Mr. Pownall Goodall says that it is impossible for him to do more than give an outliue of the Corporation's nroposals, and that he hats referred to these in his article. He adds that if his views meet with my approval he would like me to writs to you upon the subject in your capacity of chairman of the Cneshire Milk Producers' Association. Mr. Pow. nall Goodall's views, as expressud in his article, appear to me to be very sound, but as you will doubtless have an opporAinity of reading the article, I do not here ga into them at length. He says that the Manchester Cot' potation are only following the lal of Glasgow, and he mentions in his letter to me, that Salford and Stockport, have their milk clauses. Frankly I may say that it ap- pears to me "entirely impassible in the present state of public opinion to resist the demands of municipal corporatians for powers to inspect and condemn tuberculosis milk similar to thous existing under statute law with regard to the inspec- tion and condemnation of unwholesome meat, looking; to the now well-known fact that milk from a tubereu- bus udder undoubtedly produces tuberculosis in haman beings and more particularly in infants. Having failed to get the details of the Manchester City Council's pro- posals I cannot of course say whether or not I should, condemn them from my place in the House of Commons but with regard to the question of compensation for mulch cows compulsorily slaughtered on the ground of their being infected with tuberculosis, I agree that compensation should be paid, liberal in amount, but limited as to time, say for two or perhaps three years after tbe passing of the Act. It would undoubtedly be a far sounder policy to pass a general act dealing with the subject of tuberculous milk than to grant powers to municipal corporations one b:- one. It is obvious to anyone who knows anything that cow-houssa in a very large number of cases would require improvement in the matter of lighting and ventilatioa to enable farmers to keep their stock in health. Without such improve. merit it would be impossible for them to do so."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>LOCAL GOVERN 51EN't BOARD INQUIRY</head>
        <p>LOCAL GOVERN 51EN't BOARD INQUIRY</p>
        <p>AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>Last Frilly, at the Town Hall, Widnes, Colonel A. J. Hepper, D. 5.0., RE, held an inquiry into the subject.. matter of an application of she Widnes Town Council for sanction to borrow £41.200 for gas work,' purpaves. There were present the Mayer (Alderman J. W. Ware. ing), H.S.openheitn (town clerk),S.Hoyle(accounrant), and I. Carr. A.M.1.C.8. (gas and water engineer). Mr. H. S. Oppenheim said on the previeu3 0,C4611013 when the Local Government Board sinctioned the bor- rowing (if £ll,lOO, the C4rporation subn-.itted estimates in the usual manner. The amount overspent, however, amounted to £1,937, but he thought that is had been wisely and well spent. He thought it would b 3 in,enst- lug to mention the prite of gas that they were able to supply. To consumers below three million cubic feet, the price was is. 83. less 21. discount, and to consumer above three million cubic feet, the price was is. 61. less 23. discount, so that to large consumers thew were able to supply gas at is. 43. per thousand cubic feet. The Inspector remarked that it was very low. The Tows Clerk, continuing, said in 1897-8, the quan- tity of gas sold was 185,499.500 cubic fee.t.They were also able to supply gas ta pzivate householders in small quantities by means of the automatic principle. A very large quantity of gas was consumed in that manner, the profits in 1897-8, after settinfi aside a sum of about £1,500 for public lighting, was £2,073; and although the acoounts were not made up for the present year, they had every reason to hope that a similar r2sult would he attained.</p>
        <p>Mr. Carr then explain'd to the Inspector the plans f3r the extension and improvements to the Gas Works, after which an adjournment was made to the works, where the Inspector examined the various bultd.ngs.</p>
        <p>"CHRIST AND SOCIAL FROGRESS: THE LINES OF ADVANCE."</p>
        <p>At St. James's Church on Sunday afternoon the Vicar, the Rev. F. E Powell, took the above for his subject. He reminded hrs hearers that there was a social or&amp;r, a divine goal,to wards which mankind was slowly tending. The development of land-ownership was traced from the patriarchal family, which was the first unit that owned the lane, through slave-ownership and feudalism down to molern landlordism. The first thing which the de-. mocracy would write upon the slate, " said Judge Hughes—author of " Tom Brown's Schooldays "—at the Church Congress, in 1888, " will be the nationalisation of the land." In all stages the progress was not so much in a direct line as in a sort of spiral: ascent, someimee descending he'ow the level of the preceding curve, only to rise to som-thing higher and better. The last phase of development the preacher noticed was that of inaustrial organization. The fierce comp.titiott engendered was being largely in.i)dified, and would per- chance be ahortlysuperceded by some form of S All recent legislation was of a S )cialistic character, e.g. the Eiucation and Factory Acts, the Workmen's Com- pensation Act, the proposals conc*rniag the eight hours, old age pensions, the living wage, &amp;c., whileco-operative schemes, profit-sharing, labour co-partnership, concilia- tion and arbitration boards, trade unions, and such like were all ineications of the Socialist trend. In thl more or less immediate fidare all industries which were virtual monopolies, such as land, railways, tramways, gas, water, Sza., would be controlled by the State. But let them beware of falling into the secular and materialistic snare. Progress which was not moral and spiritual de-. bated its own end, and was really not progress, hut re- trogression. There was Nothing which Christ did not touch to-day, and to help forward the attainment of the social Order, to give an impetus to the Social Progress, let them devote themselves whole-heartedly to the ser- vice of Christ.</p>
      </div>
