      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>A WARRINGTON STALL-HOLDER FINED AT NEWTON. At the Newton Petty Qessions, on Thursday, before Mr. T. J. Gillespie (chairman), and other magistrates, Albert Brookfield, of Warrington, was charged with exercising the business of an auctioneer at Earlestown, on the 1311 January without having a license.—klr. Croney said defendant was formerly a licensed auctioneer, and was for some time in the habit of a. tend- ing Earlestown market. Defendant admitted the offence and said he was sorry he had done wrong. According to the Act of Parliament, gains; by auction was selling by competition. He did not wish to nr6s the case only they wanted it stopped, as it was unjust t.) others whe Melt by auction.—Defendant said it had only .occorrel once. On the day in question he had a crate of cracked china, and in the excitement of the moment to keep the crowd togethor,he picked tap a toilet set and said it was worth 2d. They started biiding for it and he sold it for 81.—The Chairm In said he was liable to a penalty of £lOO, and he Was fined 103. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (chairman), Alderman Roberts Councillor Smehuret. Mr. Hepherd, and Dr. Gornall. BITING AN OFFICER'S THUMB.—Thomas Ellison, 2. Warburton's-yard, was cnaeged with being drunk and disorderly in Buttermarket-street, the previous afternoon, and with assaulting P.C. Marsh in the execution of his duty.—Defendant pleaded not guilty to the assault.— Evidence was given that prisoner was ejected from the Cross Keys Hotel, and after he went to the Brittania. He became voilente- whilst being taken to the Police Station, and P.C. • Marsh itame to the officer's assistance going in the Itridewell. and prisoner bit a piece out of P.C. Marsh's thumb.—P.C. Marsh corroborated and defendant was fiaed 20s and costs or 14 days. WANTED ILL OTER TED: COUNTRY.—DanieI Davies was broaght up on remand for the alleged sacrilege at St. Mary's Catholic Church.—Superintendent Monaghan applied for a further remand. as the prisoner was wanted all over the COuntry.—Prisoner objected. He thought they could settle the case then. He knew nothing about what was said.—The magistrates granted a remand for a week. THRASHING A LAD. —Robert McEvoy, Mill-street,was charged with assaultine Richard Crompton on the 26th February.— Defendant pleaded guilty under great provocation.—Hr. A. Brown who appeared for the prosecution said complainant with five other la is was going through the park.Wleen passing the defendant he shouted to One of the boys "what do you call that." meaning a pot hat and Eaton jacket. One of the boys called him aames in return. Defendent then got hold of complainant and struck him several times across the lees making distinct marks 7 or 8 inches long and broke the cane. —Complainant then gave evidence in corroboration of what his solicitor had said —Other evidence was given.—Defendant said the lads called him names. One could not walk in the Park on Sunday without being annoyed. —The Magistrates said he had no doubt had provocation hut he should not have done what he did. He was fined 108 and costs or 14 days. MoNos.v.—Before Mr. F. Monks (in the chair), Alderman Suttcn, Mr. Birchall, Mr. S. Wright, Councillor Shaw Greco and Smethurst. Two MONTHS FOR REFLECTION. —Annie Smith, of no settled abode, made her 25th appearance in answer to charges of haying been drunk and disorderly in Dial-street, at 7.15 on Saturday night, with lawfully breaking 15 panes of glass value 155., the property of Thomas savage, and with assaulting Savage at the Police Station late in the even- Ing.—P.C. Leonard said he took defendant to the Police Station for being drunk and disorderly.—Thomas Savee,re stated that defendant came to his house on Saturday evening and commenced using bad language in the house. When he put her out she smashed 14 panes of glass in his window with a bucket. She struck him on the head with something sharp at the police station.—Defendant was sent to gaol for two months. WREAKED HO VENGEANCE ON THE WINDOP7B.—WMIAm Lythgoe, of no settled abode, was summoned for having been drunk and disorderly in Battersbv-lane on Saturday night, and with damagine hie father's windows to the itmount of Bs.—P.S. Smith said defendant was in the habit of annoying his father every three months by breaking his windows and the family were in terror of their lives.—John Lythgoe (defendant's father) who resides in Sharp-street. and who seemed loth to give evidence, said his son came home occa- sionally for money and clothes. Defendant often demanded money off them and if they refused he ill-used them. On Saturday he asked them far money and said if they did not give it him " out would go the windows."—Defendant who who made his 25th appearance was sent to gaol for one month. ANoTHERINJUSTICF. To THE jEWS.—Jane Kelly, 55, Foun- dry street was summoned for having been drunk and dis- orderly in Ray Horse-yard on Saturday night.—Derendant said if she was not drunk they might think she was, it was only a way she had.—The Chairman: Where did yoa get the black eye? Defendant; I knocked against a door. I had been washing for some Jews and they would not pay me. The Chairman: We shall fine you ss.—Defendant; No money. —The Chairman: Or seveta days. A DELUSION THAT COTT 5 ..—George Lightfoot, farm labourer, of Antrobus, was fined 5s for allowing .his horse and trap to stand in the thoroughfare longer than the time allowed by law.—Defendant stated that he tho ight he could leave his horse in the street as lone as he had business to do there.— The Chairman told.defendant that he wise labouring under a delusion. "G,NGER "GETS A. MONTlL—William, otherwise" Ginger," and Ada Kenny and Annie Waite, of no fixed address, were summoned far sleeping in an empty house in Ship-yerd,with out visible means of subsistence.—P.O. Reddington said that early on Monday morning he found defendant asleep in an untenanted house. They told him they had paid their lodging money to Mrs. Doeherty, who kept a lodging-house near.—Docherty stated that defendants did not give her lodging money. but Waite gave her 3d for some coal to make a fire.—Waite was dismissed on promising to leave the town. Aaa Benny was sent to prison for 14 days and William Kenny for a month. TUESDAY.—Before Mr. Garnett (in the chair) and Mr Birchen.</p>
        <p>THErr or CLITIIING.—Jane Ormston, 13 Dial-court. was charged svith stealing from dwelling-house No. 60 Scott- street, on November 23rd a pair of shoes, jacket, overcoat. and trousers, value 203.—She pleaded guilty. —Superinten- dent Monaghan said th,t prisoner was engaged by Jane Johnson, (prosecutrix) to clean the house. When she re- moved to a house in Orford-lane she discharged prisoner. She sut.sermently found that the articles had gone, and she Informed the police, who arrested prisoner on Monday and found that she had pawned the articles on November 24th for 3a., in the name of Lee.—Prisoner who had previously bad one month's imprisonment for stealing, was sent to gaol for six weeks with hard labour.</p>
        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS. THURSDAY.—Before Mr. T. J._ Gillespie (chairman), Mr. C.</p>
        <p>B. F. Borron, and Mr. Bnberi. Stone. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—The following were fined for this offence Bnoch Monsdale, Thomas Cook. James Bell, Patrick Havey, Robert Hasleton, Thomas Wood, and Martin Hughes.--Jobn Hindley was fined for being drunk at Newton on February 24th. MiscrmLesrous OFFENCE3.—Thomas Walsh-was fined lOs</p>
        <p>Never be without a bottle of° ISynairigton'sEdinburghCoffee Psserice in the house. All Grocers sell it. It makes de- in a mornent. g?, L j - -: ,2 - -,._,Sitifiliitiwitetali644oiiii44</p>
        <p>and costs for being drunk in charge at Winwick on February 28th.—The following were fined for playing "pitch and toss" in Regent-street, Rarlestown :—Martin Larkin and Daniel Whittle, Is, and costs each. and Joseph White and John Dowd 23 61 and costs each.—Caroline Wil- liamson, Michael Knight, John Price, and Robert Robinson were all fined ss, for not sending their children regularly to school. Mr. Martindele proved the cases.— Laurence Dunn, and Phillip Duffey were bound over for a breach of the peace on the 25th February at Golborne. William • Cortield and Patrick Flynn for a breach of the peace) by fighting on February 18th were bound over in their own re- cognisances. John Turner, James Norbury, John Valentine, James Ryde, James Higgins, and John Norbury were also bound over far committing a breach of the pease at Haydock en February 17. Thomas Sherwood. Willi m Bailey, John Cross, NaphO Deiwden, Alfred Help, and William Aaron for a breach of the peace at Haydock on February 18 were also bound over.—Benjamin Kenyon. secretary of the Newton Cricket and Bowling Club. was fined is and costs for allowing an ass to stray on the highway on Febrae.ry 24th. WIFE DESERTIoN.—John Leyland, who did not apoeo.r, was charged with deserting his wife Sarah Leyland on the 20th February.—Complainant said her ha baud refused to live with her. He had been to prison for neglect of family. —The Bench made an order for 7e 6d a week and the wife to , have the custody of the four children. ALTERATIONS TO SANKEY CHAPEL HOUSE.—Mr. H. J. ' Longton said he was instructed by Messrs. Greenall Whitley and Co., St. Helens, to applf for permission to make certain alterations to the Sankey Chapel House Inn. Liverpool-road, Great Sankey*. The alterations were principally sanitary and for the convenience of the tenant (Mr. William Uasworth) and his family —The application was granted. CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—William Cawley and Joseph Caw- ley were charged, the former with working a horse in an unfit state, and the latter with causing same to b 3 work 3d on the 24th February.—Defendants pleaded guilty.—lnspec- tor Slattery said he saw defendant William Cawley driving the horse attached to a cart in Crow-lane on the date in question. He called his attention to the horse, and defen- dant said it had been lame about a month. Witness subsequently saw Joseph Cawley, who admitted sending the boy out with the hotse. He knew it was lame but did not think it was very bad. Witness examined the horse and found it lame on the off-fore leg, and the lameness was accelerated by inflamed side !scones. It was totally unfit for work.—P.O. Brindle corroborated, and defendants were fined 53 and costs, and 10s and costs respectively. THE BENEFIT OF THE CoUBF.—JOhn Hardy was charged with cruelly ill-treating a horse on the 17th February. and Margaret France was charged with causing it to be worked whilst in an unfit state.—lnspector Slattery R.S.P.C.A. said he examined the horse on the 24th February. It was very lame on the off fore leg due t) a contracted foot. It had also Inflamed side bones and the natural construction of the foot was altered, and it was clattering excruciating pain, and was simply beyond human skill.—Thcmas Pope. blacksmith, Rarlestown, said he saw the horse on the 20th Febretry. It was in good condition. It only had contracted tendons and he could have put it right for 3; 91 himself —The Bench gave defendants the benefit of the doubt and dismissed the RA3O ASSAULTING. A WITNES3.—Marth. Hughes was charged with assaulting Christopher Collins on the 4th Harch.—Defendant did not appear.—Mr. A. B. White who prosecuted, said the ease arose out of a case tried at the Court a month ago. Pro• secutor was in the tap room of the New Inn at about a quarter to 10 in the evening when defendant came in and walked up to Collins and said **Yoe are a nice b—swearing false against Towey 1" Prosecutor said he did not ewe tr blase and defendant pulled him off his seat. A few minutes after defendant went up to him again charge i him with swearing false. zat him by the coat and pulled him to the floor. The landlord then came in and ordered defendant out. Proeecutor left the house shortly after and defendant who must have been waiting for him outside struck him several times in the face and kicked him about the shins.—P.o 's Johnson and Shaw came up and defendant ran away but was apprehended —P.C.'s Johnson and Shaw gave evidence and defendant was fined lOs and costs.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. BlorTnilr.—Before .Messrs. W. Timmins (presiding) G. L</p>
        <p>Davies, and John Ockleston.</p>
        <p>TIUNSFEH LisT.—The following transfers wet(' granted : Ship Inn. Heath-road from Thomas Reynolds, to John Ley- land ; Prospect Inn, Weston. from John Vose (deceased) to Rllen Vase; Royal Hotel. from S J Wilkinson to Ntery B Inc]; Q.a.rry Hotel. Weston, from Oliver Donbayin to John Nelson; Fox Tavern. Wel,lk-street. from Robert Dolan to Thomas ilerrison; Red Lion. Halton, from John Leach to Frederick Taylor ; "Man at the Wheel" Church-street, from Alfred Sllison to Robert Boardman. TErg " DANGERS "or VACCHIATION.—Peter Taylor Lawton. 31, Brackley-street. Rumeorn, applied for a certifiette of exemption from vaecination for his child (a boy) born on the 15th December last. He had a conscientious objection for he considered vaccination was injurious in its method and detremental to the heath and life of the child.—ln answer to the Chairman h. said two of his other ohildren were vaccin- ated and he believed that the last one had surfersd throogh it. The Aet was not then in force, or ha wou'd hove aoolied for an ex.mption.—The application was granted. NEcontioarae AT V.C.IANCT. 1-ierlry Beech. Stan'ey-street, was summoned by Thomas isTewis. a neigh- bour, for assauring him on the 25th al Feirmary.—Ornas- summonses were taken out against Newib.—Thomaa Newis 9, Stanley-street, stated that about 6.33 the defendant came to his house and knocked at the door rather unusually. Ire went and opened it and asked Beech what he recoire I. "Is your missa.-s " enquired Beech. and on being told she was, said " Bring the B— out." Without further ado the man struck him in his face as he stood in the doorway. He then pulled him out into thestreet somehow,as the result of a push witness gave him upon his attempting to get into the house Beech then got his arm around witness and struck hi n several times in the face. Complainant could not say what the row originated in, or why the defendant wanted to see his wife. Previous to that he had never had a wrong word with the defendant—Cross examined. Newis said he did not strike Beech at all.—Frederick Donnisnn.brother-tn-law, and Bmilv Newts. wife of the complainant, corroborate I. The latter added that, the bother arose out of a row with herself and Mrs. Beech in the morning.—P.o Edwards said he saw Beech knock Newis down. He was only ten yards off at the time.—William Henry Beech, for the defence, sail he went to the Newts' house and asked Newis "Where's your Misses e" Newts made no reply but fl-mg at him. and hit him '(,tdeech) in the eve. Subsequently when he got his arms free witness used his fists at Newiss, but only in self defence. Thm when he was going up the street back to his own house. 'Newts followed hint and he went hack into the street to get ready for him again, and it was then the Police Officer came on the scale. The offiesr was going to Newts but he said " 1 was the other man. he has got a knife in his hand," The officer then followed him into his house and asked to see his hands, which he showed to him.—Mary Beech.wife of the previous witness. corrMora- ted.—The Bench dismissed the case of aseault against Newts. Beech was, however fined 10s and costs 14s 6d, or 14 days. The Chairman remarked that it was evident he had gone to Newis' house fully determinei on a row. ASL%•,ET WHILST Is CHARGE.—Themas Gees, Pleaded guilty to being asleep whilst in charge of a horse and cart. on the 22nd of February. —Police-sergeant Bourne proved the case and a fine of 5s and 9s 61 costs was imposed and nail.— The Chairman said (}rice was old enough to know better. "Mos AT LARGE."—Considerable ignorance appears to pre- vail respecting the clauses of the County Bare-laws with re- gard to the requirements of a collar haying upon it a duly in- scribed (or rather engraved) name and address securely fastene I to it dog, whenever the dog maybe allowed to prowl about at large; sand again others draw nice distinctions between pupa and dogs. Whilst as a matter of course the word "dog "embraces the whole common lot. Had William Barrington taken the latter into coasideratlon Oa the 23rd February, whsn ha let his" pup" get about he wolld have saved the 51 that th. Bewail conaidered he ought to pay for knowing nothing about it.—Walter Hanker, of Hattori, was not a bit luckier, an i in consequence of forgetting to put his address as well as his name on his innoceot dog's c 'liar, had to part with e the price of an exquisite engrayin7,..—The Police-officers who brought the cases under notice were Con- stables H. Fry and Nichols. A NoI9Y IiARVE3THAN.—Patriek Raygan, who had just come over from the Hmerald Isle. in company with a crowd of his fellows anent an enjoyable 4th of March at the Royal Hotel. and (twin his short stay there consumed so much beer that the police were r.qu.sted to 'shift." them. Ronan did not consider the officer's intentions good enough, and though his fellows cleared off at the command of P.C. Gregory, thought fit to become riotous. Ties result was the constable was compelled to move him, and after a night of durance vile. the Bench thought fit to charge him Is 61, pre- surnchlv for lodging. A YOUNG Dom LAB3THIER ASTRLY.—Patrick Boyle, dock- labonrer. of Runeorn. admitted being drunk and disorderly in King-street on the 4th of March. From. what P.O. Wild- ing said the young fellow was very drunk and on being regnestel to go home. roundly swore at the offiser. He also refused to give him his name. Previous to that he wanted to fight although it was 11.30 n.m.—The Bench.who evidently were not unacquainted with the defendant, fined him Sc and Se 61 costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>CROMWELL STATUE FOR ‘VARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>THE CROMWELL STATUE FOR ‘VARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>11 A. lengthy article appears in "The Warrington Circuit Wesleyan Methodist Church Record .' on " Oliver Cromwell," which says—"Warrington is to have a statue of Cromwell. Thera were reports to the contrary for a few days, but the better senti- ment of the town prevailed. The Free Churchmen in the Council deserve the congratulations showered on them for the manner in which they advocated the acceptance of Mr.Monks's offer. It cannot mat- ter in the least to the memory of Cromwell, which has been fully vindicated by Macaulay and Carlyle, but it would have mattered much to the credit of the town had the offer been rejected. The present to the town is most timely, as this year marks the tercentenary of the birth of Cromwell. If our readere have not made acquaintance with Carlyle's book on Cromwell's Letters and Speeches,' they should hasten to do so. There the man is allowed to speak for himself, and all who are cipeble of appre- ciating a man when they meet one will find much to their mind. Even if they shrink from the labour of wading through the whole of the letters and speeches, they will find the opening chapters marvellously interesting, and will gather in a brief space a vivid picture of the man and the time." It concludes with a graphic picture of the kind of man Carlyle conceives the Puritan to have been : "As indeed all these Parliaments grow strangely in Puritanism ; mere and ever more earnest rises from the hearts of them all, '0 Sacred Majesty, lead us not to Antichrist, to Illegality, to temporal and eternal perdition The nobility and gentry of Eng- land were then a very strange body of men. The English squire of the seventeenth century clearly appears to have believed in God, not as a figure of speech, but as a very fact, very awful to the heart of the English squire. He wore his Bible doctrine round him, says one, as our squire wears his shot belt; went abroad with it, nothing doubting.' King Charles was going on his father's course, only with frightful acceleration: he and his respectable tr,oditione and notions.clothed in old sheepskin and respectable Church-tippets. were all pulling one way; England and the Eternal laws pulling an- other ;—the rent fast widening till no man could heal it</p>
        <p>—COACIULINIL—TRANSPARUNT CHMENT for broken articles.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 10, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MARCH 10, 1899.</p>
        <p>NOTE 3 ON GA.VIE3 AND ATFILETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOO ['BALL. Warrineton's 4isolae agaia .t me Hornets of Rochdale on Saturday was in every respect a poor one, and the home team was lucky to scrape home by a single point. It is indee I remarkable that goal-kicking should have won Warrington the game, for in many cases they have lost owing to their inefficiency in this department. To be the ugly team to seer a try, and break through the opposing defence was Rochdale's luck on Saturday, and they must therefore be accounted to have had exceed- ingly hard lines in losing the match. Warrington were almost equally fortunate in the match at Rochdale earlier in the season, winning there by one point, but on that occasion they scored a try to a goal, so that the win had a little more merit in it. It is rather curious that the two games this season between Warrington and the Hornets were played in the vilest of weather. On the first occasion of the meeting of the teams rain fall in torrents and the field looked as if it had formed but re- cently the bed of the Reach. 01 Siterday a deep gloom o'erhung the b 'We-field, and the last stages of the struggle weft fought out in a blinding blizzard of snow.</p>
        <p>The absesoe of Bate through injury and of Wilcox through inatteatiou to work seriously weakened the Warrington back division on Saturday. and at the last minute R. Bate, who had boen thrown off the" A" team, was drafted into the premier organisation to partner Ashnrst. The " gate " was somewhat balow par. and 4,000 is a comprehensive estimate of the num- ber prisent. Probably there would have been still fewer present had it been generally kaown that Wilcox was going to be an absentee. Until Saturday neither of the Welshmen had appeared ore the Warrington ground, ani the crowd was naturally anxious to see them perform. Thy have yet to se o Wilcox,and will probably have the opportunity in the Wdues match this Saturday. Wilcox is an adept with the gloves, by the way,aud before long will he seen contesting with one of oar local boxers in the Gymnasium. He is a tough customer either on the football field or off.</p>
        <p>Throughout the whole 70 minutes on Saturday play was of a poor description, both teams having their back division in a some what disorganised state. The Horne3s were, however, stroll zlv represented, and it was with some apprehension that the Warringtonians saw them file on the field. "Oo n Paul "—otherwise Kruger—re- appeared in the ranks of the visitors after sever al weeks' absence and his display at half-back was watched with some interest, as efforts were made to bring him to War- rington before the Hornets snapped him up. For a butchers' killer, as ho is by trade, he was not a v formidable looking individual and found his match in Eashurst. Both Warrinzton's goals were obtained in the first quarter when the team was playing with rare dash. 000 came from a clever drop by Hallam outside the 25 Ins, and the other NVIS from a ps.n tit? kick taken by Ashurst. The penalty was inflicted as punishmeot f a deliberate klook-on or fist-out by one of the Rochdale forwards. Probably he heartily regrets his foolishness now, for had he been a little less handy with his fist his team would have won.</p>
        <p>In the second portion of the game the energies of the home team relaxed censiderably, while those of the Horn-ts increased. The fait that the wind was very mull in their favour impelled the visitors to make a sp7cial effort to wipe offarrears. They made little head- way however, and the game for the most part was Ward in mid-field. Towards the clore the Hornets' back; shewed signs of understanding One another's Way, aid several combined movements almost brought W.r- ringtors to grief. Just on the verge of" time" Davies dashed over at the corner much to the dismay of the hom.sters. That deadly goaler, W. Rigg, took the kick with great care, but the difficult angle and the wind c nnhined proved too much for his skill and the shot f tiled. Warrineten were intensely relieved and shoe I no signs of vrrow when the wh'stle s,unlel a moment later leaving them victors by 4 points to 3. I ought to say that both the Ronhiale halvls were in- dividuals of special interest to Warrington, for b 'glee Kruger there was " Buff" Berry. the old Tyldesley vyar- horse whom the Hornets have resuscitated. He was signed ors as a trainer but the Hornets found he coul I still give their own halves a point or two and he has played regularly of late. This only goes to show the des- perate straits the Rochdale Committee have been re- duced to. Berry was terribly slow, even Bob Bate hav- ing no difficolty in checkmating nearly all his efforts. It is high time" Buff" confined himself to the dressing aid the use of the flesh-eleve. The best men on the flernets side werelßigg, Kruger, Davies and Harrison The full beck played a very steady game and was superior to Hallam, who however showed ceosiderabh improvement on his exhibition at Broughton. Woed played well and still continues to improve. It is qeite on the cards that he may turn oet a geed centre yet. At any rate he is as good as anybody else the committee have available. Isherwood, Harris and Fish all played thtlatter being particularly dangerous. R Bate was better then I expected and did net shape at all hadly,while Ashurst played hisconsistently sound game. Morrison and Naylor were the pick of the forwards.</p>
        <p>I understand that" Smack" Bate's knee is strengthen- ing and will probably be well enough to allo.w him to resume his captaincy of the team in the cup-ties. Before that time it is quite on the cards that Bate will be mine host of the -- I most not say where! The matter is not definitely decided yet but it is almost cer- tain that " Smack " will develop into a licensed victualler very shortly.</p>
        <p>The team chosen this wesk is not very likely to defeat Widnes, who are playing in splendid form this season, and have an eve on the championship. The defel,s Old- ham and Flrmightoi have sustained make it quite pos- sible for Oldham. Broughton, and Widnes te completa their elczaEremants with 40 Points each. so that it is not very likely that the "chemicals." will throw any chances away to-day. Ashurst and Fish are relegated to the " "tam, R Ltcliffe appearing on the wing, and R Bate and Wilcox at half-back. •</p>
        <p>Widnes managed to take Broughton Rangers down on Saturday, but as Oldham also lost at Runcorn the cham- pionship competition is not altersd very materially. EiroaThton Atilt have the best chance unless Swinton or Widnes prove too good for theßl in the succeeding en- agents. Warrington's place in the table is a very poor one. They just top the third-raters and are a.mong the last half-dozen, a psi' ion worse than they had last year. It does not seem very probable that they will rise any higher. so that the state of matters is far frem satis- factory. If the "A. " team can carry off the medals it will be the only redeeming feature of the football season in Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire League table up tn date is al wor. Against.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. P P. Oldham ,...25...19... 6. 0...27 93-342...11 12— 53...38 Salford Broughton Rang. 23...18.. 5... 0...37 53-248-11 15— 67...33 Leigh 25...17... 8... 0...36 32-163.-11 31-112...34 Widnes 22..15.. 5. 2 . 35-157..16 17— 83...32 *Swinton... 23..15.. 6.. 2...31 5?.-218 ..11 15— 67-30 Runcorn 23...12... 9... 2...24 31 —141...17 21-101.-26 St. Helens 22...10... 9... 3...25 39-140-15 37-141-23 Warrington 23...11...1J.... 1...14 32-121....18 40-156...23 Rochdale Ilornets23... 6..:14... 3...18 14— 78...34 47-209-.15 Wigan 23.. 4-17.- 2...14 12— 64.-30 40-180...10 Tylriesley 24.. 2...17... 5...11 15— 67 _26 53-215... 9 Stockport 22.. 3...18... 1...12 17— 75...42 70-294... 7 lidnrPcarnbe 24... 2...21... 1... 4 10— 38...37 62-230... 5 *Swinton have had two points deducted for breach of professional rules.</p>
        <p>It was a great disappointment to the Warrington pe3ple t learn on Saturda,r evening that the' A. "team had been defeated at 113chdale by five points ti Though the match was regarded as a stiff one, it was expected that he Wa.rringtan lads wo‘il I just about pull it off. I warned them of the Hornets' sting, but it was useles§, and they have suffersd in consequence. Misfor- tune attlndei Warrington, for Hockenhall sustained an injury to his knee just as he was on the point of scoring a try. Cross and Ratcliffe both played badly in the centre, and it was here in particular that th 3 team was bsaten.</p>
        <p>To secure the reeials and eh imnionship the "A" team will have to make absolutqv certain of the two remain- ing matches, that with Widnes to-day, and Runcorn on Aprl 3rd. Ths severest by far is the Widnee match, as the Widnes "A" team is one of the str3nTest in the League especially on its own ground. But Warrington won there last year when th 3 Chenecals were going strongly for the championships and with the assistance of Ashurst and Fish they ought to manage it again. But it cannot b 3 impressed upon them too strongly that they will have to play for air they are worth from start to finish. Defeat means that the medsls go to Salford. Victory will probably make thma a cntainty for War- rington. Play up lads, don't b 3 "pipped on tin post." !</p>
        <p>The" A" team league now stands as follows, the matches not played owing to the froot counting as draws For.</p>
        <p>Arningt.</p>
