      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>• THE NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR. CONIPETITION.</head>
        <p>• THE NORTHERN UNION. LANCASHIRE SENIOR. CONIPETITION.</p>
        <p>WIRRINGTON WEN BY ONE poiNr</p>
        <p>The return encounter between Warringto.l to! Roch• dale Hornets provided a stiff game at Wilderspool on Saturday. Warrington were without Bate and Wqcox at half back, R. Bats partnering Aehurst loshini the scrimmage. Rochdale brought their strenges t team, including once more Kruger the Welsh half back who was reported to have gone b Jet to his fatherland. there were about 4,000 spectators present, the bitter coldnens of the weather no dodo: preventing many from attend- ing. Ths light was vpry bad and half way through the second portion of the game snow began to fall and the game was finishod amid very unpleasant conditions Before the commencement of the game the home team went through its annual ordeal in front of the camera. Mr. F. Kennedy, Broughton. Wai the referee and at 3.30 the teams lined out as follow:</p>
        <p>Warrington : Henan:l, full back; Fish, Wood, Isher, wood, and Harris, threequerters; Ashurst and R Hate_ half-backs ; Taylor, Naelor, Boardman, F tirhurit,Merri son, Swift, Scholtze, and Sadlow forwards. Roch Isle hornets: W Rieg, full b ack ; Davie3,Every. Field, and House, threequarters ; Kruger, and Berry half-backs; H arrison, Metcalfe, Jardine, Wilson, Cooper, Elewarth, Ashworth, and Hardiker forwards. Warrington won the toss and secured the assistance of a strong breeze in the firat half. Harrison set the ball rolling. Warrington replying win a dribble headed by Morrison, Every ultimately saving at the Rochdale 25 line. Bats mede a mull from the first scrummage which almo,t let Rechdale through, and then Ashurst had a kick charge I down and it fell on Hallam to save. Berry metered but was grassed by Bate before he could pert with the ball. Warrington got away witn a pesaiag run through the hands of A.shar3t. Bate and Wood, Fish takine the ball a slide forward at the finish. Fish was applauded for smartly robbing House, and running from centre to the line where he put in a saort punt, and Rigg marked cleverly. Bate dribbled from the pack and at the quarter line Fish raced up and secured. pas- , sing beck to Woo i oa the line, and Rochdale had hard work to prevent a score. Wood was held however, and the Hornets hi:lkea out to Hellain who took a cool drop and landed a fine goal for the homeeters. A mill by anise led to another invasien of the visitors territory, Morrison and'Fairteurst rushine the bill wall up the field. Berry effected a relief by a flyine kick from the scrum which sent the ball beyond the Warrington hacks anl Wood was obliged to kick in touch to prevent a score. Tan Hornets forwards now asserted themselves and fol- lowing the touch kick up looked like scoring until Flallam made a wonderfol catch in the thick of them. Hallam kicked clear and Field was downed by Seholtze before he could reply. Rigg was next called upon bat was fund vite safe though he had to punt in touch. The kick-out sent the ball right tinier the visitors' vat, one of the forwards foolishly fisting out and get. ting pen dised. Ashurst took the kick and landed Warringtoife second goal amid cheering. Bate,Ashurst, Wo3d, and Fish male another combined effort which gained ground, the last named player try- ing a drop at pal which was no.; far out. e.stouret followed with a " similar attempt ad Rigg put the ball out of play just as tee home for /nada dashed up. A series of quick passes along the Roch- dale line should have ended in a score but Wood dropped instead of passine and the chance was lost. Rochdale obtained relief by tacking Hallam in his own h alf, and Kruger made a bold effort to score under the pots but wee, held on the linos. Warrineton were hard pressed until Berry got off-side and a free kick was given the hemeeters. Davies brought the boll back with a run te the corner, but after a brief stay here the Hornets wero repulsed by Ashurst. Half-tim,, Warrington 2 goals, H rieets nil.</p>
        <p>Taylor re-started and good work by Davies gave the Hornets the upper hand for a time. Wood followei up a punt speedily but was put off-side by Rigg. Warring- ton would not be denied however and at last the ball travelled to Harris's wing, and was carried into the visc. tors' quarters. -Warrington put in some hard play but were unable to pass Rigg, who with a run and kick sent Way to neutral territory. Fine passing by the homesters lei to another invasion, which was cleared by Jardine who put in a huge kiek. Warrington were penalised,and Every took the lc,ck from a good position, but made a very poor attempt. The Hornets began to Rho v snore combination. awl a combined movement finished up with House dashing dame the touch until bowlel over by Fish and Hallam. Snow now began to fall, and pity became of an uninteresting Fr:rambling nature. R onh- dale had rather the best of it, the gale being behind them, and were continually in tbe home half. Just before time smart p using by Field, Every, and Davies, enabled the latter to round the home backs and run in at the corner. Rigg took the goal kick from a difficult angle, and it was with a sigh of relief that the homeeters saw the ball go wide of the pests. The whistle sounded for time intme-iiately after, and WArrinvori retired winners of a well-contested game by 2 goals (4 paints) to one try (3 points). RUNCORN TURN THE TA.BLES ON OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>The return match between Runcorn and Oldham, which came off at the Irwell-lane enclosure, on Satur- day, was attended by a large concourse of spectators ; notwithstanding the fact that there was a counter at- traction at Widnes, in the match between that team and Broughton Rangers. At Oldham, it will be remembered Runcorn was beaten by 27 points to 3 points ; and al- though the opinion was expressed that Oldh trn wer s hardly any the b ttter team, and should have won by a much less score, many held a different view. But as thine turned out on Saturday, the expression that " Runcorn w3uld bsat them hollow at•horne " was fully borne out.</p>
        <p>The teams face I as follows :—ltnneorn : HouFfhton. back; D Jones, J Butterworth, T Warder and Wilkinson, three-quarters ; J Faulko•=r and J Nickson, halves ; A Taylor, 8 Walker, 11 Farmer, Darlington, Lightfoot, Gravter, Langley, and Tomlincon, forwards. Oldham : Thomas, bLck ; Williams, T Davies, Fletcher and Martin, three-quarters ; Lawton and Lees, halves ; Bonsor, Brown, Moffatt, 'Leifer, Frater, Sellars Murrell, and VVormall, forwards. Referee : Mr. Eagles (Salford).</p>
        <p>Farmer kicked off Thomas returning, when almost He- m clietely Butterworth let people knew he wag there and gained considerable groend. Fratsr renlied,but Hough- ton established his side well in the Oldham quarters. Off-side against Oldham was cleared by a rush on the part of their forwards. Nickson performed well behind the scrummage and Jones was given an opening. Wil- liams replied. his effort being capital. The Runcorn for- wardesho wed aetonishing form, their combination being superb arid their rushee irresistible. Ma.rtin was com- pelled to kick dead. Oldham three-quarters then tried to open out, only to be baulkee by trig keenness of the " linnet " front rank. Fletcher got slightly hurt. Upon resuming Thomas was again assailed. Faulkner judiciously fed his three-quarters from a scruminage.but the tackling of the viiit3rs was so keen that the ball did not get far away. Runcorn were penalised for obstruc- tion from which Oldham got away; only to be immedi- ately rushed back again and had not W'alker "knocked-on " a score would have resulted. , Sellars kisked into touch. Farmer caught and fairly burst through his opoonente carrying them along with him. Later) Lees and Wilkinson collided in millieldj Butter worh marked,but was roughly handled. Jones got away and soon tee ball was over the Oldham line. Fletcher teuchine detd. D ivies responded but , Butterworth and Tomlinson accounted for him. Receiv- ing from a scrimmsege Nickson tried to "bluff" but was collared. Things became exciting near the Oldham line, and for the third time it was necessary to kick dead. Langley was applauded f3r a good Vece of work Hough- ton failed to field the ball and was held by about three Oldliamitee who beean to look dangerous. until relief came in off-side. Moffatt was able t get away through a forward pass. Houghton was called upon to save but almost let the visitors in. Fletcher Lees mad Martin got going and came a short distance in Oldham's best style, but Martin failed to take the pass oat. Offside gave Oldham a chence but Toffees kick caught the posts go- ing about six yards back into the field, Runcorn re- sponded to it by clearing off . with a regular stampede which sorely taxed the Oldham de- fence. Butterworth got away and tried to drop. From the scrum Fieteher received and kicked te,Farmer who marked. Floughtoa saved a further attack upon his charge, arid followe I up eagerly, but Fle'cher made a mark. Jones got an opening, but ran into touch. Williams however, grassed him to make things safe. Nielrson was cautioned as well as one of the Oldhana for- wards for rouehness. Up to the half time play was mostly in the Oldharn hid ; due principally to the floe exhibition of the home for wards whose play was all that could be desired. Just on half-time however, 011- barn raised the seige, and Houghton was compelled to touch down. At the interval neither side had scored. though Wa'ker nearly got over in the last minute : Half-time, no score. Moffett reoponed after the respite, t 3 which Houghton responded. The advantage gained by him was soon re- covered and Oldham looked ugly. Lightfoot relieved by marking and from there Rutreorn had all the best of the exchanges. Nicksen fielded and kicked carefully. Frater was disappointed in not getting a mark which was certainly a good one. From the scrum Oldham broke away with a tremendous rush and Heughton had to fall on the ball to save. Play returned and Oldham were penalised about the centre. Houghton took the kick and dropped a beautiful goal. The rseeerie e was deafening. Continuing Runcore epee. vie excit-einent got to white 'vat when L'.ghtfo got over, thouzh the try was di; :Lowed. Moffatt secured an opening and was away, and got a huge kick in. Houe,h- ton marked. Thomas replied to Hough ton,</p>
        <p>Butterworth effected a wonderful mark and when fairly surrounded by his opponents. Play was really good and fast,both fifteen's going Splendidly. Later the Oldham 25 was besieged. Runcorn pressed, Fletcher saved. Thea Faulkner sent over but Runcorn did not follow up keenly enough. Thomas relieved and a penalty fell to Oldham but come to noughe. Return- ing to the attack Butterwor.th gained ground, and Faulkner sec tred, Diving finally to Jonas that player scoring a try. Houzhton's kick, though a good one. failed. Resuming it was not long before Runcorn were again dangeroul. Lees cleared and transferred the scrummage a little farther off. Back they came however, and before the excitement consequent en the first try had fairly sul:Fidsd Nickson was over with a sec-md. Tan effort was superb. Butterworth took the kick but failed to convert. Oldham forwards accomplished some magni- ficent rushes. but Warier. Butterworth and Houghton proved a stumbling block to them. Martin marked and B-msor put in a good run. Taylor marked and trans- ferred. Houghton was pressed but ant his kick in under difficulties. Runoorn got away again and it was then plainly to be seen that Oldham were beaten. Oldham br wards began to wheel the scrimmages and several ugly rushes were made. Lees securrd and passed out bviit was of no use, and Ruuc)rn soon came back again to trouble Thorn %s. Shortly after the end Came. The final result being: Runcorn, 1 g0a1,2 tries (8 points); Oldham, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>WIDNES V. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES V. BROUGHTON RANGERS.</p>
        <p>At Widnes,eon a slippery turf, before 5,000 spectators. The bro'hers James and Whitehead were absent from the Rangers' ranks, and Widnes were wiesout HeSlOl3. Widnes had the advantage of the wind in the first half, and quickly became aggressive, Baty just missing the goal with a penalty kick. Afterwards Aspey dribbled over the visitors' line, but kicked too hard, and the ball wont dead. Keen play followed at midfield, every inch of the ground being stiffly conte‘tsd. Martin ran to the visitors' quarter, but Jackson tackled him and the ball wa 3 returned. Baty neetralised fine play by Barratt, and Damon transferred the ball to the home quarter. Here Chapman made a futile drop at goal. Widnes re- lieved the pressure, Martin intercepting a pass by Duck, and Nevin dropping at goal, but without result. The ball travelled quickly, Mena% Nevitt, Baty. Messenger, Barrattiand Danson being prominent. At length Douglas passed to Rispan in Vera visitors' quarter, and the latter ran in spleadidly,scoring at the corner amid tremendous cheerieg. Baty failed at goal. Widnes resumed after the inrerval,they then leadine hy three points to nothing. They attacked again, but were repulsed, and Nelson ran brilliantly te the home line, where Martin tackled him just in time. L 0398 play followed about midfield, after which Lamb (Broughton) was badly kicked on the lee, and had to be carried off the field, but ha subse- quently returned. The Rangers attleked, but Danson kck,d toe harri.and Baty sent the ball dead. Chapman ha d an unsuccessful try at goal fr am a penalty,and then Doluelas and Martin transferred play to the visitors' quarter. Chapman relieved the nresaere, and Barrett also running stronely, Widnes had to defend. The for- wards, however, nlievel, and in the vigil-ars' half pou.g. las passed to Martin, who, eluding Jackson and Barrett, scored a fine try under the posts, Baty placing an easy goal. Afterwards the Rangers pressed, but Eleadley relieved to centre, and a selendi I game ended with the score :—Wicinee, one goal and two tries (eight peints); Broughton Rangers, nil.</p>
        <p>ST.-HELENS v. STOCKPORT.</p>
        <p>At Edgeley Park, Stockport. played at first with the wind. After a few scrummages Grange led a rush, Foulkes checking. Immediately afterwards he was cs.lle I upon again to save. The visitors' forwards drib- bled down to the home line, but before they could fall on the ball Saville kicked dead. The home back passed well but the advantage wag lost in the transfer from Saville to Grange. The visitors at length worked down by a good dribble to the home line, and Reynolds secured a try Foulkes kicking a goal. Stockport then made a good at- tack. Chorlton having hard luck in not scoring, and Saville Mige a poor bid for ("oat from an ea sy position. The visitors had the beat of the play, but after a good passing bout by the home backs Sands gained a try, no goal resulting. St. Hslens led by live points to three at the interval. The drizzie which had continued through- out the first half now developed into a blinding snow- storm, and good play was out of the question. The visitors certainly held an advantage. Briers added a try which Foulkes irnoroysd from an easy position. Sands made a good attempt to get away, but Whiteley inter- vened. Jur; before time Briers scored an unconverted trr for St. Helens, who won by two goals and three tries (1.3 points) to one try (three_neints.)__</p>
        <p>TFTE (UP COMPETITION. QUALIFYING ROUND.</p>
        <p>The Northern Union Challanrze Coo Cmnpe.ition was entered noon on Saturday, and of the 20 matches Played all were definitelv decided with one exception where a drawn gam n resulted.</p>
        <p>. - Tha ars the results of the games played:--.. 1410 _ 0_ _ E_ 0 0-0</p>
        <p>Gore_</p>
        <p>Birstal Bowling • 0 1- 3 Morley 0 .0-0 Workineoo 5 7-31. Amblesid.l" 0 0-9 Normanton 2 6-22 Hill Marlborough 0 0-0 Dalton Dewsbury 1 0— 2 Todmorden 0 0-9 Fleetwold -13 Blackpool -8 wArrington St M... 1 3-11 Runcorn Hornets 0 0-0 Werneth -13 Abbey Hills -5 Ulverston 1 3-11 rpm:miter , -6 Birkeehe 4 Wds ... 1 2 8 Lostock Gralam -3 Millom . 2 10-34 Askam 0 0-0 Marvport Altrincham 1 2 8 Leigh Shamrocks... 0 2-6 York 2 3-13. Kinsley -4 Radcliffe — —l2 Smallbridge -0 Outwood Church ... 1 2 8 Hull Stone Ferry O. 0-0 Saddleworth ...... ... 0 2 6 Lees _ l, 0-2</p>
        <p>Runcorn Recreation, Latchford' Rangers, Flatcher. Russell and Co.. Waterhead Hornets, Salford, S. Bar- tholemew's, Whitworth, Grove United, Featherstme, and Rathwell had byes,</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE " A " TEAMS COMPETITION. WARRINGTON V. ROCHDALE HORNETS. WarrinEtton " A" team vistei Rochdale on Saturday the match with the latter team being one of considerable importanc, awl the Warrington men had looked for- ward to it flr same weeks. Though the " Hornets." had not much at stake. thy had put on the best fifteen</p>
        <p>, EVERY WOMAN Should send two stamps for our NEW BOOK containina valuable information to Ladies how all Irregularities may be entirely Avoided or Removed by Simple Means. Rec)mmendel by eminent physicians as the only Safe, Sire, and genuine Remedy. "Never Fails." Thousands O Testimonials.—Mr. W. li. BLANCHARD. Claremont Hot, •e, Forest-road, Dalston, Londln;</p>
        <p>hey could possibly get for the occasion, evidently horoughly determined to beat the visitors whose arrival in the town was heralded by the announcement of the match on posters describing them as "the champion' A' team of Lancashire.' The teams were camposed as follows :—Warrington Boscow, full back ; Ratcliffe, Hockenhull, Cross, Moores, three- quarters; Allen and Duckworth, halves; Hatton, Wilcock Skelhern, Cunningham, Eden, Jolley, Hill, forwards. Rochdale Hornets, Beckwith, full-back; Thompson, Ellis, Vaughan, Isherwood. three-quarters; Mason and Henderson, halves; Tweedale, Chadwick, Leach, Smith, Burns, Nnttall, Farmer, Hirst, forwards. Warrington kicked off amidst a welcome cheer from the spectators. Isherwood touched down in the first few minutes. Thompson, misfielding the ball, almost let Warrington in. Vaughan relieved with a strong kick, and Ellis failed to take the ball when on the line. The game was stubbornly conteste4, and the Warriugton forwards well held their own. Several times the ball was dribbled over the Hornets' line but the visitors were unfortunate in not scoring. The half-backs did not play up to their usual form ; they seemed to be too eager and misjudged their passes. The play of Ratcliffe and Cross on the three-quarter line was faulty. The former was continu- ally nalsfielding the ball, and lost the team considerable ground. Cross was completely off colour and his slow- ness in getting away caused him to neglect his wing men. The Hornets had the best of matters and Farmer kicked a penalty goal. Half time : Hornete, two points; Warrington, nothing. In the second half play settled in the Hornets' quarter, Warrington making vigorous but futile efforts to sore. Hockeuhull played a good game and but for his knee giving way would have scored a brilliant try. The Hornets were penalized, but the kick was charged, and Farmer, with a fine dribble, took play to the other end. Neither team could claim any material advantage until Smith scored for the Hornets. Farmer failed at goal. Result :—Roclaciale Hornets five points, Warrington nothing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>EttRLESTOWN v. Sr. HELENS RECS.</p>
        <p>These teams met on Saturday at Earlestown to play off their Lancashire Alliance fixture. The greatest in- terest was taken in the match from the fact that both teams are in the running for the Alliance Championship, consequently there was a big crowd of spectators, a special train being run from St. Elelens. There ware fully 3,000 people present, and the financial result was most satisfactory to the home club. The afternoon, however, was not of a promising character rain falling intermittently,and a bitterly cold wind blowing obliquely over the ground. Earlestown put their usual team on the field but St. Helens were short of Jackson who was playing for Wales against Ireland. Punctually at 3.30 the teams lined up as follows :</p>
        <p>Earlestown: Chew, goal; Wilkinson and Hayes, backs ; Lee. Parr, and Green, half backs ; Settle,Leach, Hampson, Harrison, and Smith, forwards. St. Helens Recs: Hoy, goal; Monks and Woodward, backs; Ellaby, Preston, and Hunter, half backs; Davies,Baines, Smith, Jones, and Dumbell, forwards: Wilkinson won the toss and elected to play up bill, against the wind, and Smith kicked off. Immediately the Recs. by pretty passing, became dangerous and Parr handling, gave the Recs a free kick close in goal, which resulted in Chew having to concede a corner in order to save his citadel. This was nicely placed by Davies,end Durnbell getting his head in the way, scored out of the reach of Chew. The score opening within a minute from the start, put the Recs. on good terms with them- selves, and they went off with a rattle from the centre. Their short passing completely upset the calculations of the home team, and for the first quarter-of•an-hour, meters looked decidedly bad for Earlestown. The Rees pressed considerably and another corner was ob- tained but the ball was worked away. The home eleven from this point appeared to get the measure of their opponents, and some long passing by the forwards enabled Smith to get hold, and he shot hard into Hoy's hands, who threw away. They again returned to the attack, and Hampson in a favourable position shot weakly. A foul close in to the Earlestown Foal looked dangerous, but the defence was sound, and the Rees were sent to the right about. A pretty passing move- ment by the home forwards brought trounle to the Roes defenders, but Settle when in good position was unable to get round for hist'final effort. The`game then opened out, and frequent visits were made to each goal, but the final efforts of the Earlestown front rank were in every case lacking in finish. At half time the score was :—St. Helens Recs., 1 ; Earlestown. 0. On turning round the Rees were considerably ham- pored by a snow storm which broke over the ground, but this was in favour of the home eleven. The Earle. town play improved somewhat, and they pressed the Rees considerably. After hovering about the goal for a short time, Hampsoe shot in hard, and equalised. The Rees afterwards played as a beatee team, and refused to be drawn out of their own quarters. Only twice did they cross the half way line, and then only three of the front rank were up, their tactics being decidedly un- sportsmanlike. However, the Earlestown men pegged away at the goal, and Hay saved miraculously on several occasions. The game to the finish was entirely without interest, and no further addition was made to the score. Final, Earlestown 1, St. Helens Rees. 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>SOUTH AFRICAN SHARES.</head>
        <p>SOUTH AFRICAN SHARES.</p>
        <p>There are signs of an approaching mining boom in the shares of Matabeleland Rhodesian Companies, since the surprisina possibilities attaching to those shares rez..eived another illustration in a cablegram just to hand an- nouncing a sensational discovery on orie of these pro- perties, the roof being stated to give 17i ounces of gold o the ton. The shares of Matabele Mines have risen to over £3 lOs and those of Matabele Gold Reefs to over £9 within the last week. Taylor's Matabele Gild Fields £1 shares b-.fore the slump which took place in mining shares in 1895, were selling at over £2 15s per share. On the Company's "Battery Reef" there are very ex- tensive old workings, and on this property a shaft has b.en sunk to the depth of 35 feet, exposing a reef of more than four feet in width, and panning svell over two ounces of gold to the ton. These shires are now selling on the London Stqck Exchange at 7s per share and as they have raised all the additional working capital they require, and own some los the most valuable gold pro- perties in Matabeleland close to where the latest strike of 17-} ounces of gold to the ton has been made, those in the ksaow have been quietly picking up the shares at their present low prices and say they expect to see the prices of the shares of Taylor's Matabele Gold Fields in the near future standinp, at as many pounds as they are now shillings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>WARRINGTON CORPORATION BILL.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON COR- PORATION BILL.</p>
        <p>THE POLL-LARGE MAJORITY IN FAVOUR OF THE BILL.</p>
        <p>The collection of the voting papers was satisfae• torily carried out on Tuesday by 2 members of the Borough Police Force, who had delivered them Oa, the previous Friday. The work of examining !Ina, ascertaining the number of votes for and against the Bill was commenced in the Council Chamber av the Town Hall on Wednesday morning at tell o'clock. It was completed under the superinteu. deuce of the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) e'q the Deputy Town Clerk(Mr. Stanley Wilson.) About 20 counters were employed for the purpose, and it; was soon ascertained beyond a doubt that there was a large majority in favour of the Bill. ExcePV the Mayor, the officials of the Corporation and the representatives of the press, who were permitted te be present on behalf of the public, there was 0° one in attendance during the morning and after noon, and the proceedings were of a routine charge" ter. Numerous mistakes had been made by voters in filling up the papers. Many of the voters ill. serted X, others their initials only, while not a fey; wrote " Yes " or" No." Some voted both in favour, of and against the Bill,and many sent in unmarkso papers. In a number of cases the signature of the voter had been put in tbe wrong place, and the task e papers sf which deciding devolvedupon upon thelegality o:laymany w oafs t liaeo light one.</p>
        <p>-Shortly after three o'clock the result of the poll was announced as follows : For the 8i11... 9 419 Against... ...</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Majority for the Bill__ 7,037</head>
        <p>Majority for the Bill__ 7,037</p>
        <p>An analysis of the voting shows that there wets, 7,749 voting papers sent in in favour of the Bill ana 1907 against. No fewer than 121 papers were die' allowed and 714 were returned blank. It will be seen that the total number 'of papers returned CO the Town Hall was 10,491, and the number sent was 11,817. leaving 133 d unaccounted for. At the conclusion of the counting, the Mayer said he was glad to see Mr. Gough present as a representative of the opponents of the Bill. lie thought he would go away satisfied with the yrs/ the counting had been transacted, and with tbe papers he (the Mayor) had disallowed. He strongli advised the opponents of the Bill not to put the ratepayers to the expense of a ppll but nevertheless he did not regret it for so important a matter. lif? felt confident the Council of the Borough woula strive to fulfil their promises anti deserve the con: fidenee the ratepayers and property owners luta reposed in them. (Hear hear.) Mr. GOUGH, on behalf of those opposed to the Bill, regretted that the voting had not been by ballot, as in his opinion,the result would have been different; He did net believe the true views of the people ha' been taken on the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Y.M.C.A.—At the open meeting of the Y.M.C.A. the Mission-room on Sunday, Mr. J. Mounfield pre sided. The Rev. J. F. Matthews, of Latchford, gave addresses on" Young Men's Religion," ana Mr.J.Hobia. Jun., read the lesson. Messrs. E. and F. Grounds were responsible for a solo, and in company win the Misses E. and L. Grounds rendered a quartette. Mr.S.Spencer officiated at the organ.</p>
        <p>Sr. THOMAS'S CHu-sca.—The Rev. S. P. Gray, curate in-charge of this church, has been appointed to the curacy of AU Sainte Mission (St. Margaret's Pan, it). Altrincharn. He will succeed the Rev.E.R. Tarbuckwho has been appointed Vicar of St. Paul's, Warrington, Th Rev. H. V. Pigot, the present Vicar of St. Pa ul's, vier, instituted to the living of Stackton Head. eaterde afternoon at Chester, by the Bishop. Eirly Cour munion was held at 7.30 at St. Thomas's Church 00 Friday, to which all those holding any office in connew tion with the church were specially invited. FUNERAL OF 'MRS. J. S. HUGHES, HILL CLIFFE.--001 Saturday afternoon the funeral of Mrs. Hughes, wife the Rev. J. S. Hughes, 20, Cliffe-view, pastor of 110' Cliffe Chapel, took place with every sign of deep 55111- pathy with Mr. Hughes at the sad loss. Prior to the re" moval of the body.the Revs J F Matthews and J T Sew" field conducted a service in the house. Represeneativ.ce from the following to and towne proceeded front of the corteze to Hill Ciiffe Chapel :—Hill Baptist, Irwell-terrace Church. Bacarp ; Earlestowtt Wrexham, Ebenezer Church, Cefn, Nefith Wales; Lit"° Leigh ; Latchford. Golborne-street, Wycliffe, Ebereeero and Providence (Stockton Heath) Churches, and Liver: pool. The chief mourners were the Rev J S Iluebt (husband), Bertie (adopted son), Mr and Miss Edward' (lather and sister, Wrexham), Mr J PE3war3s (trotlV.l,4 Dublin), Miss P Edwards (oiece), Mr, Mrs mi Griffiths (Cefamawr, North Wales), AL- atT Mrs Davies (Newport), Mr and Mrs RobeF,e (Newport), and Mn, Mrs and Master Jenk.lo,e, (Newbridge, North Wales). In the chapel the follovels6 hymns were very impressively sung by a large eongre" gation of deceased's friends :—"Rest from thy laboured rest," and "Peace, perfec'.; peace." lessons were rea by the Revs. J F Matthews and J T the Rl' John Yenge offered prayer, and the Rep. John Aldo: Little Leigh, gave a short address. Al the body ige,i conveyed, from the church the organist Flayed the "D March" in Saul. The service at the graveside was Or ducted by the Rev. J Aldis, and the hymn. "Tner will be done" was sung. Wreaths were snit ty the) following'''. Hill Cliffe Y.P.S.C.E., Mr Booth and iamily, Mrs Dodd. Walton; Miss Davies,Hyde Park,Lonflon; Miss Edwarie, Llangollen; Mrs Veraables, and Mrs Milliams, Chink ; ?If and Mrs England, Mrs Bamford, Mr and Mrs Harreso Irwell Baptist Church, Bacup; Mr and Mn lienworthi and family (Cliffe-view), Mr and Are J Strange, Mr V:d Mrs E Lloyd-Jones, and the Rev, J F Mitthews. Hughes has received a large number of le:ters of BP' patlay and condolences from various parts. " LINSEED COMPOUND " (Trad,t Mark for Coughs and Colas. of proven erfleny. 131 d. Sold by Chemists</p>
