      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>letters to the Maar.</p>
        <p>THE WATER SCHEM.E.</p>
        <p>Sir,—My suspicion that the liquor interest had some- thing to do with this scheme received a very emphatic confirmation at the ratepayers' meeting in the Parr Hell on Wednesday the Ist inst. On that occasion Mr. C cillor Lee told the audience that " it was a fact that if the present state of things continued it would be impos- sible for them, for one thing, to supply Messrs. Walker 'with sufficient water for the making of their beer." And as a mem why such a calamity should be prevented he Stated that "it would be a serious thing if Messrs Walker removed their industry from the town,although, as those before him well knew, the Winwiek water was snore suitable for the making of beer than any other in England except Burton. U. they went away, however, it would be a serieue matter for the town." As these utterances evoked no discreet one may assume that they represented the views of the members of the Council and thereto:layers prenent.But as majorities are net infallible but are often on the wrong side it will do ne harm if I present one or two reasons why the witearawal of Messrs. Walker and their establishments from Warring- ton would be rather beneficial than otherwise to the inhabitant 1. The trade carried on by Messrs. W siker cannot be de- Scribed as a good and useful one, for it ministers to no human necessity and creates appetites whose indulgence leads to deplorable consequences. I think it was John Wesley who said that it would be far better if the grain destroyed by the brewery processes had been carted into the sea, ss in that case the only lose would be the grain and we should be saved from the terrible and costly expenditure on prisons, poorhouses, lunatic asylums &amp;c., which result from the" finished article." The ratepayers of Warrington will shortly be called upon to pay some £30.600 for a new" ornament to the town " in the shape of Police Buildings for the detention and punishment of evil doers male up chiefly of drunken cases. And again, fully endorsing as Ido Mr. Wesley's view of the waste of grain involved in the manufacture of strong drink, what shall I say of the loss of water caused by the same process? Is it not a crime against God and man to pervert that pure and health giving flail from -its natural me and, by the in- fusion of injurious drugs, to convert it into a powerful agent of intoxication. Would it not be infinitely better to allow it to flow on to the ocean refreshing man and beast and fertilising the fields on its way than to ter- tare it into an instrument of drunkenness, crime and poverty. The liquor makers and sellers of Warrington do flou- rish and prosper, but at what a cost to the nemmunity How many thousands of pounds a year of the hard earned wages of the working classes find their way into the tills of the brewers and publicans. And what value do they receive for their money? Incipient or fully de- veloped intoxication and the deplorable consequences which result therefrom. And these are all. it is greatly to be regretted that our Town Council and Magistrates cannot or will not se 6 that the liquor traffic is an enemy and not a friend to mankind wherever it exists. Insead of benefitting a c mum nity as other trades do it impo- verishes it; and is responsible for fully three fourths of the crime and poverty which prevail in all our towns sad cities. May we hope that the time will come when those in authority will change their attitude towerds this traffic, and we their powerful infleenca to suppress it as the greatest obetaclo that stands in the way of the physical, social, and intellectual progress of the people. Without it England would in lead be the happiest and most prosperous country in the world.—Yours truly lONA.•</p>
        <p>PIONEER LECTURE'S AT EA.RLESTOWN,</p>
        <p>THE GROWTH OF THE BRITISH E'VEPIRE.</p>
        <p>The second of a course of lecture; on" The Growth of the British Empire—The British in South Africa" was given in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on eledntsday evenieg, before a large audience by the Rev. A. Calde- con, 8.D., Cambridge. Dr Hayward presided, and said in the absence of Mr. Gsimke-Draeten whe was announced to take the chair that night, he had been asked to occupy his place. The duties of the position were not very onerous, in fact, without wasting any more of their time, he would simply introduce the Lecturer. (Applause.) The Lecturer said he was there with the subject "The Eeglish in South Africa." He did not appear before them as a traveller who had spent any length of time in S;uth Africa. He had lived abroad but not in South Africa. He was there as a student, who liked to watch what was going on in other parts of the world, and he had done tnis for more than 20 years, and especially with regard to the growth of the British Empire. He ceuld aesure them that he had found it exceedingly in- terestuig, and if he could not excite any interest in their minds, the fault would be entirely ,his own. They were only cencerneel with South Africa that night. The whole of that Continent had come into closer touch with Europe of late. We touched on the East, and had colonies in the West, but they were only of minor isnportance,but in the Seuth we were deeply eneagel. By South Africa he meant Africa south of the great river Z tmteai, and in that part of Africa, south of tkat river, England was the predominating power, and it depenifyi upon us what the future of that part of the world wou:d be. We were not the first Europeans to enter into South Africa; we were busy in other parts of the world, and it was a nation close to us in alliance, in blood, and language wno first took up the position there, and that nation was the Dutch. Tne Dutch were our very serious rivals at one time, and if his audience would read books about the time of Cromwell,they would see treat the matter uppermost in people's minds was our rivalry with the Detch. The Ditch landed, built a fort, and took possession at the Cape of Good Hope, and therefore they had a footing there, and they fixed them- selves there for 200 years. Oar entry into the field began just 100 ye ire ago and we first had interest there in 1798. The great eiapoleonie wars were disturbing the world. Wapo'eon stretched out Ws hand, and laid it upon Hot- land.The Dutch were toe weak to resist himeanci Holland was for a short time, practically part of the French dominions. Knowing that if the Dutch were not strong enough to hold Holland, they could not hold their colonies, England said they were not going to allow the Dutch celoniee to fall into French hands,and sent an expe- dition to the Cape, ani took possession of it. The Dutch did not ohj -ct, and did not mind letting Us have it as we were in c!ose relationship to them. When peace was made after the battle of Waterloo, the Cape remained in England's possession. Some compensation was paid the Dutch, and since that time the E oelish had been planted in South Africa, so that they would see we had no right there except as protectors, from 1798, until the batie of Wet,Tloe. Toe Dutch were not very enterpriting. It was mei some wool was brought down to the coast to be shipped, but there was so much mismanagement that the wool was b'own away before the ship came up. The Hors or farmers did nnt.cake kindly,to the British rule, and many of them trekkel to the Interior away from the British rule. They moved across the Orange and Taal rivers, and settled down in the country there- abouts, and the Orange Free State, and the Transvaal Republic came abate. Ultimately, as the British race spread, and pushed their territories on, their way was blocked by the two Republics. The Beers were approached and asked to give up their independence and allow themselves to be annexed by Britian. One of our Governors out there obtained some sort of a halt hearted concurrence, and he immediately communicated the fact that the Transvaal Republic was no more. People in this country were surprised,and very soon after it began te be rumoured that the Boers were going to resist an- nexatieb, and ultimately the Boer war broke out. He gave some particulars of this war, ref 2rring to the dis- asters which overtook the British troops at Laings IN,ck and Mejub Hill. Reinforcements were sent out, and Sir Frederick Roberta ha I coins-need of about 8,000 troops. He had just landed in Africa when word was sent out that it was decided to let the Boers have their indepen. dance. The question had been a difficult one, and many had said that it was a crowning mistake to hr. the Boers have their way. The .drafting of the treaty giving England and the Suzerainty was faulty and there had been squabbles on that point. He thee alluded briefly to the Jameion Raid and its effect upon British influence. He next spoke of the Kimberley diemond mines, the dis- covery of gold on :he Randt, and the influence exerted by Mr Cecil Rhodes on aff,irs in South Africa. He next peered on to a descriAtou of th 3 Bushmen, Hottentots, and Zeliee and referred to the wars with the Zulus and the Kat-allele and pointed out teat under our cello these races were increaiinet and flourishing. (Applause.) The lecture was illustrated by a number of limelight views. Votes of thanks to the lecturer and chairman terminated the proceedings.</p>
