      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>EX A MINER..' FRIDAY, MARCH 10, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EX A MINER..' FRIDAY, MARCH 10, 1899.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.INEWTON TTRBAN DISTRT.CT NEWTON DEBATING SOCIETY. COUNCIL. The usual weekly meeting was held in the Con- gregational Schoolroom on Monday evening, Mr. A. Bowes presiding. The subject was an essay on "Professor Blackie ; his sayings and doings,' by Mr. Colin Mcflardy, on which discussion after- wardi tollowed.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Counc:l was held in the Towl Hall, Earlestown, on Menday afternoon. Mr. C. B. F: Borrm, C C., presided, and there wer present :—Messrs. W. Collinewood, J. W. Emmett, J.P.. P. McMahon, W. Gilmour, R. Stone, J.P., J. Sinspsrm, Dr. J. N. Patter- son, W. Thompson, E. T. Evans, W. Beatty, J. Slee, T. Ormand,with Mr; W. W. Shirley (clerk), Mr. J. Brierley (sanitary inspeltor). Mr. 83W0.5 (911CVAVOr). TECHNICAL INSTRUCTION.</p>
        <p>P.S.A. The usual weekly meeting of this Society held on Sunday afternocn in the Co-operative Hall, was presided over by Mr. Joseph Smith. The principal speaker was the Rev. F. E. Miller. and vocal items were given by Miss Corbett. Recitltiong of a suit- able character were given by Mr. E.• Robinson, and Mr. R. Leyland presidel at the piano. HARLESTOWN WESLEYA.N BAZAAR.</p>
        <p>The Technical Initruction Committee recommended that the sum of £B5 be recommended to the Council for payment to the Viaduct Institute Technical Instruction Committee. being a grant from the Council for the session 1898.9 in aid of Techsical lastrustion for the dis- trict.</p>
        <p>This three days' .bs-zaar was brought to a SUCCOSS- f ill termination on Saturday evening. It was opened on Friday by Mr. Burgess, and on Saturday by Mr. J. J. Cooke, of St. Ilelens, in the unavoidable absence of Mrs Rogers of Birmingham, who was an- nounced to perform the opening ceremony. There was a good attendance on each day, and in the evening the hall was crowded until a late hour. The half-hourly entertainmews given by the Pierottes, under the management of NI iRS Greenall were excep- tionally well attended, and the finances of the bazaar were considerably assisted thereby. The concerts given were very interesting, and being sGmewhat of a novelty, attr leted crowded audi- ences. Another side show which proved an attrac- tion, was that of the " Living Pictures," represent- ing various suhjects, such as "The Better Land,' "Before and After Marriage,"" Mischievous Boys," &amp;c., and mach amusement was created by some of the representations, which were admirably carriffd out. There was also a Mariohette Show operated by Messrs Kelly Wilkins and Co., which was of .cnn- siderable interest to the youngsters. The bazaar all through was admirably managed. an 4 on Satur- day evening the various stalls presented a thin and deserted appearance. The Washing Competition, open to gentlemen only, was decided on Saturday evening, the judges being Mrs. Chandley, Mrs. R. Alderson, and Mrs. Stringer. and thekir awards were: —Mr.W H. Daleast ; and Mr. Laycock (Nut Grove) 2nd ; the prizes being given by the Sunlight Soap Company.—The result of the three days' sales was stated to be about £340, which was conSidered to be very satisfactory.</p>
        <p>Patt9rson moved, and Mr. Gilmour siconcled the adoption of the rninor"e which were approved. GAS AND WATER CONINIITTEE—REDUCTIO,N OF GAS BILLS.</p>
        <p>• The Gas and Water Committee reported that the Clerk was directed to write to the firms at present supplying the Gas Committee with coil and cannel, end state that the Gas Manager had complained that the fuel at present being suoplied wag not equal to sample, and to endeav- our to obtain an allowance for the inferior quality sup- plied of late.—Tee Gas Manager was directed in future to at once report to the Clerk to the Council all com- plaints of inferior articles or fuel, so that the matter might be taken iip w'thout delay.—A letter dated De- cember 29h,1893(which had been purposely held over), was read from rhe Davies Gas Stove Company, London, requesting to be allowed to hold a gas exhibition (stoves, cookers and cookery lectures) in the ' Town Hall during the coming Spring, they agreeing to pay all expeeses on condition that the Council let their steves out on hire during the exeiei- tion, and sanction theiti taking orders during the week. Wheroupon it was resolved that the foregoing applica- tion be acceded to.—The Chairmen brought forward tee evestion of the numerous complaints which ha i b?en made of the heavy gas accounts for the quarter ended December 31, 1898, as compared with the corresponding eparter of 1897, and suggested that the Committee might take the matter into consideration. After this had been done it WAS resolved that a reduction of 10 per cent, be allowed to every consumer of gas, such reduction to he based upon the gross amount of the accounts for the quirter ended 31st I:lcember list, and to be allowed from the accounts for the quarter ending on the 31st of March next. Automatic meters to be included in this resolutien ; and to be clearly understood that the reduc- tion is not a permanent one, and to be in respect of December quarter, 1898 only. The Assistant Surveyor rad a report noon the enlargement of the gas mains of the district necessary for a more adequate supply of gas, and submitted an estimate of the eoct thereof (including the sum of £1,650 decided upon at the last meeting of the c-mmittee for the purpose of laying a separate main to Messr.. McCorquodale's) amounting to a total sum of I£4 500. nd .t resolv.,d that the foregoing report be ap• proved and adopted. The Chairman stated that the Clerk suegrsted that the Council be recommended to apply to the Local Government Board for sanction to borrow the sum of £3,500, as a working capital for new meters, new mains, new services, and new street lamps, and explained that a similar sum had been expended during the past three years for these purposes out of re- venue, whereupon it was received that the Committee recommend the Council to apply to the Local Govern- ment Be-ri for sanction to borrow the sums of £4,500 and £3,500 above referred to, for the several pueposes named. It was also resolved that Mr. Berron, J•P., Mr. Collingwold, Mr. Stone, J.P:, and Mr. Ormend, be a snlo-nommi"eo for the purpose of considering and reduc- ing the number of applicants for the position of gas and water engineer, and to arrange ta call a suitable number before the Committee. The Chairmen moved, and Mr. Simpsot seconded the adoption of the minutes which were approved. HIGHWAYS AND SANITARY COMSIIrTEE.</p>
        <p>This C-marnittee in additio tl dealing with matters relative to the Medical Offl !er's report, which will be found in another column, recomnalndecl the adoption of the building plans of Peter Rigby, of Warrington, for an amendod building line of hack passage, Healel•street, and of Dr. Valentine, of Earlestown, for a house in flavdock-street. Matters relating tl Water•street were brought forward, and afterwards referred to a sub committeq, consisting nf Maesr3. Borr on, Collingwood; Simpson, Ormond and McMahon. The sub-comm.ittee appointed_ in that behalf</p>
        <p>reported that be Cannel ha re-lammended to a; once invite tenders for the making of back Market-stre t (from Stanles -street to Alpine-street), and that Haydeek, Athol, Albion, Crown, Tamworth. S. John, and Bank-</p>
        <p>tree te be proceeded with as soon as the -funds of the Council allowed. The sub-committv appointed in that beha,,f, reported that they had interviewed Mr. Pardey with reference to the laving of a sewer across .Newton Common, and stated that he had sugc,,ested that Wharf- road be al'ered to correspond with the proposed line of sewer, whereupon it was resolved that the sewer be laid as proposed, and that the question of the road being di. verted be considered when the proposed new bridge and the approaches thereto across the canal at Newton Com- mon are made. A letter, dated January 21st, was read from the Common Lookers. The time for dealing with the questions referred to therein was considered to be very inopportune, and it was therefore resolved that the letter be considered this day sir months. It was re- solved that the following tenders be accepted, viz. :- 15. Finning, Earlestown, catch pit, Golborne, Dale-road, £7 15s. ; James Smethurst and Son, Warrington, two manhole frames, £5; Ham, Baker ani Com- pany, Loncian, one syphon. tcr catch pit, £5 103. _ .</p>
        <p>The Sub-Committee appointed in that behalf .rsported that they had considered the estimat s for main roads far the year ending on the 31st March, 1900, and re- commended that application be made to the Lancashire County Council for the sum of £B2O for ordinary repair and maintenance, and the sam of £484 17s id for special work. Also tkat the Clerk write to the Lancashire Cointy Council asking them to transfer the whole of Market and Queen-streets, Etrlestown, from secondary roals to main roads. It WAS rebe'ved that the report be approved and adopts&amp; A letter, dath 9th February, 1899, arts also read from the Lancashire County Council</p>
        <p>statinz that the M tin R )ads awl Bridges Committee hid rPfueed to declare Market and Queen-street, Earlestown to be main r ltds. GENERAL PURPOSES COMIWITTEE.—. IMPROVED POSTAL FACILITIES. SUNDAY TRADING,</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Comtnir,tee reported that let'ers were read from the Secretary of the General Post Office dated Ist February, acceding to the application of the Council with respect to additional. collections and de-</p>
        <p>liveries of letters at Wargrave and Vuic in villages ; and dated 10:Lt February, stating that the sub-Poitrnaster is about to enlarge the alcommodatlon at Etrltistown Pt OfficA, by r.movinz his private bosiness, and devoting the whole of the shop to Post Office purposes, A letter, _ _</p>
        <p>'dated 3rd of February, was read from the Rsv James Allett, Preeid ent of the Free Church Council, eilling the a tention of the District Council to the practice of Sun- day trading at one of their shops on the Market, and re- questing a discontinuance of the practice. It was re- solved that in future the shops on the Market be not allowed to be open on Sondaye, nor Sunday trading of any kind whatever to be allowed on the Market Ground. The Clerk stated that tee report of Her Majesty's CommissirOners appointed to inquire into the subject of local taxation had been issued, and that they recom- in -eel to parliament that a new valuation authority seoeld be anpointed,consisting of members of the Couniy Couecil and county /boroughs, with power to appeint distrist committees from members of the Valuation Au- thority, the Urban District Councils. and Guardians. He further stated that the Royal Commission recommend that all rates (incluling Poor Rates), should be collected in boroughs by the B rough Councils, but in urban dis- tricts by the Guardians, whereupon it was resolved that the Clerk writO to the secretaries of the Urban Dietrist C noncils Association, and state that this Council are strongly of opinion that all rates in urban dithicts should, as in the case of borouehs, be collected by the Urban District Councils, and that the Association should support them in toeir endeavour to bring about this re- sult and that the Council be recommended to forward petitions to Parlia.Ment against the two bills of the National Telephone Company, now before the House of Commons. It was also resolved that the Sub-Committee apprinted on the 21st of November, 108, consisting of Mr. Borreio, Mr. Collingwood, Mr. Stone, and the Clerk, be re-appointed for the purpose of taking sueh steps, eith,r by interviewing the Directors of the L. and NW. Railway Co., or otherwise as they may deem advisable, for the purrs. of obtaining better and more ad 'qate passeeger station accommodation at Earlestown and that the Clerk write to Mr. Shaw, District Inspector of the L. and N.W. Railway Company, at Liverpool. and .draw his attention to the anomaly at present existing with reference to cheap tickets to Liverpool from New- ton and Earlestown ; ani also endeavour to obtain in- creaced train service from E triestown to Manchester and Liverpool, particularly before mid-day. The minutes were approved on the proposition of the Chairman. APPOINTMENT OF A GAS AND WATER ENGINEER.</p>
        <p>. At a meeting of the Gas and Water Committee held (..n M nday it was decided to recommend the appointment of Mr. D. H. Lloyd, of Wallasey, as gas ad water en- gineer, a': a salary of £l5O per annum. The appointment was u..animously agreed to. THE OVERSEERS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman moved "That Mr. James Sankey, Mr. John Simpson, Dr, J. N. Patterson, and Mr. William Beatty, all of Newton-in-Makerfield, he, and they are hereby appointed Overseers of tie Poor for the Parish of Newton-in-Makerfield for the year commencing April Ist, 1899, and ending 31st March, 1900 ; and tha: the Common Seal of the Council be affixed to the appoint- ment."</p>
        <p>Mr. Thompsnn seconded, and the motion was unani- mously agreed to.</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR BALL</p>
        <p>The Chairman road a letter fr•,m Mr. P. E. Walsh acknowledging the vote of condolence with the relatives of the late Mr. Ball. This was all the business.</p>
        <p>NEWTON MEDICAL OFFICER OF HEALTH'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>STRONG COMMENTS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Newton Urban District Council on Monday, the annual report for the year ended 31st Dec. of the MeJical Officer of Health (Dr. Watkins) was sub- mitted.</p>
        <p>AIBTIf AND DEATH RATE.</p>
        <p>Dr. Watkins states that there were registered 536 births and 232 deaths, giving an excess of births of 304. The birth-rate was 363 and the death-rate 156 per thousand. The infant death-rate (under one year of age) was 1567 For 100 births registered. The deaths if children (under five years of age) were 46 01 per cent. of the total number of deaths. These figures, he re- grettel to say, continued abnormally high. The deaths (22) tram the principal zymotic diseases were 1.4 per 1,000 of the estimated population. Diarrhoea and dysentery ware certifiel as the causes of 16 of thess deaths(l4 bslow 5 years and 2 ab we),and as llout of the 16 occurred in the third quarter of the year, they might probably be attributab,e chiefly to climatic conciiions. The deaths (17) certifiei as due to phthisis (1 below and 16 above 5 years of age); indicated that an available sanatlrium fr tuberculons diseases—the great national proj,ct of the day—would be a boon and a blessing. Of the 37 d.atlis fram pulmonary diseases other than phthisis 23 occurred in the first and last quarters (11. in the former and 12 in the latter.)</p>
        <p>Lord Newton's agent, was most careful in seeing that it was not deplsited where there was any likelihood of it affecting streams. With reference to tee infantile death rate it seemed to him really acc arida' that it should be as high as it.was in the distgict. He hved that the calling of the public attention to it would be sufficient to all whom it cince:ned. Mr. Gilmour said the Medical Officer of Health told them that street-making was the cause of the high death rate. Out of 55 cases they had 23 deaths in Town Flall Ward. He would like to ask the sub•comfnittee when they were going to make the streets in this ward. He would also like ti know whether the Council had adopted the Public Health Act of 1890, because if they had not thay could not enforce the making of back pas- sages.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ormand said he would like to know how Mr. Gil- mour had arrived at his figures. The Medical Officer stated that only one died out of 55 infectious cases. Mr. Gdmour said he was glad of the correction. The report, however, stated that out of 55 cases, 23 were from Town Hall Ward.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he was glad that the Chairman o the Highways and Sanitary Committee, and Mr Stone ha I referred to the Medica Officer's report. If it had hem allowed to go forth without comment people vv.-mid have thought they were in a miserable state and In an unhealthy condition in the neighbourhood. The facts which came befhre them from other distric's did not bear this out. The Newton district was healthier than most districts in the county. Mr Gilmour : Except in Town Hall Ward. _ _</p>
        <p>The Chtirman sail their death rate for the previous twelve months nas 15 6 whereas the average of the County Urban districts was 161. In three contiguous urban districts he found that in one distriat they were 20 per cent higher; in another 39 Par cent and in an- other 39 per cent and in another 9 per cent. In New- ton they had during the year improved their pcsition by 16 prr cent, So that the Council would see that in place of it being in a condition that was alarming they found from facts before them that it was a healthy district. The medical alines report should therefore not go forth without being considerably watered down. The I deposit heaps were situated in a semi-agricultural dis- trict. The course adopted was one of economy and one of the best for getting rid of what had been a nuieance since the world began. When asked to point t 3 any die trict likely to suffer by them the Medical Officer men- tioned Vista-read, and when asked if any of the persons nad suffered in that neighbourhood, the Medical Officer said the wind didn't blow that way. Lord Newton, through his agent, Mr. Pa,rdy had taken etre that they did not create any nuisance. They had had hard and fast agreements so that the drainage should not be taken into the streams or where it might be deleterious to the health of the inh ibltaate. If the Local Government Board aeked for explanations the Council would be able to rneet'thern in a eatilifactbry way. The report wae then advted, Mr. Gilmour only dis- senting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>SINGULAR ESCAPADE.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens Police Court on Tuesday, Frank Lawny a young man who said he came from South Shields, but had worked at various p'aces in St. Helens, was charged with being on the premises of Mr J H Nickson, figa salegman, Market•place, fir. an unlawful purnose. Mr. Nickson etated that about ten minutes past ten o'clock on the previous night, just after he had retired for the night, he heard a noise at the bedroom window. He looked and saw prisoner on the window Fill. Prisoner asked to be let in the room, as he was falling. He (wit- ness), however, went downstairs and called the nolice The prisoner had then got off the window sill. Police- constable Monteith deposed that he saw the trlgllVl' leaving a house close by, wearing his stockings, withent boots or clogs, and he arrested him. Prisoner's clogs wet-) found in a washhouse at the back of Mr Nickson's premises. Prisoner said he had been drinking heavily since Saturday, and did not know what he had been doing. Ho was" off his head."—The Bench committed him to prison for 28 days. THE COLLIERY FRIENDLY SOCIETY ACTION.</p>
        <p>The adjourned case in which the White Cross Colliery Sick, Burial, and Dividing Society claimed £ll from Thomas Lilley,of 4,Greenhough•street,their late treasurer again c ame on for hearing at the St. Helens Count Court on Wednesia.y afternoon, tlefon3 Ms Honour Judge Collier. Mr. R. W. H. Thomas appearei for the claim- ants, and Mr. H. L. Riley for the defeorlants.—lt ap peared that the amount claimed was the balance in hand at the last audit, but defendant; it was stated, had handed the money over to the then secretary, a man named John Penningtors, who had since left the town. The plaintiffs contended that the clefendantihad handed the money over to Penningtnn contrary to the rules, and claimed the amount from him.—For the defence it was submitted that the form in which the action was en- tered was wrong and irregular.and till Judge ultimately suggested that the proper course would be for the p-esent ananmens to be withdrawn, and a general meeting of the members called to sanction the proceedings.—The case was withdrawn on those grounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>COTTAGE WINDOW GARDENING.</head>
        <p>COTTAGE WINDOW GARDENING.</p>
        <p>MR. JOHN CROSFIELD'S SCHEME.</p>
        <p>It is now four years since Mr. John Crosfield of Walton Lea made arrangements for the distribution of Hyacinth bulbs to any of the working people who s',ould apply far them. On Saturdar the result of the care and attention bestowed on the 1,500 bulb; distributed in Oltnber last —were shown in the Lecture Room of ths Mu,eam. Each exhibitor received ..three bulba.—red, white and blue—and they were invited to show them in pots or in glasses. Four groups of exhibits were arranged for namely two plants grown in rots—two plants in glastPs —one plant in either pot or glass—which gave each one two chances to ob'ain a prize. In all there were 28 prizes in money offered -ranging frGrn 12s to is aec•rding to success ill growth of spike, cleanness, and neatness of the exhibit.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Ryan and Mr. J. Fulton Smith had been entrusted with the arrangements for the carrying out of thie scheme of Councillor Cro:4field's to encourage a taste for flowers and an interest in their culture. The aisp!ay on Saturday was in all respects highly creditable. and showed a very marked improvement over all pravions competitions. The p*ize-takers deservel their a waris. Mr. Kipp, head gar lener at Walton Lea. who viiitel the show, expressed himself very much satisfied with the selections made in an the grouna. A. large numher of petple availed themselves of the opportunity to see th, exhibition after the judging had taken place, and it must be some satisfaction to Mr. Cresfield to learn drat the intelligent criticism of several of the visitors as they compared the points of oceellence which gained the prizes, was taken to heart by some who have resolved to " score " next year. The supolv of bulbs is estimated to have been distributed to 500 separate families and it may be premi=el that much pleasure and no little instruction has been given during the winter months by these beautiful flocre.s. Mr. Ryan deserges great praise for the unostentatious but care- fully prepared arrangements.-first in the distribution of the bulbs in October and in the highly successful re- sults of his labours as were seen last Saturday.</p>
        <p>The following were the successful competitors out of a tntal of 250 :</p>
        <p>Two HYACINTHS IN POTS :-1.2t prize, A Roylance 12s; 2nd prize, ThOMAS Worthington Clarke MI ; 3r4 priz., Mrs Millington, 8s ; 4th prize, Mrs G Firsmpsol, 6.4 ' • sth prize, Miss Sarah Douzhty, 4. ; 6th oriz Mrs If Wal- lington, 2s; 7th prize, Mr W Massey Is; CertificVes of Merit. WHL Shalreshaft, J Unsworth, Tnornas T sylor and Frank M Davies. Two HYACINTHS IN GLASSES :-Ist prizs, Thomas Me- Whirter, 12s ; 2nd prize, Mrs Peers, 10s ' • 311 p.z,.. John Clough, 8a; 4th priz., Mrs C Caterall, 6s ; 511 or;z Sarnuel Gerrard, 4s ; 6:h priz J T P.da'ay. 2a; 7h prize W Walker, is. Certificates of Merit,Mr3 Harding, Joseph Moss, and Miss Annie Plevin. ONE HYACINTH IN A POT :-ISt prizs, Miss SATIJI Doughty, 64 ; 2nd prize, Mrs Eiough, 5 : ; 3-d prizs, Johl Oakes, 4s ; 4th prize, Harry Odle, 33 ; s:h priz., Ms Clarke, 21 ; 6th prize, Thomas Thornlev,ls. Certificates of Merit, Thomas Worthingt n, Mrs Craddock and Mrs Starkey. ONE HYACINTH IN A GLASS :—.lSt Mrs Coe- rill, 6a ; 2nd peze, George WP.tkin, 5s ; 3rd prize, W, Itaircloug,n, 4s ; 4th prize, W lkoberts, ; s:h pr ze Mrs M Forbes aa; 6th prize,Thamas Catt.r 11,l Corzifi- estes of Merit, Gorge Illgby, A Jo 's an.l Miss P Ccok.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT ORDER OF ODDFBILLOWS,</p>
        <p>LOYAL OLIVE BRANCH EA,DOE</p>
        <p>At the usual fortnightly meeting of the above Lodge, held last Saturday evening, P.G. Henry Walker, was the recipient of a nicely framed mat officer's certificate, presented to bim by the officers and members of the lodge, for past 9krvices. Amongst those present were :—P.P.D.G.NI. Norman, (treasurer), P. P.G. NI. Thomas Rigby, P. P.G. M. Alban Boyle (secretary), P.G. Walker, N.G.' Ellis, Rclare, V.G. W, Watkins, P.V. Bradshaw, arid others P.P.G.M. Rigby having been catled upon by the N.G. to make the presentation, in the (Rasurd6 of his remarks said it gave him great pleasure to present P.G. Walker with that framed past officer's certifi- cate. It must be gratifying to any member of the Lodge to find that he had given satisfaction during his term of office, and he understoid that P.G. Walker had undertaken the duties of P.G. Turnover. He hoped the recipient would look upon the pre- sentation, not in the light of recompense for his services, but as a small token of appreciation; and accept it in the spirit in which the Lodge desired to present it. P.G. Walker said that he was glad that his efforts on behalf of the Lodge had given satisfaction. He had often de- sired to earn such a beautiful certificate as that presented to him that night, and he made up his mind to work hard for his lodge and try and merit the appreciation of the officers and members, and he was pleased to find he had done so. That presentation would always bring back to his memory very pleasant recollections and he hoped it ' would be an incentive to many of his friends to join the good work which was being done by the Olive Brunch Lodge and similar lodges in the town. The meeting was closed in the usual manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>_ WAR RINCi PON LAF, it.a:s.x.Y AND DISPENSARY.</head>
        <p>_ WAR RINCi PON LAF, it.a:s.x.Y AND DISPENSARY.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING OF WORKS REPRESENTA- TIVES.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon, the annual meeting of the repres-ntatives of works from which centributtons have been sent during the year to the Infirmary and. Diapen- sary, took place at the Town Hell. In the unavoidable absence of Mr. J. C. Parr (the president), Mr. John Feirclough presided, and there were also present :—Drs. Lenesdale, Fox and White. and Messrs. F. Monks, J. L. Tunstall, J. Fulton Smith, G. To/meson. Councillor J. Roberts. D Fanshaw, Thomas Burton (treasurer), and Joseph Taylor (secretary). The financial statement showed that the cash handed to the Treasurer of the Infirmary was £6BB ss. 31, against £624 10s. 9d. last year. Councillor Jong ROBERTS, in moving the adoption of the finaneal statement, said there was an increase of £64 over last year. He thought it was a very haedsome balance sheet indeed. The first subeeription was in 1867, and amounted to 12; 6i,and the increase year after year preyed that the Workingmen's Committee was doing very good werk.The committee was net in existence until 1880. In 1889 the subscription was £3 10; 6 ',and in 1892 ;t amounted to £42- It Ml in 1893 to £34 7s. The Roe- pital Saturday collection commenced in 1874, and con- tinuer] until 1879. The money so collected amounted to £734 is Bd. As stated, the workingmen's Infirmary Committee commenced in 1880,and the first subscriptioa amounted to £165 19s 41, from which it had risen up to the present time to over £6BB. The total amount con- tributed was £8,348 lOs Bd. Every bit of the money, , however, was wanted, aed he coe'd assure them that it was well spent. Be hoped that in time they would be able to raise £l,OOO. Mr. FANSTIAW seconded the =orlon. It was very im- portant that the vrork of the institution should be con- tinually brought brfore the people. Councillor BURTON in supporting the motion said if they could only get a little help from the] principals of the large works in the town who were not at present supportine the institution,they would soon get the £lOOO. He did net wish them to subscribe personally, for th,A they ceuld hardly call it a wzrkingrnea's committen,but If they would use their influence amongst their eamloye; it would be a good thing. The CHAIRMAN read letters of apology for non-attend- ance from several gentlemen. fie added that he con- sidered the amount contributed a very handsome one, and was pleased to say that generally, 38 would be Istated at the annual meeting, the income for the year bad exeeedsd the expenditure by almost £2OO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>ELECTIONS</head>
        <p>ELECTIONS</p>
        <p>It was decided that the deiegates present should form a general committee,with power to add to their number. The following Esocutivn Cmomittee was appointed: —Messrs John Roberts, D Fanshaw, Thomas Burton, Joseph Taylor, J P Taylor, George lb.c..!ge' W Crosskill, J J Attierton,P Atherton, CI Simnroo, E Gritliths,VWads- worth, C H Hibbert. J F Waring,o M Watkins, A Basks, T Ince, W Gleave, T Corbett, W Daintoth, N Emmerson, T J Bennie, W Todd, John Harrison, W Jhckson, John Wilkinson, John Hartley, Thomas Rigby, and John Berry. Councillor J Roberts was re-elected chairman of ths committee ; Mr. David Fanshaw vice-chairman ; Coun- cillor Barton treasurer; and Mr. Joseph Taylor secre- tary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>VOTES OF TH&amp;NICS.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF TH&amp;NICS.</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks were passed to the Senior ani Junior House Surgeons an the Honorary lktedios4 Officers ; to the Matron and Nurses; t, the Chief Constable aril police, to the Cenimittee of the Warrington Foothill Club, and tl M. Fairelough for presiding over the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CliU KC/ tibS.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CliU KC/ tibS.</p>
        <p>BUCKLEY S FREE r P.S. A .</p>
        <p>On Sunday, --at the above place of worship, the monthly open meeting and half yearly distribltion of prizes took place. There was a larle conzregation of members and friends. Mr. Clegge (secret try of the Y.M.C.A. P.S.A.) presided, and after a shark address introduced the speaker, Mr Hall, of Liverpool, who gave an excellent address. Miss P. Shaw was heard to ad7an- tare in" The Holy City," as was Miss A Rigby in " The Children's Home" with violin obligato. Mr. A. Bate rendered two violin solos, "0 Rest in the Lord" and Intermezzo from " Ca.valleria Rusticana," with ability. Mr. Clegge afterwards presented the priz,s. Mr. Fair- hurst (president) announced tbat 45 new members hid been enrolled.</p>
        <p>BEWSEY-ROAD WESLEYAN CHURCH. On Sunday morning and evenihg, the annual trust eermcns were preached by the Rev. George Harbottie Special anthems were rendered by the choir, and in addition a solo. "There is a green hill far away," was contributed with much expression by Mrs. D. Stone. On Monday evening the Rev. S. G. Walker, one of the younger ministers of the Methodist Church. gave a lecture upon "Some social problems," emohasizinz the phrase now so much in use. " What wouli Jesul do ?" He alladed to his social work in Liverpool, and said that on a Sunday afternoon upwards of a thousand men att, n- ded his lectures on such questi-ms as the housing of the ',working clasys, St.% At the conclusion Mr. Walker was warmly thanked for his se-vices. CHRISTADELPHI t.N ROOM.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening•Mr S A Garside,of Ormskirk, gave an address on" The decay of the Turkioh Empire—an indication that the return of Christ is drawing near." He said that for Christian people to profess subjection to the Bible and admit that Christ might return to the earth was not, uncommon, but they grew impatient if that evAnt were associated with political events, and accused them of strange views arid queer notions on re- ligious matters, though the Bible was full of the affairs of nations. It dealt with natisns and people, and par- ticularly with Jews and their land on which the Turkish Power now stood. That land had had an extraordinary past and would have a elorious future,and the promises made to Abraham necessitated the return of Christ and Abraham to the land of Palestine. After tracing the History of the Jews to the time of Christ's appearing, which appearing confirmed the promises made to Abra- ham, Mr Garside gave particulars of the conflicts waged on account of Jerusalem, and obierved that the land was not yet done with, for they had the assurance that God would rule the world by His Son: In symbolic language they were told when to expect this event. Symbols were used in some pavan to-day which they all understood, as the lion for England, the bear for Russia, &amp;c., and the symbols used in the Bible were equally plain it they were studied. The river Euphrates stood for the Turkish Empir,. Students of the Bible expected 100 years ago that Turkey was doomed to fall from her then great position, and tl-day her downfall was expected throughout the world. WARRINGToN MARKETS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Corn Market on Wednesday new wheat (white) fetched 3e 10d to 3s lid (701 b to the bushel) I • red wheat, 3s 9d to 38 10d 9 • new oats, 2s 3d to 2i 4d. Flour best seconds. per 2401 b., 183 6d;Bran, Hid to is 3d per 201 b. Prices ruled as follow in the provision markets :—Fresh butter is 2d and Id 4i per lb; fresh eggs, 13 for is ; fowls, 4s 6d to 5s per couple; ducks, 6i Od ; English beef, 5d to 10d; mutton, 6d to 94; Teal, 7d to lid; pork, 6d to Bd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>INFECTIOUS SICVNESS.</head>
        <p>INFECTIOUS SICVNESS.</p>
        <p>Fifty-die cases of infectious sickness were notified to him fifteen below, and f orty ab)ve 5 years of age—and were, thus classified ;—Scarlatina 33 (13 under and 23 above 5 years of age) ; enteric or typhoid fever 7 (ill above 5 years of age); puerperal fever 1; erysipelas 11(2 below and 9 above 5 years of age.) Tao Cases were then distributed in the following Wards :—Viaduct,l3; Town Hall, 23; Wargrave, 8; Crow-lane, 4; Newton, 7 ; totals, 55.—Twenty-3ix of these cases were removed to the Iso'a•ion Fiospital at Warrington, and one child (under five years of age) died there. Dlsinfectints were freely supplied to all infected houses during sickness,and further disinfection (by sulphur fumigation and carbolic aciLi), was carried out on recovery, or after death, of the patients. The townshirf was under his constant pur- view, and recently he had made a more particular inspection accompanied in p trt by the Inspector of Nuisances. Notes had been made and promises of aten- tipn to uos ttisfactory conditions roceived from persons responsible for them.</p>
        <p>_ REMOVAL OF EXCREMENTITIOUS MATTER-" NIGRTSOIL."</p>
        <p>The pres,in.; question just now is to find places where to put the material. He had pointed out before the evil of the multi olication of places of deposit, and the evil was progressively increasing. He would ask who amongst the members of the Uouncit-possessing land that in the nesr future might possibly be desirable for build- ing sites, would be so short-sighted as to let it be appro- priated almost entirely for its conversion into nuisance depots. rrws difficulty need I imperatively to be faced sooner or later, and if later, it would probably have to be dealt with under pressure from without, and at a great expenditure of money—some of which mieht be saved by the anticipation of such an evil day, and the timely adoption of such practical and efficient measures as time would most surely prove to be absolutely neces- sary and unavoidable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>SEWAGE-THE POLLUTION OF NEWTON LAKE.</head>
        <p>SEWAGE-THE POLLUTION OF NEWTON LAKE.</p>
        <p>He bracketed these because they had both been remntly subjected to the Inspection of the County Medical Officer of Flealth,and he could not do better than avail himself or his permission to copy the report on the subject—the result of his recent visits to Newton— addressed to the Public Health Committee of the County Council on the 19th J nuary, which read as follows "I b '57, to place before you my report of inopection made on the 8:h ult. (Bth December, 1898). with respect to the polluted condition of Newton Mere. In the hot weather, I am informed that offensive smells arise, owing to the putrefaction of sewage matters brought down by the Millingford Bro,k from Golberne and Ashtln-in-M-iker- field.. To a slight extent the Urban District of Newton- in-Makerfield also pollutes the Lake, and solid pollution oats into it when the reservoirs of Golhorne Mills are s'ludged. At the time of my visit the effluent from the Golborne So wage Works was not satisfactory, and the condition of the tanks called for more careful attention.</p>
        <p>The wage of Ashton-in-Makerfield was being treated more or less efficiently on farm land, but a large amlnnt of crude sewage, equal to a daily flow of about 50,000 gallons was passing directly into the brook on the east side of Heath-lane Bridge, thus causiug a serious don which ought to be discontinued. Oa the 22 id ult. (22 December, 1898),I made an inspection of the Newton Sewage Works, and my report shows that at the West Outfall Work.. where about 50,000 gallons are daily re- ceived, there is an imperfect treatment of the sewage. In order to secure good results these works ought to be re-modelled, and further provision made for filtration. At the Central Sewage Works the treatment was equally unsatisfactory, the effluent being allowed to leave the works in a putrid condition aft r passing through subsi- dence tanks. I may point out that these work; have Hot the ordinary requirements for rendering sewage treatment in any way efficient."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WATER SUPPLY</head>
        <p>WATER SUPPLY</p>
        <p>continued to be good in quality but not uniformly satis- factory as regarded quantity and amount of pressure, thus the streets and sswers were not sufficiently (as re garcled frequency and amount used) gushed and waterei last summer. It was greatly to be hopnd that the acti- vity in boring for a larger supply would soon be at- tended with success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>STREET-MAYING</head>
        <p>STREET-MAYING</p>
        <p>proceeded too slowly as must be well-known to the Council. He believed that the greater incidence of in- fectious disease (alone) in the Town Hall Ward wee mainly attributable to the non-completion of its streets, paving, and sewerage. The ward was not the most populous, nor were the houses, generally speaking, so closely situated, and yet 15 out of 36 cases of scarlatina, and 23 out of 55 (the total number of infectious cases notified) occurred in this ward. The infamous condition remains in statu quo of Athol-strelt, and he would par- ticularise the extensions (west) of Haydock, Grafton, rainworth, and Jolla-streets. A large area west of the two last-named streets and of Crown-street were now no better than a huge" tip." SLAUGHTF.R-HOUSE3</p>
        <p>were inspected, but he suspected the bye-laws relating to tbam were not rigidly carried into practice. He re- cently inspected the largest of theta just after the kill- ing ot a beast. and he was of opinion that sufficient care was not taken to keep the blood eut of the drain. VITAL STATISTICS OF 1898.</p>
        <p>The following arB the rates for England and Win. 1898 :—Birth rate, 294 par 1,000 living ; death rate,l7 per 1,080 living; zyrnotie rate, 2.22 per 1,000 living ; infant mortality, 161 per 1,000 births. The Highways and Stnitary Committee ordered that</p>
        <p>the Clerk write t) the Golborne Urban District Council remindina them that the pollution of Millingford.tirauk</p>
        <p>atilt continued, and coesequently the pollution of New- ton Lake. A letter, dated 31st January, 1899, was read from the Lancashire County Council, stating the Cuntx Medical Officer of Health bad reported upon the condition of Newton Lake, and that upon the occasion of his visit to the Urban District Ocsuncil'.3 Sew- age Works, sewage was being imperfectly treated, and the effluent allowed to leave in a putrid condition. The Clerk was directed to reply thereto, and state that the Council had under consideration the best means of deal- ing with sewage, and that the effluent was at present turned into Senkey Brook, into which a very large amount of chemical refuse was discharged, which of it- elf in the opinion of the Council, was sufficient to de- odorise and ceunteract any ill affects of any sewage which might be turned therein. Mr. W. Clllingwood, in moving the adoption of the report and the minutes of the Highways Sec. Committee, said he wished to draw attention to the Medical Officer's report. He thought it only right to make a few obser- vations as it was likely to mislead persons if allowed to go to the public; -without remark. With reference to the removal of excrementitieus matter, Mr. Coiling wood said there was no difficulty with respect to it. The Council had just completed satisfactory arrangements with one of the farmers in the district for taking it. The ground was not likely to be used for building pur- poses. It was well known that there were very large areas near the district which would be available for the disposal ef night soil without in any way being injuri- ous to the health of the district. Their death rate was still very low and the report on that head he thought was highly Fain factory. With regard to street-making, the Medical Officer said they were proc ealing They were quite well aware of that. Perhaps the public did not know, nut their application for a loan bad been refused by the Local Government Bard on account of their area for dealing with sewage outfall being inadequate. Knowing that experiments were tried in many places to deal with sewage in a more modern fashion the Council thought it would be better to await the results of those experiments before taking up a lot of extra land and spending an enormous amount of money in porpetuatine a system whieh so far had been found un.uitable. He might say that the most recent reports which bad come under his notice in connection with the bacteriological method of dealing with sewage showed that that was the system of the future. At Blackburn the rsults had been most remarkable, and he hoped that within a very few years they would be able with a small ex- penditure to adopt that system, and then the Local Government Board would probably be able to see their way to give the Council borrowing powers to raise a loan for the completion of their streeti, although he could not see the connection between the two. He did not see why they 'mould be stopped from making their streets because the outfall was insufficient. It had been stiegetted that toe Coun- cil should approach the Local Government Board again. He hoped that if they did s; the Lccel Governmen Board would be able to see their way to alter their pro viola decision. Mr. R. Stone thought the report of the Medical Officer of Health was calculated to create alarm in the minds of the inhabitants of the district. He would like to add that this excrementitious matter was deposited far away from where any building was likely to take place, The excremeutitieus matter was not deposited in a heap but mixed by the farmers and ploughed in the land. T:la matter deposited was simply dry rubbish, and great care was taken that it was covered over with soil. Mr Pardy,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>OLD FOLK'S TREAT AT THE PUBLIC HALL.</p>
        <p>A PLEASANT EVENING WIT IT THE AGED.</p>
        <p>The aanual treat to the aged poor of Runcorn and district was given on Wednesday at the Public Hall, Runcorn. As ulual, it took the fora' of a bountitul tea followed by an' enterrainm Ant, and the wile passed off with. e.atire satisfaction.</p>
        <p>The tea was laid out in the Public Hall, and at about 5.30, some 400 persons, whose ages ranged from 59 to 90 years,sat down and partook of a well- served meal. The appearance of the room was pleasant, the tasteful dec-irations setting off to per- fection the refleetive and happy expressions visible on all sides, giving one a picture of ripened age mellowed with passing pleasure. that should live in the memory of all who beheld it fir many a day. The tea was good sari everything that accompanied it was of the best, and we understand that the whole was subscribed for or given gratis by the ladies and gentlemen of the neighbourhood. The tables were presided over by the following ladies, who were ably assisted by a staff of young ladies and gentlemen who worked with a delight that added greatly to the comfort of tl-..-e aged .—M.is3 Clara Taylor, Mrs Broekleharst ; Mrs llailvyood, Mrs Wilson; Mrs T Handley, Miss Ryder; Mrs J W Lightbura, Mrs Whitaker; Mrs J El West m. Mrs T [each; Mrs Baker, Miss Stubbs; Mrs A It Norman, Mrs Percival; Mrs Turner, Mrs W B Say; Mrs Wiswall, Mrs Allen; Mrs T Cooke, Mrs IV Cooke ; Mrs J M Frith, Mrs W Shaw (sent.); Mrs T C Williams, Mrs P Leathwood ; Mrs W Timmins, Mrs G Bellamy; Miss Savage, Miss Riley (Soapery); Miss A Riley, Miss A Taylor; Mrs J D Jones, Miss Woodland; Mrs F J Norman; Miss ' Coward; Mrs R Moore, Miss Millington ; Mrs A Timmins, Mrs Richardson; Mrs T Wood, Mis.. D Littlemore ; Mrs 'W Shaw (junr.), Miss G Howard, Mrs G Howard; Miss Bowyer, Mrs W Leathwood ; Mrs S Crosby, Mrs Coventry; Miss Satage, Mrs Posnett ' • Mrs R Shaw • Mrs Mayston, Mrs lmison ; Mrs J RFrith ; Miss Norman; Mrs W At the evening entertainment, which consisted of a nigger minstrel at fresco by the • Widnee Borough Minstrels (who kindly gave their services gratis), Mr. Samuel Taylor, J.P., presided, and was supported on the natforrn by Messrs. E. Imison, C.C. (treasurer), W. Howard (secretary), A. R. Norman, J.P., F. J. Norman, J.P., W. Shaw, and I.Bowyer. Mr. S. Taylor, who was received with applause, said the time had come round ones again for the annual entertainneent,and he gave all a hearty welcorne.He was quite sure he was speaking for every lady and gentleman who had taken part in getting up the tea for the after- noon, and also the entertainment that evening, when he said they were all delighted to see so many of the aged people present. He noticed in looking round that most of those present had been present at those annual gatherings for many years. From what he knew of the committees, that had been at work assiduously for many weeks holding a large number of meetings each -week, it was as it always hal been a pleasure to themn to provide the tea which they had participated in. He could also speak for the ladies and of the pleasant way and the amount A energy they lead thrown into their efforts in cutting up and providing the tea. As well as to the ladies and gentlemen and the committee their thanks were due to their kind friends from Widnes who had come forward to give their cervices for their enter- tainment. (Applause.) He understood there were a few who were with them last year, who were absent, but he was glad to hear they were very few, still a small percentage had been taken to their long home. However, he thought they had more present that night than they hal last year. (Applause). He would assure them, from what he knew,there was a growing feeling amongst both subscribers of food and meney as well as those people who had taken so much interest and devoted so much time in get:ing up the annual tea and entertain- ment,that there was not much fear of it falling through. (Loud applause.) It was a duty, and it was looked upon in the most serious form as the duty of the Chairman of the Urban District Council. to take the leading part in that annual gathering. He could assure them it had been an extreme pleasure to hits during his term of °Mee for the last two years ; and he was sure that who- ever followed him would have a very warm five minutes if he did not take _ a deep interest</p>
        <p>in the gathering. He would not take up much of their time with a long speech, as there those there who would entertain them. There was a lady—an aged lady, Mrs Billingtorh—who was going to give them a song first. (Applause.). He had great pleasure in introducing her to them. Mrs Billington then came forward and sang" The roll call " in a remarkably clear voice, the whole of the audience going in the refrain. The following programme was the proceeded with:— Part 1: Opening chorus. "Jingle Belle," the Troupe ; coolie song. "Susan Browa," Mr Clarles Locke"; song, "Driven from Home," Master Teflon; comic song. "Ta-ra-rum," Mr J Edwards; song,"Volunte.er Organist," Mr P Chadwick; comic song,"Such an Heddicated Gal" Mr G R Jones; song," the Pardon came too late,"M3,ster Rutter, calor°, "I love somehody's wife"; comic song, "Meet Till at the Golden Gate," Mr J Ryan; song,"When She's just about too full," selected, Mr S Pope; comic song," Why did I leave my little back room"Mr Johnson; eomic song "Razors are fleing in the Air,"Mr G Outrarn; humorous ;ketch, " Doctor's Shop," by Messrs Rawlins and Ryan, and members of troupe; a few words by Charles Locke. The sketch" Black Justice," Was highly appreciated. The characters were, Judge I'll fix him, Mr C Locke; P.C. Jackson, Mr J Ryan ; Magistrates' Clerk, Mr S Rawlins ; prisoners, merahsre of the troupe. The principals of the troupe %inn :—Pianist, Mr J Hart ; interlocutor, Mr S Rawlins ; c inductor, Mr 8 Pope ; bones, Mr J Ryan, Mr J Ea wards, Mr G Outram. Tam- bos, Mr C Locke, Mr G R Jules, Mr C Johnson. The piano used at the entertainment was kindly lent by Mr W Shaw, jun., Tep Locks' Runcorn. During the intery il Mr. Peacock, on behalf of his aged friends, said he felt he had a very pleasing duty t 3 per- form. He was there to move a vote of thanks to those kind ladies and gentlemen who had given them such a handsome and bountiful tea. (Applause-) He hoped that every one of the" boys and girls " who had partaken ef that bounty would show their appreciation in a hearty round of applause. (Loud applause). Mr. Powell (another aged friend) seconded the vote, and hoped that the ladies and gentlemen would be long spared to live and be happy. (Loud applause). The vote was heartily accorded and responded ta by Mr. S. Taylor, who expressed the great pleasure all had felt at the several parts they had taken in the move- ment. (Applause.), fie took that opportunity of pro- postag a vote of thanks to the friends from Widnes for so kindly coming forward that evening. It was not the first time they had received help from Widnes in various good (muses, and he hoped it would not be the last. (Applause). Mr. Taylor having conveyed the thanks of the gather- ing to the troupe, Mr. Pape responded and said, on behalf of the troupe, if the audience were as pleased with them as the troupe was with the audience they would get on very well. (Loud. applause.) A vote of thanks to the Chairman proposed by Mr. E. Imison, and seconded by Mr. J. W. Lightburn, and the singing of the National Anthem brought the proceedings to a close. - . -</p>
        <p>The following were the several cimmitte.es which were responsible for the great success achieved :—General Committee: Messrs S Taylor (chairman), E !mist-in (treasurer), W Howard (secre:ary), J-iseph Nall (assis- tant secretary), F J Norman, TWo J W Woodland, G Bellamy, R H Posnett, F Wiswall, J E Mere- dith, W Collier, H Hakim, Dr. Ormsby, J B Chadwick, Revs R J Clifton' C W L Christian, H B Gore, Canon A M Wood, H B Firth, W Scholes, Mes•rs Thomas Cooke, W Byfield, W E Dudley, T C Williams, and the members of the District Council. St )res Com- mittee: Messrs M Sweetman, H Hughes and W Byfield. Scrutiny Committee : Messrs I' B3vvyer,T C Williams, P Leathwood, S Littler, M Sweecman and T Cook. Tea and Provision Committee: Messrs T C Williams, J W Woodland, T Riley, J floxworth, E Gatley, J Hayes, W Byfield, and W Shaw. Hall Committee : Messrs T Riley; T C Williams, W Shaw, %V Byfield, flox- worth, E Gatley, D Littemore, J Hayes, and J W Wood- land. Cab Committee : Messrs Tic Williams, G Riley, D Litttlemore, Parry and S Littler. Entertainment Com- mittee : Messrs W B Kay, F L Scott, W Millington, W T Crosby, ad W Cooke.</p>
