        <p>THE LIEFIA EPIDE. IC. An Added Danger. When from any cause—bodily fatigue, mental over- strain, loss of appetite, etc.—vitality is lowered and health impaired, the danger of an attack of Influenza is increased tenfold. An occasional cup of hot Bovril, between meals and at night, affords the necessary sus- tenance, stimulation, and natural body-warmth to resist the impending attack. When the well-known symptoms of Influenza develop and the patient's appetite fails, it is a popular fallacy to resort to Beef-Tea as a source of nourishment; whereas it has been proved conclusively that Beef Tea merely acts as a stimulant, pcssessing but the minimum of nutrient properties, if any. When, therefore, Beef-Tea is given to an Invalid, the latter is really in danger of starvation : he is absorbing a so-called liquid food which contains no nourishment. 'Ztp,_47:7l4-*--Aqk . ..• 1110 *.nti ;C; 4 th • 44., • .A.v015„.44 •"?... ' 1 lt" egS; ft 41. v s , 49'44.4,,A3%01,4t - 4,.: * r•or Bovril, on the contrary, contains all the nourishing as well as all the stimulating properties of the primest ox beef. It is prepared under medical supervision by a scientific process which ensures this eminently beneficial result. Bovril is, in fact, the beef itself divide&amp; into infinitesimal particles, and condensed and liquified. Bovril ranks first among the notable achievements of modern science applied to dietetics; in the smallest bulk it has every virtue an article of food .can possess—it is nourishing and strengthening—it is a stimulant and a warmth-giver, and, besides being eminently palatable, it is assimilable by persons of the weakest digestive powers. Its value as a food for the invalid and the con- valescent cannot be overrated. It makes the weak strong and the strong stronger, and has been aptly called "Liquid Life."</p>
