        <p>The Early Bird Catches the 'worm. Still we always feel sorry for that worm. If he bad taken an extra 40 winks that Morning he might have prevented his premature decease. Still the proverb deals with the bird,and commends his early rising habits. He got a sweet reward, if a worm for breakfast can go by that term. The Bird was early on the spot to get a good thing, viz., his Breakfast. Perhaps he took part of the meal home to tbe family, and said an extra grace if the worm was extra fat. Now to moral- Read on please. Page Woodcock's Wind rills are a good thing. For Indigestion, Biliousnes3, Sick Headache, Liver Complaints, &amp;c., they are in- valuable. We've more sense than to keep spend- ing Money by the sackful to talk up a spurious article. We like money too much for that. If we did not believe in the remedy ourselves we would llot waste time and money in advertising. Rev. I. P. Dickerson, Ex-Jubilee Singer, writes from 58, Walmer-road, Plumstead, S.E. :—"After haying thoroughly tested your Wind Pills, lam full prepared to effirm that there are none to eclUal them. In my extensive travels I have tried all sorts of Pills. I give all preference to your Wind Pills. I am clad to tell you they have quite Cured me. Use my name as you please." All sufferers from Indigestion, Liver Complaints Wind on the Stomach, Costiveness, Sick Headache § Nervous Debility, Palpitation of the Heart, Bilious- ne3e, should avail themselves of this most ex- cellent Medicine. Page Woodcock's Wind Pills are sold by all Medicine Vendors at is. lid. and 2s. 9d. Post free for price from Page Woodcock, Lincoln.</p>
