        <p>ELLAND ELECTION, RESULT OF THE POLL. The votes recorded in the Edam' division, at the &amp;ea tion to fill the vacancy caused by the retirement of Mr. T. Wayman (L), were counted yesterday, with the fol- lowing result . C. P. Trevelyan (L) P. Foster (C) Majority DATE OF THE PEACE CONFERENCE HAGUE, THURSDAY. The International Peace Conference will meet here on May 18th. DEATH OF A PAPAL NuNato. PARIS, THURSDAY. Mgr. Clari, the Papal Nuncio to France, died at tea o'clock this morning. A FATAL DIVE. NEW YORK, THURSDAY. Professor Donaldson, a well-known exhibition high diver,fractured his skull last night. He was performing at an exhibition here, known as the Sportsman's Show, and the feat he was attempting was to dive into a shalt tank from a high! of 501 t. It is supposed he had slightly miscalculated his leap. LANCASHIRE CONGREGATIONAL UNION. ELECTION OF CHAIRMAN. The annual conference of the Lancashire Congrega- tional Union was continued at Roctid de yesterday. The Rev. E. Barrett, Liverpool, was elected chairman of the Union for the ensuing year, and it was decided to bold the next annual meeting at St. Helens. LATEST LOCAL WEATHER. FORECAST. The following brecast of the weather for the ensuing tvvPritY-four hours in the north we3t of England wag issued at the Meteorological OiflC3 at three o'clock yesterday afternoon : Northerly winds, strong in places. Fair generally, but. local showers.</p>
